Revision history for Tira

5.55  2026-09-04

    - TKT-930. A monitor that prints a block of lines gets all of them onto the
      board, rather than the first and then nothing until it speaks again.

      Found by his question - *"So why I sent message on TG you didn't see any
      notification from the policy bridge? Still something broken?"* - and it
      was. His message reached the police bridge as

          VIO-3057 JOB-006 said: NEW TG [6903] from Michael (chat 398296603):

      with the line under it, the text he actually sent, absent. The board said
      he had spoken without saying what he said, which is worse than silence
      because it looks like delivery.

      **`select` answers about the descriptor; `readline` answers about its own
      buffer.** The loop asked `select` whether to read and then read a *line*:
      `readline` filled its buffer from the pipe - typically the whole block -
      handed back the first line, and left the rest in Perl's memory where
      `select` could not see them. The next `can_read` reported nothing to read,
      so the loop sat out its quiet window holding lines it had already been
      given, and delivered them only when the *next* write woke it. A monitor
      was permanently one message behind rather than lossy.

      It now reads the descriptor with `sysread` into a buffer the loop owns and
      splits complete lines out of it, keeping any half-line for the next read.
      At end of input, or on a read error, whatever is held still goes in: a
      monitor cut off mid-sentence has spoken, and dropping it would be the
      silent truncation this epic exists to end.

      TKT-851's bounded quiet is untouched - a monitor that speaks once an hour
      is still heard within seconds rather than after twenty-five lines.

      **The loop is from 5.42 and TKT-927 only moved it**, so this reaches
      `tira.job.feed` as well as the feeder. It did not matter until a monitor
      printed a block, and his Telegram poller is the one that does - so the
      epic's own instrument for hearing him was the thing hiding him.

5.54  2026-09-04

    - TKT-924. The dashboard header says what is wrong rather than what exists.
      His message, 6812: *"Add X monitor(s) stopped if any / X schedual jobs is
      disabled if any along the line of `0 questions awaiting - 0 tasks` / Same
      for quesitons and tasks, if 0 no need to show / At the top right of the
      screen"*.

      Two requests, and the second is easy to read past. The counts are the
      obvious half; the other is a change to what already shipped, since the
      header printed `0 questions awaiting - 0 tasks` on a board with nothing
      outstanding. That spends the corner somebody checks for trouble on the
      news that there is none - and adding two counts without fixing it would
      have made the header worse, with a stopped monitor arriving beside two
      zeroes. **Every count is hidden when it is zero**, and the line is empty
      when there is nothing at all to report.

      A stopped monitor is why this epic exists: three standing hunts died on
      2026-09-02 and nobody noticed for hours. The count comes from the jobs
      provider's own `running` field - `Tira::Job::job_monitor_alive` against
      the process table, the same call `monitor-dead` makes and the same one
      the row indicator shows - rather than a second opinion computed in the
      page, because the top of the page disagreeing with a row halfway down it
      is worse than either being wrong.

      **`running === false` rather than falsy**, and that is what keeps a
      disabled monitor out of the stopped count. The provider sets `running`
      only for an *enabled* monitor, so a disabled one and a cron job carry no
      such field: absent means *not applicable*. A disabled monitor is counted
      once, as disabled, because it is off on purpose - which is also why
      `monitor-dead` says nothing about it.

5.53  2026-09-04

    - TKT-927. A monitor is one process and its ps line says whose it is. His
      message, quoting his own JOB-006:

        sh -c PERL="$1"; FEEDER="$2"; ID="$3"; EVERY="$4"; shift 4; if [ ...
        tira-monitor /usr/bin/perl /home/.../lib/Tira/CLI/../../../skills/job/
        cli/feed JOB-006 5 tail -F -n0 /home/mv/dd-tg/bot.log

      and asking for "a helper like `tira.job.feeder ... --ref <which project>
      -JOB-NNN` / on the ps -ef table show much more tidy process list".

      **The missing word was the board, and it is not cosmetic.** Job ids are
      per-board and one machine runs this skill for several projects, so
      `JOB-006` exists four times over and a monitor cannot be identified from
      `ps` at all. That is how another project's monitors came to be reported
      as his, with an offer to kill four of them.

      `tira.job.feeder --id ID [--interval SECONDS] [--command TEXT]` is now the
      whole monitor: it puts itself in a process group, runs the command through
      `open3` as a **list**, reads that pipe itself, and starts the command
      again after it ends when the job asked for that. Its `$0` reads

        tira.job.feeder JOB-006 [Tira Development] -- tail -F -n0 /home/.../bot.log

      the board's **name** and never its path, which travels in `TIRA_HOME`
      precisely so that it stays out of the process table.

      **It is a subtraction from three cards rather than a fourth mechanism
      beside them**, which was the card's own test of whether it had been
      written correctly. TKT-851 kept the job's words out of shell text - there
      is now no shell to keep them from. TKT-842 chose a file because a pipe
      with no reader blocks the child - the reader is the same process, so it
      cannot be forgotten. TKT-920 put the pipeline in a process group - the
      `setpgrp` is inside the process that owns the child. The `sh` script and
      the `perl -e` shim are both gone, and `t/538` asserts they are.

      It does no `fork` of its own: `open3` does that, which keeps a branch that
      `exec`s - and therefore can never report coverage - out of the file.

      **And it fixes a monitor that could not start at all, shipped in 5.45.**
      The spawn resolved its entrypoint as `dirname(__FILE__)` plus three
      updirs - correct while that code lived in `lib/Tira/CLI/Job.pm`. TKT-920
      lifted it one level deeper to stay under the 500-line rule, so the same
      three updirs landed on `lib/` and the path became
      `<root>/lib/skills/job/cli/feed`. `exec` fails, the child is a zombie
      before it can print anything, and `open3` has already returned the pid the
      board records - so the board held a pid for a monitor that never started.

      Nothing caught it because every test passes the feeder path **in**;
      `t/529` says so in its own comment. So no test compared the path the spawn
      *computes* with the file that exists, and `t/538` now does. The resolution
      walks up for the directory holding `lib/Tira/CLI.pm` - what every
      entrypoint script itself looks for - rather than counting levels, because
      a path counted in levels is the same fault as a test that names a file
      (TKT-921, the same evening).

5.52  2026-09-04

    - TKT-921. No test finds code by naming a file under `lib/`. `t/486` has
      refused this since 5.26, for `lib/Tira.pm` alone - the file TKT-746 is
      decomposing, and TKT-835 converted exactly the tests that read it. The
      eighth occurrence came from elsewhere: TKT-920 lifted the monitor
      lifecycle out of `lib/Tira/CLI/Job.pm` and `t/516` failed three
      assertions reporting a shell loop that had moved twenty lines into
      another file, which reads as "the code regressed" and is really "the test
      named a file".

      So the guard covers `.pm`, `.js` and `.css` under `lib/` now. Thirty
      files were converted: thirteen view reads to a new
      `Suite::view_source(BASENAME)`, the rest to the existing
      `engine_source()` and `cli_source()`. `view_source` finds a view by
      **basename** and does not concatenate the way its siblings do - a test
      about `jobs-editor.js` asks what that file does, and a concatenation
      would let an assertion match another view and pass for the wrong reason.

      Four tests keep their path and now say why on the line, joining the four
      that already did: `t/430` compares `lib/Tira/CLI.pm`'s own size against
      the modules it indexes, `t/402` asserts a module's POD describes its own
      providers, `t/426` asserts the View carries a template engine and the
      engine carries none, and `t/439`'s second half asks whether the module
      implementing `police.outstanding` consumes an option - a claim the whole
      layer would answer wrongly.

      Seven of the thirty offenders were written the same day the widening was,
      hours after the card describing the fault was filed. That is why this is
      a guard rather than a sweep.

    - TKT-911. The jobs editor stops offering a restart interval on a cron job.
      The row that carries "Restart when it ends, every N seconds" was shown
      whenever the job was in command mode, without asking whether it was a
      **monitor** - so on a cron job the checkbox ticked, the seconds field took
      a number, and the save died with the engine's own sentence: *"Restarting
      belongs to a 'monitor' job - a cron job fires on a tick rather than staying
      up, so there is nothing to restart"*.

      One term, and the interesting part is which rule was already there. The
      condition excluded a **message** job, for the engine's other refusal - a
      loop can only wrap a command. `_job_fields` refuses the cron case two
      lines earlier and the form had never been told. The fix adds the kind and
      keeps both halves of the mode test, because the kind alone would offer the
      row on a cron **message** job, which is refused for its own separate
      reason.

      Hidden rather than disabled, following 5.46's mode radio rather than the
      schedule box: the box is disabled-with-a-reason so the form keeps its
      shape as somebody clicks through it, and a control whose only possible
      answer is "no" is not shape.

      **And a hidden row now sends an explicit null rather than nothing**, which
      is the half the card's own walkthrough forced and the more interesting
      one. `job_update` validates the job as it *would* be, merging any field
      the payload does not mention with what the record already holds -
      deliberately, so an edit naming only the command cannot drop how often a
      monitor said it would speak. The save skipped a field whose row was
      hidden, on the mirror-image reasoning. Put together, hiding the loop row
      turned *the user cannot set it* into *the stored interval is merged into a
      cron job and refused*: a save that fails over a control no longer on
      screen, where before it could at least be unticked.

      The expectation row had exactly that fault from the day it was written -
      it has always been hidden for a cron job, so a monitor that declared how
      often it speaks could never be turned into a cron job from this page at
      all. Both fields are fixed here, because they are two lines under one
      rule: hidden means precisely *the engine refuses this field for this
      kind*, so null is the only legal value and clearing it cannot take away
      anything that was allowed to stay.

5.51  2026-09-04

    - TKT-928. `tira.job.feed` looks the job up before it watches standard
      input. It refused an **empty** job id and accepted a **nonexistent** one,
      then added standard input to a watcher and looped for ever - two-second
      polls, no CPU, never touching the job record and so never discovering the
      job was not there.

      That is not a tidiness fix. `tira.job.feed --id ID` is a **documented
      example**, and `t/70-doc-examples.t` runs every documented example
      in-process - so the suite ran this verb against a job called `ID` on a
      fixture board. It hangs whenever `prove` hands that worker a standard input
      that stays open, which is why it only bit sometimes: when prove has already
      closed the write end, `<STDIN>` returns undef, the loop ends, and nothing
      looks wrong. It cost two coverage gate runs, twenty-one and twenty-nine
      minutes.

      A **cron job** is refused too, for the reason `job_started` already refuses
      to record a pid for one: a cron job is not up between runs, so nothing is
      feeding on its behalf.

      The lookup happens **once, before the loop**. Inside it, it would be a
      job-list read every two seconds - and the loop's shape is TKT-851's answer
      to a monitor that speaks rarely: the wait is bounded and whatever is held
      is flushed on the timeout, so a poller printing once an hour is heard in
      seconds rather than after twenty-five lines. `t/535` asserts both survive.

      The existing empty-id check is **added to, not replaced**, and `t/535`
      asserts that separately - a fix that swapped one for the other would
      satisfy the new assertion and silently lose the old behaviour.

5.50  2026-09-04

    - TKT-917. The schedule on a job card reads as words for far more of cron.
      His report: *"the cron schedualer style translation on the card to human
      readable isn't fully covered. like `0 */2 * * *` is not showing readable
      text."* Measured before anything was written, twelve of sixteen common
      shapes fell through to raw cron. Now:

          0 */2 * * *      Every 2 hours, on the hour
          30 */2 * * *     Every 2 hours, at 30 minutes past
          0 9-17 * * *     Every hour from 09:00 to 17:00
          0 9,17 * * *     At 09:00 and 17:00
          0 22 * * 1-5     Every weekday at 22:00
          0 22 * * 6,0     Every weekend day at 22:00
          5 4 * * sun      Every Sunday at 04:05
          30 9 1 * *       At 09:30 on the 1st of each month
          0 0 1 1 *        At 00:00 on 1 January

      **It reads the values a field selects, not the text it was written as.**
      `Tira::Job` has expanded cron fields since `_cron_parse` was written, and
      this now uses that expander instead of matching patterns. So a schedule is
      described by what it MEANS: `0 0,4,8,12,16,20 * * *` reads as *Every 4
      hours*, however it was typed.

      That also made the step check stronger than the arithmetic it replaces.
      The old test was `60 % $step == 0`; the new one asks whether the gaps
      between the selected values are all equal **including the wrap**, which is
      the half divisibility could not see for ranges and lists: `0-20/2` has two
      between every pair and **four** from 20 back to 0, which is exactly where
      *every 2 hours* stops being true.

    - TKT-917. Every minute of a restricted hour reads as a span:

          * 9 * * *        Every minute from 09:00 to 09:59
          * 9-17 * * *     Every minute from 09:00 to 17:59
          * 9,17 * * *     Every minute of 09:00 and 17:00
          0 9 * 3 *        Every day in March at 09:00
          0 0 1 jan *      At 00:00 on 1 January

      The first three returned raw cron until the **coverage gate** refused this
      card at 99.3% and named three unreached lines. Two were working code
      nothing had asked about; the third was that fallback - his Q-119 rule
      broken in a corner neither the card nor the test had looked at. An untested
      line and an unreachable-by-design line look identical until something asks
      why the coverage is short.

      A single hour shares the span branch rather than being listed, because
      *Every minute of 09:00* reads as one firing at nine o'clock. The `:59` is
      deliberate: the schedule runs **through** the hour, not at it.

    - TKT-917. And nothing renders as raw cron, on his answer to Q-119:
      *"Describe everything, marking the approximate ones as approximate - e.g.
      'About every 7 minutes (restarts each hour)'"*.

          */7 * * * *        About every 7 minutes (restarts each hour)
          */45 * * * *       About every 45 minutes (restarts each hour)
          23 0-20/2 * * *    About every 2 hours from 00:23 to 20:23 (restarts each day)
          */60 * * * *       Every hour, on the hour
          0 0 1 * 1          At 00:00 on the 1st of each month, and also every Monday

      That replaces the rule this function has followed since it was written -
      anything it could not describe with certainty came back unchanged - and his
      reasoning removes its premise rather than contradicting it. The old rule
      protected a reader from a **confident** sentence that is false. A sentence
      opening with *About*, saying what makes it inexact, is not confident; there
      is nothing left to protect against, and the reader gets something instead
      of a cron string.

      **The mark is for inexactness, not for complexity.** `*/60` fires at minute
      0 alone, so *Every hour, on the hour* is exactly true and is said plainly.
      **The mark carries its reason**: *About every 7 minutes* alone is a hedge,
      and `(restarts each hour)` makes it an explanation.

      **The day-field OR is stated rather than marked**, which is the one place
      his instruction is not followed literally and the card says so. `0 0 1 * 1`
      fires on the 1st **and** on every Monday - exactly, and surprisingly - so
      *About* would describe the wrong difficulty. It gets *and also*, the one
      phrasing that cannot be read as an AND of the two conditions.

5.49  2026-09-04

    - TKT-915. A looping monitor says so on the board. `restart_every` appeared
      three times in the jobs view and all three were the editor or the save, so
      a monitor that restarts itself and one that runs once both read **Runs
      continuously** on their cards. His report: *"If I job original added as a
      loop and sleep for 5 seconds... didn't show on the card that is a loop."*

      The fix is in the engine, not the view. The card renders `schedule_words`
      and says so in its own comment; `Tira::Job::job_schedule_words` builds that
      phrase and took only a schedule string. It now takes the interval as well:

          Runs continuously, restarting 5 seconds after it ends
          Runs continuously, restarting 1 second after it ends
          Runs continuously

      Added to the phrase rather than replacing it, because both things are true
      and the first matters more. The second argument is **optional** and must
      stay so: `Tira::CLI::Browser` called this with a schedule alone and `t/517`
      asserts a dozen cron phrasings through the one-argument form, so a required
      parameter would have taken the whole schedule column with it. `t/533`
      asserts the one-argument call explicitly.

      **The other half of his report does not reproduce**, and the card records
      that rather than closing it quietly. For a monitor stored with an interval,
      the editor ticks the loop box, fills the seconds field, and `applyKind`
      runs on render to show the row and enable it; the engine preserves the
      field on update with `exists` rather than `defined`. Every step reads
      correct and three of them are now asserted. So *"when I edit it that
      setting is gone"* is what a person concludes when the card never showed it
      in the first place - one screen described twice. If it survives this
      release, the load path is eliminated and the browser is the next place to
      look.

5.48  2026-09-04

    - TKT-922. A monitor's card shows what the monitor is saying. It never had:
      the log panel on a job card was filled only from the response to a
      **Run now** click, and a monitor's button is **Start** - decided that way
      at TKT-843 because a monitor has no schedule to bypass - which returns a
      job record rather than output. So the branch that paints the panel could
      not run for a monitor, however much it was saying. His report, with a
      screenshot of two running monitors beside a cron job with a full panel:
      *"why only 1 job got the tail logs?"*.

      Reading `job.output` would not have fixed it. That queue is a **handover**:
      the feeder fills it, the police pass takes what it announced, and it is
      meant to be emptied - so a card reading it finds nothing a second after the
      monitor spoke. The record now keeps a second, smaller thing beside it: a
      bounded tail the drain never touches, newest kept and oldest dropped, for
      the same reason the queue does it.

      One feeder delivery's worth. That number is **mirrored** from
      `Tira::CLI::Job`'s batch size rather than referenced, because the engine
      does not depend on the CLI - the same boundary that keeps `Tira::Job` away
      from the process table - and `t/532` asserts the two stay equal, which buys
      back what a shared reference would have guaranteed without the dependency.

      The page was already being handed whole job records, so the new field
      arrived without a line of provider code. The card seeds it into the log the
      way a run's output is seeded, so there is one rendering path rather than
      two - and only when nothing is remembered already, since a card is rebuilt
      on every poll and re-seeding would reset the freshness colouring, making a
      monitor that spoke an hour ago look as though it had just called in.

5.47  2026-09-04

    - TKT-925. A monitor's words reach the bridge. They had not, since the
      feature landed: this board's bridge holds exactly ONE `monitor-output`
      entry across three weeks, and it is the first one it ever made.

      `lib/Tira.pm` declares two closures called `$report`, and they did not
      agree. `policy_evaluate`'s takes `( $policy, $record, $detail, $for,
      $sub_key )`; `_police_environment_violations`'s took `( $policy, $ref,
      $detail )`. `monitor-output` lives inside the second and calls it with
      five arguments, passing `"$job-id:$what-it-said"` as the sub_key. Perl
      discarded the last two without a word.

      The ledger keys an entry by `rule|policy|ref|sub_key`. `monitor-output`
      reports with no ref, so with the sub_key gone **every finding it ever
      made on a board collapsed into one entry** - every monitor, every pass,
      every different set of words. The quiet ladder then marked each telling
      after the first as a repeat and the bridge skipped it, which is correct
      behaviour on the information it had. The comment above the call already
      said what was needed: *"the quiet ladder keys on it, so two passes
      carrying different words must not look like one rule repeating itself."*
      Right in every word, handed to a function that could not receive it.

      The environment closure now takes the same five parameters and records
      both. Nine other callers in that sub pass three and are unaffected.

    - TKT-925. And the drain follows what was announced, not what was intended.
      `advance_monitor_output` removed a monitor's lines whenever the mark said
      `spoke` - and `spoke` is set from the same condition that CALLS the
      reporter, so it was true when the reporter returned early (a suspended
      rule, a declined card) and true when `bridge_write` skipped the violation
      as quiet. That is what turned a silenced announcement into **destroyed
      output**: the words came off the record and nobody had seen them.

      The drain now also asks which monitors were actually announced, matched on
      the sub_key the rule already builds. `spoke` stays, because it answers a
      different question - did this monitor have anything to say at all - and
      removing it would drain a monitor that produced nothing.

      Found while answering his question of 2026-09-04: *"i am worry that why
      the tg message didn't pick up even the police announce there are new
      message on the tira policy bridge"*. His Telegram messages had been
      reaching the job record all day and being taken off it, unannounced, by
      every pass.

5.46  2026-09-04

    - TKT-912. The dashboard's Save button was dead for every monitor. Not a
      validation refusing the form - a validation that never finished. The jobs
      editor disables Save while it asks the server whether the schedule parses,
      then throws the answer away if the input changed while the request was in
      flight. It tested that by comparing the schedule box against the value it
      had sent, which is the same thing for a cron job and never true for a
      monitor: there the value sent is the literal `monitor` while the box holds
      whatever cron text was typed before, or nothing. So the callback returned
      on its first line every time and the line that re-enables Save, below it,
      never ran. The button was dead from the first keystroke whatever else was
      filled in - his report was "after filled in the command, the save button
      still cannot be clicked", and the command field was never the point,
      because the code that reads it was never reached.

      The guard now recomputes the value the same way it was built and compares
      that. For a cron job this is byte-for-byte the old behaviour. The guard is
      **not** removed: deleting it would re-enable the button by throwing away
      the protection it was written for, and let a verdict about an old schedule
      paint itself onto one the user has since retyped.

    - TKT-912. The Command/Message radio is no longer offered for a monitor.
      His words: "you don't need to show the Command radio button since there is
      only 1 option to select". A monitor cannot be message-mode - the engine
      refuses that pairing, since a monitor with no command could never be found
      alive in the process table - so the group showed one real choice and read
      as a question nobody had answered. The row is **hidden, not removed**: the
      save path reads `modeMessage.checked`, and the form itself sets
      `modeCommand.checked` when a message job is switched to Monitor.

5.45  2026-09-04

    - TKT-920. Stopping a monitor stops all of it. `tira.job.stop` sent `TERM`
      to the pid the board recorded, which is the shell that owns the pipe
      between the command and the feeder reading its output - so the command,
      the feeder, and the loop subshell `--restart-every` adds were orphaned to
      init and carried on running. The record was cleared at the same moment,
      which is correct for the fault TKT-893 fixed and is also why this was
      invisible: a leaked stop and a clean one read identically from the board.

      The next `tira.job.start` then asked that emptied record whether the job
      was already running, was told no, and started a second monitor beside the
      first. `JOB-006` was found running under two pids with the board holding a
      third that was dead, which is how this was noticed at all.

      A monitor is now spawned through a shim that makes the recorded pid a
      **process group leader**, and the stop signals the group. The recorded pid
      is unchanged - the shim execs, so it is the same process it always was.

      `tira.job.stop` now answers with `signalled`: `group` when the whole
      monitor was signalled, `process` when only the recorded one was - a
      monitor started before this release leads no group, so the rest of it is
      still running - and `gone` when there was nothing there. Reporting success
      either way is what let this go a release unseen, so the word matters as
      much as the group does.

      A **process group rather than a walk of parents**, and that is forced
      rather than preferred: the process table this CLI gathers is
      `ps -eo pid=,lstart=,args=`, and `tasklist` has no parent column either,
      so walking `ppid` would mean changing gathering code every police rule
      reads. `setpgrp` is wrapped in `eval` because Windows has none; there the
      shim execs anyway, behaviour is exactly what it was, and `signalled` says
      `process` rather than pretending otherwise.

      NOT a regression. `TKT-851` put the feeder on the far end of a pipe in
      5.41 and `tira.job.stop` arrived in 5.42, so a monitor was already three
      processes on the day the single `kill` was written. `--restart-every`
      (5.42) is not the cause either - it adds a fourth process and makes the
      orphans self-restarting, which is what made an old fault loud.

    - The monitor lifecycle moved to `Tira::CLI::Job::Monitor`. The work above
      took `Tira::CLI::Job` from 496 lines to 583, past the 500-line rule
      `t/524` enforces, and raising the number is not how that guard is
      answered. The split is the concern this card is about: the spawn and the
      signal have to agree about which pid the board records, they sat in
      different subs of a 583-line file, and for a release they disagreed. The
      old names stay as forwarders so nothing outside has to know.

    - `t/516` stopped naming `lib/Tira/CLI/Job.pm` by path and reads
      `cli_source()` instead. When the lift above moved the shell script, three
      of its assertions failed and reported a loop that had been deleted. It had
      not been deleted; it had moved. That is the third time a lift has been
      read as a regression, and walking `lib/` is the fix the suite already had.

5.44  2026-09-04

    - TKT-914. The jobs editor never read job.mode on load, and the two symptoms
      he reported were one omission. The mode radios were hardcoded, so every job
      opened showing Command; and the single text input was filled from
      job.command whatever the job was, then read back into payload.message on
      save. A message job opened with an empty box, and saving from that screen
      wrote the empty box over the stored message.

      The mode now derives from job.mode the way the kind radio already derived
      from job.schedule eight lines earlier, and the field is filled from
      job.message or job.command accordingly. Both halves ship together
      deliberately: fixing the radio alone would be worse than the bug, because
      Message selected over an empty box looks correct where Command over an
      empty box at least looks wrong.

      A save that would erase a stored message is REFUSED rather than warned
      about. JOB-001, JOB-002 and JOB-003 carry their hunt instruction in
      `message`, and an emptied one fires on schedule and says nothing - which
      reads as the agent ignoring a hunt rather than as a lost field. There is no
      undo on that form.

      Reported by Michael, and the fault is entirely client-side: the test
      asserts the jobs provider already hands the page both mode and message
      before it asserts anything about the JS.

    - TKT-762. link.add accepted --from and --to naming the same card, and with a
      directional type it recorded the OPPOSITE of what was asked for. A link
      writes the forward type on --from and the reciprocal on --to; when both are
      one card, both writes land on it and the second overwrites the first, so
      `A blocks A` came back as `A is blocked by A`. Silently.

      It is refused now, naming the record, for every declared type - the rule
      keys on the two ends being one card rather than on which relation was
      asked for. In link_add rather than the CLI, so the browser dashboard's
      /link/add route and a direct engine call get the same answer.

      Refusing removes the only path to the overwrite rather than correcting it:
      there is no other way for both writes to land on one record, so the state
      that produced the wrong answer can no longer be created.

      Self-links already stored are LEFT ALONE - the refusal is at the point of
      writing only. They remain removable, though only by the type they were
      ADDED with, which is a separate defect: link.remove reports success and
      removes nothing when given the type the card visibly holds. Found while
      answering this card's own criterion 4 and filed as TKT-910 rather than
      widened into this one.

    - TKT-894. The Telegram poller is the last repeated process still living
      inside a session rather than on the board, and two things had to change in
      it before it could move - both the kind that only appear when a private
      process becomes a shared one.

      Its output carried a reply hint on every message: a shell command naming a
      venv python, a reply script, a chat id and a message id. Harmless while one
      session read its own process's output; on the police bridge it would put a
      command line into a stream other agents and other boards read, when the ids
      are on the event already. Michael's ruling, Q-116: "Keep it in bot.log for
      you, strip it from what reaches the bridge - both, since the log is yours
      and the bridge is shared." The poller now writes two sinks, through one
      emit point so the media path cannot be fixed while the text path is
      forgotten.

      And its log WAS stdout, by shell redirection. Once the process writes the
      log itself that redirect has to go, or every line lands twice - asserted
      rather than left as a thing to remember.

      The cutover is not a code step and is not done: Telegram allows one
      getUpdates consumer per token, so the running poller must stop as the
      monitor starts, and a message sent in that window can land in either. It is
      timed by the person whose messages they are.

      Documented in SKILLS.md beside the jobs table, and in docs/POLICIES.md as
      guidance to declare monitor-silent BEFORE owning a poller - it is the only
      rule that separates a process which is up but wedged from one with nothing
      to say.

      The poller lives in a directory that is not a git repository, so this
      entry is the only place in this repo that records the change to it. Its
      own test is ~/projects/skills/tg/t/test_hint_off_the_bridge.py.

    - TKT-760. priority-skipped reported a card as worked out of turn whenever it
      came from the column the owner uses to hand out work. TKT-383 makes a card
      sitting in that column outrank everything WHILE IT WAITS, but this rule
      judges the card being WORKED - and taking a card means moving it out, so
      the act of obeying him was the act that destroyed the evidence that obeying
      him was right.

      It fired three times on correct picks, escalating to URGENT on one. The
      third was a genuine rank inversion - the card left waiting outranked the
      one worked - so no tie-break could have helped: the fact "he placed this"
      had to be recovered rather than inferred.

      It is recovered from the card's own column history, where the move is
      journalled with its before and after. Reading history rather than writing a
      new field is deliberate: a field would have to be set by every path that
      moves a card - the CLI, the browser, the wizard - and the one that forgot
      would reproduce this exact bug. The journal already records every move by
      every path, unconditionally.

      The exemption follows the ROLE, so a board naming that column something
      else is covered and a board declaring no next role behaves exactly as
      before. A card taken out of turn from the backlog is still reported, which
      is the check the rule exists for. And a card already being worked keeps its
      authorisation when he queues a newer one, because the exemption reads a
      move that really happened rather than the column's current contents.

5.43  2026-09-04

    - TKT-753. `comment.add` accepted an empty `--text`, stored a comment whose
      body was the empty string, exited 0 and printed the comment back. It was
      alone in accepting an EMPTY one: every sibling refuses that - evidence.add
      a summary, warning.add a message, question.answer some text,
      checklist.add/required-action.add an item. On WHITESPACE they disagree,
      and comment.add joins the stricter pair: warning.add and question.answer
      test /\S/, while evidence.add, checklist.add and required-action.add test
      `eq ''` and still accept a value made of spaces.

      An empty or whitespace-only body is refused now - "A comment needs some
      text". The test is /\S/ rather than a length check for two reasons: a
      space is not a smaller comment than none, which TKT-585 settled for
      --command/--proof; and it is the SAME test discard-unexplained applies to
      a body when deciding whether a card was explained, so the command and the
      rule cannot drift apart about what counts as saying something.

      In the engine rather than the option parser, so the browser dashboard's
      comment provider is guarded by the same rule rather than by a second copy.

      The card was filed claiming an empty comment SATISFIED discard-unexplained.
      Measured before the work started, it does not: TKT-638, TKT-777 and TKT-778
      rebuilt that branch after the card was filed and closed the hole on the way
      past. The card was re-scoped and re-titled to the defect that remains, and
      t/525 asserts the rule's current behaviour as a control - so the two halves
      are now pinned to the same test.

      The refusal immediately found three test fixtures that had never done what
      they said. t/141, t/86 and t/523 called comment_add with `body => '...'`;
      the engine takes `text`, and `body` is the STORED field name, so all three
      had been writing comments with an empty body and passing anyway - the rules
      they exercise care that a comment exists, not what is in it. t/141 asserts
      conversation-not-folded fires over "Look at this example, it is the best
      evidence on the card", and that string was never stored. commands.md
      already calls comment "the only one of four write/read pairs where the
      write flag and the field name disagree", which is why the mistake is the
      predictable one.

      t/448 records TKT-638's decision to leave comment_add permissive, "since
      the rule below is where an empty explanation should be caught, not at write
      time". That principle is preserved and sharpened rather than overruled: the
      rule still catches it, and the whitespace body now arrives through
      comment_update - the one path this card deliberately left permissive - so
      the rule is proved against a body it did not control the writing of.

    - TKT-751. The standing rule that any Perl file over 500 lines is decomposed
      had nothing enforcing it, and had been losing ground quietly. The card was
      filed naming fourteen files; there were eighteen when it was picked up,
      two of them test files written after it was filed.

      t/524 walks every Perl file that ships - lib/ and t/ by extension, cli/
      and tools/ by SHEBANG - and fails for any over 500 lines that is not
      exempted. Shebang rather than directory because the rule says PERL:
      prove-the-gate and browser-tests are bash and card-holes is python, and
      the card wrongly listed all three as offenders. A guard built from that
      list would have been wrong on its first run.

      An exemption costs a reason and a card reference; a bare path refuses,
      which is the shape tools/gate-run already uses for coverage. An exemption
      for a file since split, deleted or renamed fails, so the list can only
      shrink, and the count is reported live rather than written down.

      Eighteen entries today, none split. The card's own criterion is "either
      split or in the list with a reason and a card reference" - the deliverable
      is the guard and an honest backlog, not eighteen decompositions behind one
      commit.

    - TKT-748. `--status` is parsed in the global option table, so every
      command in Tira accepts it and only the ones that read it honour it.
      `checklist.list` was the one list on the board with a status field that
      did not: `--status done` returned EVERY item, in a shape
      indistinguishable from a filtered answer, and exited 0.

      It now filters, taking the same three values `checklist.add` accepts -
      `pending`, `done` and `To Do`, compared case-insensitively - and refusing
      anything else by name rather than matching nothing, since an empty list
      reads as "no items are done". `To Do` is in the set because it is the
      spelling this board itself writes on move-in, so a filter that knew only
      the other two would have made every unmarked item unfindable.

      Michael's answer to Q-113 is what settled the shape, against the card's
      own title: "if --status goes with *.list like this. We should should only
      those ones with the wanted status. It is very straightforward to me. No?"
      The card had asked for --status to be REFUSED on a list.

    - TKT-748. Every command that cannot act on `--status` now refuses it by
      name, with a pointer to the four lists that can.

      A sweep of all twenty *.list entrypoints, against a board carrying two
      items of differing status in every list that has one, found sixteen
      taking the option with no status field at all - including `record.list`,
      which `ticket.list`, `epic.list` and `sow.list` all reach. The refusal
      comes from `%OPTION_READ_BY`, so all sixteen are covered without one of
      them being edited. Fifteen were measured by the sweep; `notify.list` came
      back empty in the fixture and was settled by reading `notification_list`,
      which returns ref, column and at.

      It also covers `question.ask` and `question.update`, which nobody had
      reported. Tira::CLI::Records passes --status into every question action
      and neither question_add nor question_update reads it, so both took the
      option and dropped it. They were found by walking the readers rather than
      by anyone being bitten.

      The filter went in the engine and the refusal in the option parser: the
      browser dashboard reads the checklist through a provider on a timer, so a
      filter in the parser would have left the browser answering the old way.

      BEHAVIOUR CHANGE BEYOND THE LISTS: `tira.<type>.move --status` used to
      succeed and ignore the option; it is refused now. record.move never read
      --status, and a move that already refuses --sdlc-gate and 24 card fields
      for the same reason should refuse this one too. The refusal is a refusal -
      the card does not move and its linked task is left alone.

      TKT-632's guard against that option splatting into the tasklist lookup is
      unchanged and still needed: --sort, --all-sessions and --unlinked are
      still unguarded on every command (TKT-581), so t/446 probes the splat with
      --sort now, which tasklist_list also refuses (TKT-888) and which is the
      same shape --status was.

5.42  2026-09-03

    - TKT-888. `tasklist.list --sort` accepted a direction it could not honour
      and a field that does not exist, and answered both with a wrong list that
      looked like a right one: `status:DESC` returned ASCENDING order, and
      `bogus:desc` returned the list unsorted.

      `DESC` in any capitalisation is now read as `desc` - SQL writes DESC,
      every spreadsheet writes DESC, and somebody typing it means desc.
      Refusing it would be pedantry; silently returning the opposite never had
      a defence. Anything else is refused, naming what was given and what is
      accepted, and an unknown field is refused naming the fields that exist.

      The refusal is the engine's, so the browser's one-second tasklist poll is
      guarded by the same rule rather than a second copy of it. The default
      spec, a bare field meaning ascending, and both working directions are
      unchanged and asserted - a refusal that broke those would be a worse bug
      than the one it fixes.


    - TKT-899. `job-due` read the jobs record without guarding the read, so a
      record that could not be read made every due job silently invisible and
      the pass reported nothing at all - a board with a corrupt jobs file looked
      exactly like a board with nothing due.

      The other three rules that read that record - `monitor-dead`,
      `monitor-output`, `monitor-silent` - had guarded it from the start, and the
      reasoning was already written beside `monitor-dead`: swallowing a read
      failure into "there are no jobs" is silence standing in for an answer,
      which is the failure this epic exists to end, rebuilt inside a rule.

      This was the fourth reader and the only unguarded one. All four now go
      through one shared helper rather than four copies of the same eight lines,
      each keeping its own sentence for what could not be checked - that is the
      part that should differ. The test asserts the shape as well: four callers,
      one helper, the guarded read in exactly one place, and no bare read
      anywhere, so a fifth reader cannot quietly go unguarded.


    - TKT-898. A job command was split on spaces alone, so any argument
      containing one was torn into pieces - and the job ran, exited 0, and did
      the wrong thing. `--text "two words"` reached the program as three
      arguments with the quote marks still attached; a cron expression passed as
      an argument arrived as five.

      The confusion was between two things a shell does. It GROUPS arguments and
      it INTERPRETS metacharacters. TKT-851 removed the shell to stop the second
      and took the first with it, which was never the intention - its own
      examples pass messages with spaces in them.

      So quotes group, and everything else is literal: a semicolon, backtick,
      `$(...)`, pipe or redirect inside an argument is text, and TKT-851's
      guarantee is untouched. Written out rather than using Text::ParseWords,
      because shellwords treats a backslash as an escape and ate every separator
      in a Windows path - caught by the test that asserts a full path and a .exe
      on either side still name the same program.

      An unbalanced quote is refused, with a message naming the quote and showing
      the command back. A job whose command is a typo is not a job with no
      command, and it does not run half of itself. The police pass reports that
      as a failed run rather than dying of it: the bridge is the single reporting
      path, and one malformed command must not silence every other finding.

      Three sites read that field and now share one helper - both execution
      sites and the Windows liveness comparator.


    - TKT-897. `tira.dashboard -o browser --with-police` runs the board and the
      police bridge in one terminal, which is what he asked for: "So the user
      doesn't need to run 2 terminals. All in 1 go." The pass is forked beside
      the server and reaped when it stops, so nothing is left holding the
      singleton claim after the board has gone. The flag is refused in any other
      output format - a JSON dump exits immediately, so there is no terminal to
      share and the flag would parse, do nothing, and read as confirmation.

      The pass is started with `open3` rather than a hand-rolled fork, which is
      the same decision `tira.job.start` already made for starting a monitor and
      for the same reason: a fork puts the child's work in a branch that only
      ever runs in the child, where coverage cannot follow and no test has
      watched it. It writes to the server's own handles, so its findings land in
      the terminal somebody is already looking at rather than in a pipe nobody
      reads. Being a separate process it cannot inherit the fact that it belongs
      to a dashboard, so `TIRA_POLICE_HOLDER` carries it - and an unrecognised
      value normalises to an ordinary claim, so a stray environment cannot buy
      the one protection the dashboard has.

      His second sentence asked for the opposite of his own earlier ruling, and
      the card described that ruling backwards - it said a second police is
      refused today, when in fact the newest claimant kills the previous one and
      wins, which is TKT-486 and is quoted in the code that implements it. So it
      was asked rather than inferred, and his answer scoped the change: the
      dashboard is a special case, and TKT-486 still applies everywhere else.

      The singleton claim can therefore name a HOLDER as well as a pid, and an
      ordinary police meeting a live dashboard says which process holds the
      watch and exits 0 rather than killing it. Standing aside is the correct
      outcome, not a failure, so the status says so. Three details are
      deliberate: the ordinary claim is still written as a bare pid, because
      nothing about that case changed; a yielding claimant does not write the
      file, since stamping its own pid would leave the record naming a process
      about to exit; and a claim left by a dashboard that has DIED is still
      taken, or one unclean exit would block police until somebody deleted a
      file by hand.


    - TKT-892. The job card and its editor, rebuilt once. His three complaints:
      "In the UI there is no way i can delete any existing job card / I cannot
      edit and card / I cannot pick between command or message." Grouped from
      six tickets on his instruction, so one gate walk delivers all six.

      The page can now correct, stop, start, enable, disable and delete a job.
      Every one of those verbs already existed on the board; none of them had a
      surface, which is why the fix is mostly routes and controls rather than
      engine work. `tira.job.stop` in particular arrived with TKT-893, and that
      is what unblocked the Stop button here - a button that can only refuse is
      worse than no button.

      One form serves create and edit, filled from the job. The schedule kind is
      a pair of radio buttons rather than the word `monitor` typed into a
      schedule box, which was a magic value somebody had to know. Command or
      Message is a second pair, and Message is not offered under Monitor -
      because the engine refuses that pairing outright, a monitor with no
      command being one that can never be found alive in the process table.

      Looping is a checkbox with an interval, off unless asked for, defaulting
      to five seconds and floored at two. The floor is measured rather than
      chosen: the feeder flushes after 25 lines or two seconds of quiet, so a
      command restarting every second never leaves a gap and a healthy monitor
      reports no output at all.

      The save now carries `expect_every` and `restart_every`. Both landed after
      the save was written and neither was wired in, so the page could have
      offered a control whose value the save discarded - the form reporting
      success while the board held something else.

      Both can also be UNSET, which the first attempt missed and the card's own
      test step caught: unticking the box left the old interval in place. The
      engine reads these two with `exists` rather than `defined`, so an explicit
      null clears and an absent key leaves alone - two instructions the provider
      had collapsed into one. The checkbox was one-way: it could add an interval
      and never take one away, over a form reporting success.

      `job_schedule_words` puts the schedule into words on the card face, with
      the cron kept as the tooltip. A step is described only when it divides the
      hour - `*/7` fires at 0,7,...,56 and then at 0 again, a gap of four minutes
      rather than seven, and `*/60` and above are hourly - so those are shown as
      themselves rather than described nearly-rightly. In the engine rather than the browser, for
      the reason the schedule check already is: two readings of one format drift
      apart. It refuses to guess - anything it cannot describe with certainty is
      returned unchanged, because a nearly-right description would be believed
      and the cron never read again.

      Run now writes into a tail below the card: a hundred lines, newest
      visible, under a minute old in yellow. It survives the section's own
      thirty-second refresh, which is the part that took care - a log owned by
      the card element would have been wiped twice a minute by the reload that
      rebuilds every card, leaving less than the status line it replaced.

      The buttons follow the job's state: a running monitor offers Stop and
      Restart and not Start, since offering Start invites a second process
      beside the first. A monitor whose liveness cannot be read keeps the button
      it had rather than guessing. Restart stops and then starts, only if the
      stop succeeded.

      Deleting a running monitor is refused with the engine's own words, which
      name `tira.job.stop` - the useful thing being not that the delete failed
      but what to do first.


    - TKT-893. What the board knows about a running monitor, and nine ways it
      was wrong. Grouped on his instruction - "group the same type into one
      ticket... the full process is slow" - and they turned out to be one root
      rather than nine faults: the board records a monitor's pid and command at
      start, and nothing kept that record true afterwards.

      `tira.job.stop` is new, and three verbs now refuse while a monitor is
      running rather than quietly diverging from it: changing its command (the
      pid still runs the old one), disabling it (`monitor-dead` is silent about
      a disabled monitor, so this hid a live process in both directions), and
      deleting it (the record went and the process did not, taking with it the
      only thing that could have reported the orphan). One shared guard, named
      once. A monitor that is not running, and every cron job, are unaffected.

      `monitor-silent` is a new police rule for the case `monitor-dead` and
      `monitor-output` leave between them - a monitor that is up and has stopped
      speaking. It reads the expectation the monitor itself declared, so the
      bridge and the dashboard heartbeat cannot disagree about whether something
      is overdue. Silent for a cron job, a disabled monitor, one with no
      command, one that declared no expectation, and one that has never spoken
      at all - the last because there is no silence to measure yet. Whole-board,
      and it refuses `--age` rather than ignoring it, since the lateness is
      already measured against what the monitor said. A jobs record it cannot
      read is reported, not taken for a board with no monitors.

      `tira.job.stop` clears the recorded start time as well as the pid, and
      clears both BEFORE signalling, so a signal that fails cannot leave the
      board pointing at a process nobody is responsible for.

      `--restart-every` keeps a command running without anybody typing a `while`
      loop. The loop is in the fixed pipeline script and wraps the positional
      parameters, so a job command still never becomes shell source - and
      because the supervising shell survives, the pid the board holds never
      changes and a restart is invisible to `monitor-dead`.

      A monitor that runs a police pass no longer reports ITSELF dead: the
      process table was built with the current process removed, which was right
      for `leftover-process` and broken for `monitor-dead`, and they read one
      list. The exclusion moved to the rule that wants it.

      `run_due_job` can no longer block the bridge for ever. It waited on
      `can_read` with no timeout, so one command that neither wrote nor exited
      silenced the whole board. Now an absolute deadline - 300 seconds, or a
      job's own `run_timeout` - then TERM and KILL rather than abandoning the
      process, a status of -1 rather than the 143 the signal arithmetic would
      give, and output naming the timeout. A timeout that finished the pass and
      said nothing would have swapped a hang for a lie.

      And `job_output_drain` stops removing lines the bridge never announced.
      It took the announced count off the front, which holds only while nothing
      trims the front - and `job_feed` trims exactly there on overflow. The
      count is now reduced by what the overflow already took, and only when the
      caller passed one: without a count the call means "take what is here", so
      adjusting there would have stopped a chatty monitor being drained at all.

      One of the nine needed nothing: TKT-871's spool-offset fault lives in code
      TKT-851 deleted, recorded with the evidence rather than ticked.

    - TKT-863. A monitor can say how often it expects to speak, and the
      dashboard shows one that has stopped saying it. His title: "List the
      monitors heatbeat with a red/green dim and light up on and off".

      TKT-861's indicator answers one question - is the process there. A monitor
      that is alive but WEDGED, process up and polling stopped, read as green
      for ever. docs/POLICIES.md admitted that in its own words and said
      catching it "needs the monitor to report progress, which needs it to
      cooperate". TKT-851's feeder made them cooperate; `last_output_at` has
      been arriving at the browser unread ever since.

      So each enabled monitor row carries its own heartbeat beside the running
      light. Two facts, not one - a monitor can be up and silent, which is the
      whole case this exists for.

      Deciding when silence is too long had no source in the record: a monitor's
      schedule is the literal string `monitor`, and JOB-005 is legitimately
      quiet for over an hour because it speaks only when the owner goes away, so
      any short constant would paint the only real monitor on the board red most
      of the day. Q-115 asked him; he chose per-job declaration over a
      board-wide constant and over deriving one. `--expect-every MINUTES` is
      that, refused on a cron job, and UNDECLARED MEANS NO EXPECTATION rather
      than a default - a default would be the constant he turned down arriving
      through the back door.

      Never spoken is a third state, not a bad one: null, an absent field and an
      unparseable stamp all land on it, where a version guarding only null would
      paint an unparseable stamp as fresh.

      The comparison reads `last_output_at` itself. A probe over the real
      expressions caught the label reading "Silent 3 hours ago, expects every 3
      hours" for a 3h09m silence against a 180-minute expectation - both numbers
      true, both rounded, and together reading as though the monitor were not
      late at all.

    - TKT-886. `tira.job.help` prints a jobs document the way `tira.policies`
      prints POLICIES.md - his words: "a dedicated helpline for the agent to
      read like the tira.policies... so that will be read by the agent to stop
      using share crontab and built in monitor or schedual jobs."

      It is the fifth documentation command and the only one written to talk an
      agent out of something. `docs/commands.md` already documented every job
      verb throughout, and it stopped nobody: three standing hunts ran as
      in-session monitors and were dead for hours on 2026-09-02 with nothing to
      say so, the Telegram poller is still a log tailed by a monitor, and
      JOB-006 was a `while` loop typed into a command field that never ran once.
      What was missing was never the argument list. So this page is the reason
      rather than the reference, and it says so in its own first paragraph.

      Its problem statement is drawn entirely from this board's own history,
      because an agent reading an invented cautionary tale has no reason to
      believe it and there were four real ones to hand.

      65 worked examples, not the hundred he asked for, and the document states
      the count and the reason in its second paragraph rather than letting the
      number pass. A hundred was reachable only by writing the same example with
      different words, which would have met the number and defeated the purpose
      he gave for it.

      Every example is executed by `t/509`, which exists because `t/70` scans
      only SKILLS.md and docs/commands.md - a new document would have been run
      by nothing at all, and all 56 would have shipped unproven. POLICIES.md is
      outside that list too and answers it the same way, with `t/85`.

      A fallback names the verbs when the document is absent, for the reason
      `_policy_help_fallback` gives and which matters more here: an agent that
      asks for help and gets silence has been taught the surface is unreliable,
      which is how it decides to build its own scheduling.

    - TKT-881. The Task List section stops growing with the queue, so the
      Repeated Jobs section below it is reachable. His words: "Huge Task List
      section bury the Jobs", and the fix he specified - "initially only show 5
      and every next show 10".

      The list held 142 tasks when he complained, 139 of them pending, and every
      one was rendered - so the section was exactly as tall as the queue was
      long, and Jobs started wherever that ended. That is the band of empty page
      in his screenshot: the section below was not missing, it was under one
      with no ceiling.

      Five cards now, ten more per press, with the remaining count on the
      button. The cap is on what is BUILT rather than a scrolling box - a
      max-height would have made the section short while the page still
      constructed all 142 cards, and Jobs would have come back into view only by
      scrolling past a scroll container.

      Working tasks rank above pending ones before the cut, so the three being
      worked right now are not hidden behind five arbitrary pending ones. The
      cap still applies to them: it is a sort, not an exemption, because a cap
      any status can escape stops being a cap at fifty working tasks.

      The filter still narrows first - five of the matches, not five of
      everything - and the shown-count survives the 1000ms reload, so the list
      does not snap back to five while it is being read.

    - TKT-879. An answer that is replaced is kept, and the card stops naming
      the wrong person. `question_answer` replaced the text and left the
      `author` alone, so a second answer showed one person's name above another
      person's words - silently, with nothing in the history to recover the
      original from.

      It happened here. An agent read Michael's answer to Q-113, ran the command
      to record that it had acted on it, and replaced his answer with its own
      acknowledgement. Police surfaced it as "Q-113 was answered at 10:50:03 and
      nobody has read it" - a fresh answer timestamp on a question already read.
      His words came back only because they had been quoted verbatim into a key
      detail first.

      The previous answer now moves to a `superseded` list on the answer, with
      its text, its author, when it was given, when it was replaced and by whom.
      The `author` becomes whoever wrote the new text - but only when one is
      given, since answering again without naming an author should not blank the
      name that is there.

      IT IS NOT REFUSED, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FIX WAS WRONG TO TRY.
      Requiring a `--replace` flag broke `t/57`, whose assertion predates this
      card: "answering again replaces the answer". An owner correcting himself
      is a supported operation and this card does not get to revoke it. The
      honest limit is that the fix would not have PREVENTED the mistake above -
      it would have made it visible the moment it happened, and recoverable
      afterwards.

      `--replace` was implemented, then removed with the refusal it existed for.
      Leaving a flag the parser accepts and nothing acts on would have been the
      exact fault TKT-748 is about, added by the card fixing a neighbouring one.

    - TKT-746. The fourth concern leaves the engine. `lib/Tira/Attachment.pm`
      takes the attachment subsystem - store a file, sniff its type, read its
      head, attach it to a record, list, fetch, detach, discard - out of
      `lib/Tira.pm`, which goes from 14,584 lines to 14,164.

      BOTH NUMBERS MEASURED AT THE MOMENT OF WRITING, which on this card is the
      point rather than a nicety. Four places in the tree stated this file's
      size when the work began - README said 14,177, SKILLS.md 14,256,
      `lib/Tira/Job.pm` 14,177, and the card's own title 14,621 - and every one
      was wrong, because each had been copied from the last sentence that looked
      authoritative. Three now agree at the measured figure; the fourth is the
      card title, left to TKT-876, which is filed for the guard none of them has.

      It takes `$self` and reaches the engine's shared helpers through it, the
      shape `Tira::Job` already uses, so only the concern moved. `Tira` keeps a
      forwarder at every public name, requiring the module at the call site, so
      a command that never touches an attachment never compiles it.

      THREE HELPERS KEEP A NAME IN THE ENGINE, and working out which took three
      attempts. `_store_attachment_file` and `_attachment_path` are called by
      `question_attach`, `question_voice` and `_backfill_added_at` - questions
      attach files through this store. `_attachment_content_type` is called by
      `lib/Tira/CLI.pm` and `t/423` as `Tira::_attachment_content_type`, fully
      qualified across a file boundary; nothing inside `lib/Tira.pm` calls it at
      all, so two caller searches in a row said it was free to leave and the
      suite caught it - "Undefined subroutine &Tira::_attachment_content_type
      called at lib/Tira/CLI.pm line 919". A leading underscore marks a name
      private to a PACKAGE, not to a file.

      TWO THINGS TRAVELLED THAT THEIR NAMES DID NOT SUGGEST.
      `%QUESTION_VOICE_TYPES` was declared `our`, but its only reader across
      `lib/` and `t/` was `_store_attachment_file`, so the `our` was vestigial.
      `_looks_like_text` had one caller, `_attachment_content_type` - leaving it
      behind would have split "is this file text?" across two files.

      `t/431` found three dangling references before any of this was true:
      `_epoch_of_datetime` called bare (50 callers in the engine, so it stays
      and is reached by package name), `_looks_like_text` called bare, and
      `encode_utf8` imported by `Tira.pm` and not by the new module - which is
      exactly the third failure that file was written for: it compiles, and dies
      when reached. A hand-check against a list of remembered imports found two
      of the three.

      No behaviour changed, and that is what the suite says rather than what the
      change intends: 495 files, 9944 tests, green, with no existing test file
      edited to accommodate the move.

5.41  2026-09-03

    - TKT-851. A running monitor's output reaches the police bridge. His
      instruction, 2026-09-02 16:55: "Monitors should not have separate logs.
      They all go to the policy bridge." `monitor-dead` made a STOPPED monitor
      say so; a RUNNING one was still unheard, which is why TKT-840 could not
      migrate the Telegram bridge onto a board-owned job - it would have put
      those messages in a file nobody opens and silently cut the channel.

      New rule `monitor-output`, taking the rule count to 43.

      THE MONITOR CALLS IN; ITS LEAVINGS ARE NOT READ. The first implementation
      gave each monitor a per-job file and had police follow it from a stored
      offset. It worked, and he challenged it before it shipped: it kept the log
      his instruction said should not exist, and it made "this monitor is alive
      and working" an inference from a file's contents. A spool nobody has
      written to and a monitor that has died look identical from outside. His
      answer to Q-112 on 2026-09-03 settled the shape: "each output will be
      registered who talked to the police."

      So `tira.job.start` now runs the monitor inside a pipeline whose other
      half is the new `tira.job.feed` entrypoint, which records what it prints
      against the job that said it and stamps `last_output_at` - once per
      delivery rather than per line, so the field answers when the monitor
      last spoke. That timestamp is a fact the
      monitor reported rather than one inferred from a file, and it is what
      TKT-873's silence rule needs to exist at all.

      A PIPE IS SAFE HERE FOR THE REASON ONE WAS REJECTED BEFORE. TKT-842 chose
      a file because a pipe with no reader fills at about 64KB and blocks the
      child forever. The feeder IS the reader: it drains continuously, and it
      lives inside the same pipeline as the monitor, so it cannot be forgotten
      or started separately.

      NOTHING OF THE JOB'S IS SHELL TEXT. The shell owns the pipe, but the words
      it runs arrive as positional parameters, so a command holding a semicolon
      or a backtick stays an argument - which is what `open3(@command)` gave
      before there was a pipeline, and what a stored, editable job record has to
      keep.

      AND THE COMMAND IS CHECKED BEFORE THE SHELL IS ASKED TO RUN IT. `open3`
      gave that refusal for free by failing to exec; a shell always starts and
      then fails inside itself, so a monitor with a misspelled command would
      have looked started until `monitor-dead` noticed minutes later. Caught by
      t/493 and t/498, which had asserted the refusal.

      TAKING IT, not the ledger `job-due` uses - that announces once per due
      window, and a stream has no windows. A ledger keyed on text would swallow
      the repetitions a poller legitimately prints, so what has been carried is
      removed from the record instead, and the test asserts that a line the
      monitor genuinely printed twice is announced twice.

      THE PASS DOES NOT WRITE. `t/86` asserts that a pass which found twenty
      things wrong changed not one byte of the board. Police observes and does
      not mutate, so what was announced travels out on the result and
      `Tira::CLI::Police` drains it after the bridge - at all three call sites,
      the watch loop included, since that is the one that runs continuously.
      The drain takes the COUNT that was announced rather than whatever is in
      the buffer: the monitor may have called in during the gap, and taking
      those lines would lose output nobody ever saw.

      Capped at twenty lines per pass - the NEWEST, because a monitor is for
      what is true now, and carrying the oldest first would leave a fast poller
      permanently announcing stale lines while the current ones aged out behind
      them. What is skipped to reach them is counted with whatever the record
      could not hold and said in the same finding, because a bridge that
      truncates silently is a bridge that lies. Silent for a cron job, a
      disabled monitor, one with no command, and one with nothing new to say.

      AND THE FEEDER WAITS FOR A BOUNDED QUIET, not only for a full batch.
      The first version wrote when it had 25 lines or when its input ended -
      and a monitor never ends, which is what makes it a monitor. A poller
      printing once a minute would have sat unheard for twenty-five minutes and
      an hourly one for a day, with the board reading "never called in" while
      the monitor was talking. That is the silence this epic exists to end,
      rebuilt inside the fix for it, and it would have made TKT-873's silence
      rule fire on monitors that were working. It now blocks for at most two
      seconds for more input and writes what it holds if none arrives, so a
      chatty poller still batches and a rare one is heard within seconds.

      Found by the container walkthrough, with the full suite green and 100%
      statement and subroutine coverage on all three touched modules. The
      handler's loop was being driven from a string filehandle, which reaches
      end of input at once, so every statement ran and the one situation that
      mattered - input open, nothing arriving, batch not full - never occurred.
      Coverage measures which lines ran, not which situations were tried.

      The per-job log path goes with the spool it named, along with the offset
      machinery that followed it.

    - TKT-875. The commit gate refuses the opposite drift too. It already
      refused a commit whose card was in backlog, discard or done - work
      running ahead of the board. It could not see a card that had run ahead of
      its WORK: one sitting in a column that claims the code is settled while
      the code is being changed. Both are the same broken rule, a card's column
      not matching its real state, and it has now happened three times on this
      board - twice one way, once the other.

      The third time is what forced this. TKT-851 sat in `verify` while its
      implementation was rewritten after the walkthrough found a defect;
      `verify` is not idle, so every gate waved it through, and Michael caught
      it by eye. His words: "Why MSK-02 kept on repeating?" and "Find a solution
      to prevent it happening again and again."

      The answer is that its prevention had been a rule to remember and
      detectors that fire once a trace appears, where every other lesson in this
      project became a gate. The pre-push hook already says why that is not
      enough: "Everything here was previously something I always do ... None of
      those were caught by a habit. They are caught here."

      So the gate now asks what a column CLAIMS - only `tests-red` and
      `implement` claim the code is being written - rather than holding a list
      of bad columns, and it decides by what the commit actually touches, so a
      documentation commit in a documentation column is not refused as code.
      Both copies carry it: this repository's own hook, and the gate
      `tira.gates.install` writes into every other project, which is the one
      that matters more.

      It stands at the commit, so work between commits is still unguarded. That
      limit is stated rather than claimed away.

    - TKT-854. Both sides of the page header line up. His words: "Page header
      project title on the left not align to the right side", with a screenshot
      carrying TWO red lines - one along the top edges, one along the bottom -
      and "make both sides align" written between them.

      `.hero` was already `align-items: end`, which is why this was not a
      missing alignment. That aligns the columns' BOXES at the bottom, and the
      left box is much taller than its text: the h1 is
      `clamp(2.2rem, 6vw, 4.8rem)` with `line-height: 1.15`, so leading sits
      below the baseline. The boxes met and the text did not, and nothing met at
      the top at all, because the left column is simply taller.

      Now `align-items: stretch`, so both columns take the height of the taller
      one and there is a shared top and bottom edge to line up against, with
      `align-content: space-between` on `.hero__aside` spreading its rows across
      that height. `align-content` rather than `justify-content`: the aside is a
      grid, so the block axis is the `align-` one, and `justify-content` would
      have moved its columns sideways and changed neither edge.

      `t/501` failed on this and was right to - TKT-855 had pinned
      `align-items: end` with the note that it belonged to another card. That
      card is this one. The expected value moved and the assertion stayed: what
      it guards is that a card about padding must not quietly change the
      alignment.

      The requirement was in an ATTACHMENT rather than in the card's text, and
      it sat there for four hours while a question was asked that the image
      already answered.

