5.39  2026-09-03

    - TKT-861. The Repeated Jobs section says whether a monitor is actually
      running. Until now it showed a monitor's id, schedule, command and
      enabled flag and nothing about the process, so one that died an hour ago
      looked exactly like one polling happily - the gap EPC-014 was filed for,
      one layer up: `monitor-dead` announces a stopped monitor on the police
      bridge while the board he actually watches stays silent. Michael: "No way
      to tell if the monitor is running."

      The verdict comes from `Tira::Job::job_monitor_alive`, the same call
      `monitor-dead` makes, so the page and the bridge cannot answer one
      question two ways. Nothing new decides liveness; the payload carries what
      the engine already knows.

      The process table is read once per request and only when there is an
      enabled monitor to judge - it is the expensive half, and `/jobs` is
      polled every thirty seconds. A test replaces the read with a counter and
      requires exactly one call.

      A cron job and a disabled monitor carry no liveness field at all, matching
      `monitor-dead`'s own stance: neither is supposed to be up, and a row
      reading "Not running" against every cron job is a false alarm by design.
      The page tests for the field's PRESENCE rather than its truth, which is
      what keeps that distinction. The state is shown as a green or red dot and
      also in words, because colour alone is not a signal everybody can read.

