5.35  2026-09-02

    - TKT-852. `tira.dashboard -o browser --show-logs` keeps a record of the
      requests the board answers, so somebody can see whether requests are
      arriving at all. His words, asked what the flag should mean: "A logs
      panel inside the browser dashboard itself, so the log is read in the page
      rather than in the terminal."

      He asked after the dashboard would not load for him and nothing could
      say whether a request was being refused or never arriving - the server
      starts and then says nothing about what it serves.

    - The record is a fixed ring of 200 entries, not a time window. A window
      bounds memory only if a request rate is assumed, and this is read
      precisely when the rate is unusual.

      The panel does NOT record its own polling. It reads `/logs` every five
      seconds - twelve requests a minute, more than the four board routes
      produce between them - so recording those would fill the ring with the
      act of looking, and the requests somebody opened the panel to see would
      fall off the end within minutes. Raised in review, where the retention
      arithmetic gave it away.

    - Recorded in an `after` hook, which is the half that matters: the `before`
      hook returns early for public paths and halts for unauthorised ones, so
      the status does not exist there yet. A REFUSED request is the case he was
      diagnosing, and in `before` it would have been recorded as though it had
      merely arrived.

    - `--show-logs` is refused with any output format but `-o browser`, naming
      what it needs. Accepted-and-ignored is the fault this file refuses
      everywhere else - a flag that parses reads as confirmation, which is how
      `--field` was stored and dropped by every command that never read it.

    - `/logs` answers 404 with a reason when the flag was not given, rather
      than an empty list. An empty list would say the board had answered
      nothing, which is a different claim from not keeping a record.

    - Nothing is written to disk: the last 200 are held in memory and served
      to the page that shows them. Starting the board with the flag says so on
      stderr. An earlier draft of that line claimed the record "goes no
      further", which was false - serving it to the browser is the whole
      feature.

5.34  2026-09-02

    - TKT-860. `monitor-dead` reported a RUNNING monitor as dead whenever its
      command was a `d2` wrapper - which is nearly every command on this board.
      Found in production on the first real use of the feature, an hour after
      it shipped: JOB-005 was started as `d2 is-agent-sleeping`, its process
      was alive, and the bridge said it was not.

      `d2` execs perl with the resolved cli path, so the process table holds
      `/usr/bin/perl -I ... /cli/is-agent-sleeping` and the stored string
      `d2 is-agent-sleeping` never appears in it. The containment check
      therefore failed. The comment defending that check claimed it coped with
      "the interpreter, the absolute path and whatever the shell expanded" -
      true when the stored command IS the program that runs, false for a
      wrapper, whose own name is gone the moment it execs.

    - The fix needs no new stored field: when both start times are known they
      settle identity between them, and the command is only consulted where no
      start time exists (Windows, where tasklist supplies none). A reused pid is
      now rejected on a FACT - when it started - rather than on command text
      that can coincide. The window is symmetric: later means a recycled pid,
      and earlier cannot be ours either, since a process that began before we
      spawned ours could not have held our pid while alive.

    - Two consequences worth naming. The false-dead reading also defeated the
      already-running refusal added in 5.33, so `job.start` would happily launch
      a SECOND copy and orphan the first - observed live, two
      `is-agent-sleeping` processes for one job record. And two fixtures in
      t/493 had a monitor's process starting an hour before the board recorded
      it, which cannot happen; they are corrected rather than worked around.

5.33  2026-09-02

    - TKT-843 (EPC-014). A play button on every repeated-job row, and an editor
      that will not save a crontab the engine would refuse. His msg 6484: "each
      repeated job record has a play button. That the user can run them anytime
      bypass the schedule." His msg 6485: "if the format is wrong, will be
      highlighted red and not letting the user save it until it is corrected".

    - `tira.job.run --id JOB-NNN` runs one job now whatever its schedule says.
      It is TKT-841's executor with the due-check simply not asked, rather than
      a second way to run a command - that executor carries a deadlock fix and
      a signal-status fix that only came out of review, and a fresh one written
      for a button would have carried neither. A failing job reports its exit
      status, because silence after a failure cannot be told from never having
      run.

    - A MONITOR row starts rather than fires, since a monitor has no schedule
      to bypass. And `job.start` now REFUSES a monitor that is already running:
      before this it spawned a second process and overwrote the recorded pid,
      leaving the first with nothing on the board pointing at it - an orphan
      `monitor-dead` could not see, which is the failure this epic exists to
      end. From a terminal that took a deliberate mistake; with a play button
      it was one stray click.

    - The editor validates through `Tira::Job::schedule_refusal`, the same
      answer the write path would give. For a cron expression that is
      `_cron_parse`'s own message (trailing whitespace trimmed); the literal
      `monitor` is accepted without parsing, and a blank schedule is refused by
      `schedule_refusal` itself rather than by the parser. The point is that
      the browser ASKS rather than decides, so it cannot drift into accepting
      something the save would reject - the fault this project already shipped
      once, when the engine and the browser disagreed about attachment content
      types.

    - Three new dashboard providers: `job_run`, `job_check` and `job_save`.

5.32  2026-09-02

    - TKT-842 (EPC-014). A monitor-kind repeated job can now be STARTED, and
      a monitor that DIES is reported by name instead of going quiet. This is
      the rule the epic was filed for: on 2026-09-02 three standing hunt loops
      had been dead for hours and nothing said so, because a loop that has
      stopped and a loop with nothing to report produce identical output.

    - `tira.job.start --id JOB-NNN` starts a monitor and records the pid it
      started as. Its output is appended to a log named for the job rather
      than to a pipe: a pipe with no reader fills at around 64KB and blocks
      the monitor forever, which is a stopped monitor that still looks
      started.

    - New police rule `monitor-dead`, taking the rule count to 42. Liveness is
      the recorded pid CONFIRMED BY A COMMAND MATCH against the process table.
      The second half is not belt-and-braces: pids are reused, a reused pid
      answers `kill 0` in the affirmative, and a check built on the pid alone
      would report a dead monitor as alive - the exact silence the rule exists
      to end. A disabled monitor and a cron job stay silent; an enabled
      monitor that was never started is reported, having no pid at all.

    - The rule takes no `--age`, and refuses one rather than ignoring it: a
      monitor that has stopped does not become more stopped, and a grace
      period is that silence spelled as policy.

    - A `monitor` job now requires a command: `--message` is refused for one.
      A monitor announces nothing, so such a record was unreachable in every
      direction - never due, unstartable, and with no command to look for in
      the process table - and `monitor-dead` would have reported it dead on
      every pass forever. Found reading the diff rather than by a failing
      test. A commandless monitor already stored is skipped by the rule rather
      than reported, since refusing new ones does not clean up old ones.

    - Three defects found in adversarial review before release, all of them
      failure-looks-like-success shapes inside the rule meant to end exactly
      that. The liveness check now compares START TIMES as well as the pid and
      command, so a pid reused by the same command started again by hand no
      longer reads as alive. `monitor-dead` REPORTS a jobs record it cannot
      read instead of treating the failure as "no monitors", which had put the
      card's own thesis - silence standing in for an answer - inside its
      implementation. And a monitor that starts but whose pid cannot be
      written is now stopped again rather than left running unrecorded, where
      it would have been reported dead every pass and duplicated by the next
      `job.start`.

    - Known limit, documented rather than left to be discovered: a monitor
      that is alive but WEDGED - process up, polling stopped - reads as alive.
      Detecting that needs the monitor to report progress, and every monitor
      this feature absorbs is an existing command that will never do so.

5.31  2026-09-02

    - TKT-841 (EPC-014). A command-mode repeated job now RUNS when it is due,
      and what it produced reaches the police bridge - including when it
      fails. His msg 6487: "either set to run a command and output to the
      police bridge or direct message to the bridge".

      Until this, job-due did `next if mode ne 'message'`, so a command-mode
      job was skipped outright and nothing downstream ever heard it was due -
      a schedule nobody read, one level below the one this epic was filed
      about. The rule now announces a due job of either mode, a command-mode
      one as "runs: <command>", so the bridge says what is about to happen
      before it says what happened.

      WHERE EXECUTION LIVES WAS DECIDED BY A TEST. The engine is forbidden
      every shell-invoking construct and the guard catches the list form too,
      so "without a shell" bought no exception there; writing the list form
      without parentheses to slip past the pattern would have been defeating a
      guard rather than respecting it. Suite::engine_source() already excludes
      lib/Tira/CLI, because the board server legitimately needs a shell, so
      the CLI layer was the sanctioned home and no second exception had to be
      invented. t/489's assertion that the rule body runs nothing did not need
      to move after all: execution went to another layer, so the text is
      unchanged and its meaning strengthened - it now guards a boundary that
      exists rather than one not yet crossed. t/492 asserts the same denial of
      the whole engine.

      Tira::CLI::Police::run_due_job splits the command and hands the list to
      IPC::Open3, so the program is named separately from its arguments and a
      semicolon is an argument rather than an instruction. Stdout is captured
      in full and stderr appended - all of both, but ordering BETWEEN them is
      not preserved, which every document now says rather than leaving a
      reader to assume otherwise. The exit status is returned rather than
      discarded, because a command that failed silently cannot be told apart
      from one that never ran. A program that is not installed is a result and
      not a crash: what could not be run is named, the same judgement
      _reading already makes about a missing docker.

      The honest limitation, stated in the code and in all four documents:
      splitting on whitespace means no quoting, so `echo "two words"` is four
      arguments. A job needing quoting should be a script, which is also the
      answer that keeps the no-shell guarantee.

      One assertion in t/492 was vacuous in its first draft. isnt($ran->{status},
      0) passed against the pre-fix code because run_due_job did not exist, so
      $ran was undef and "not zero" was true - a MISSING EXECUTOR looked exactly
      like A REPORTED FAILURE, the precise inversion this card exists to
      prevent, reproduced inside its own test. It establishes the result before
      reading a field out of it now.

      And the comment explaining the no-shell rule failed t/492 by naming the
      forbidden constructs literally: the guard reads comments as well as code,
      so the prose describing the rule violated the rule's own pattern.

5.30  2026-09-02

    - TKT-837 (EPC-014). tira.job.add, list, update and delete: the repeated
      jobs TKT-836 gave the board a record for can now be typed at a command
      line. The bodies live in Tira::CLI::Job, required at the point one of
      those verbs runs. What makes a schedule valid stays in Tira::Job - this
      layer passes arguments through and lets the engine's refusal reach the
      caller unchanged, and t/488 proves it by capturing the engine's message,
      extracting its core, and requiring the command-line output to contain
      that exact string. Two validators for one format is how the engine and
      the browser came to disagree about attachment content types on TKT-713.

      THE CAP WAS THE POINT, NOT AN OBSTACLE. These verbs took
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm past t/430's 3,000-line limit, and the card sat blocked
      overnight on what had been framed as a decision for the owner: raise the
      cap, shave comments, or split further. It was never his to make. TKT-746
      is an open epic for lifting concerns out of oversized files and TKT-751
      makes decomposition of any Perl file over 500 lines standing policy, so
      "make room" was already the answer the board held. The option guard -
      %MISLEADING_OPTIONS, %OPTION_READ_BY and the refusal enforcing them -
      moved to Tira::CLI::Options; the index went 2,999 -> 2,817 before the
      verbs and 2,844 after, under a cap that did not move.

      A duplicate option specification was caught before it shipped. The
      draft declared 'command=s' for a job's command while CLI.pm already
      binds 'command=s@' for required-action proofs - what t/450 refuses,
      since Getopt::Long then prints "Duplicate specification" to STDERR on
      every invocation. Its own comment claimed 'command' was a new name
      rather than a reuse, which was not true. Tira::CLI::Job reads the
      existing repeatable form and refuses two --command flags outright: a job
      runs one command, and quietly taking one of two is the fault the option
      guard exists to prevent.

      The lift broke two tests, and both breakages were about where code
      lives rather than what it does. Three test files disable the guard by
      localising *Tira::CLI::_refuse_unread_options; calling the lifted
      subroutine at its new address moved the name out from under them, so the
      override silently stopped overriding and the tests failed as though the
      code had regressed. The name stays here as a forwarder - a lift must not
      change what a caller can reach. And t/239 parsed both tables out of
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm by name, so after the lift it reported zero declared
      refusals, which reads as "the table is empty" rather than "the table
      moved". It asks t/lib/Suite.pm's new cli_source() for the command
      surface now, the same way seven engine tests were taught to ask for the
      engine on TKT-835.

      Four entrypoints ship with this: skills/job/cli/{add,list,update,delete}.
      Their absence was invisible until the documented examples were run - the
      verbs dispatched correctly from inside Tira::CLI and could not be reached
      from a shell at all, and t/172 is what said so.

      Five of t/488's assertions passed against code where nothing existed.
      All five were negative - three "refused with non-zero status", two "not
      present in the output" - and a missing verb has exactly that shape. Each
      is now anchored so it can only pass for the right reason; 21 assertions
      became 27.

5.29  2026-09-02

    - TKT-839 (EPC-014). A Repeated Jobs section on the dashboard, directly
      under the Task List, where he asked for it: "a new section under the
      tasklist to see all the scheduled jobs, they like cronjob style the
      record". Until now, seeing what a board was scheduled to do meant
      reading .tira/jobs.json - the same "nobody can see it" problem the
      epic started from.

      Built the way the Task List section already is, rather than a new
      pattern: a browser provider, an empty list in the page shell, and
      lib/Tira/views/jobs-editor.js filling it from a /jobs route. Each job
      is its own row with its id, whether it is enabled, its schedule, what
      it says or runs, and its mode and schedule kind.

      /jobs is in %POLLED, and that is checked rather than remembered:
      jobs-editor.js refreshes on a timer, and t/479 refuses any
      setInterval-driven GET whose route %POLLED does not name. That guard
      exists because /tasklist and /tasklist/sessions were missed exactly
      this way until TKT-807, leaving a tab that never expired its session.
      The refresh is 30 seconds rather than the Task List's one second: a
      schedule changes when somebody edits it, which is rare, and the
      cheapest way not to repeat TKT-694 is not to create the pressure.

      READ-ONLY. The play button and the editor modal with its crontab
      tooltip and save-blocking validation are their own card - a control
      that runs a job is a different kind of thing from a table that shows
      one, and deserves its own tests.

      Three of the test's own assertions were wrong before the code was.
      They asked for the section in a .tt template, when the Task List
      section is not in one either - it is built by tasklist-editor.js - and
      they asked the static HTML to contain each job's message and id, when
      that data arrives by fetch. Both would have forced a design the rest
      of the page does not use. What is asserted now is that the section
      exists, that it comes after the Task List, and that the part which
      renders a job lives in lib/Tira/views rather than in Perl string
      concatenation. The empty shell itself IS emitted from Perl - exactly
      as the Task List's own <ol> is - so the claim is about the rows, not
      about the section element. Worth stating precisely: an earlier
      wording here said the section lived in the View layer full stop,
      which overstated it, and t/426's rule is about job data never being
      concatenated into the page rather than about the container.

5.28  2026-09-02

    - TKT-838 (EPC-014). job-due: the police rule that makes a schedule on
      the board reach somebody. When a repeated job comes due, its own
      message is announced on the bridge - not a generic reminder, but the
      words whoever wrote the job chose.

      ANNOUNCED ONCE PER DUE WINDOW, not once per pass. The due minute is
      the finding's sub_key, so the ledger settles this announcement and
      opens a fresh one when the job next comes due - the mechanism TKT-698
      added for one rule reporting several distinct occurrences about one
      subject. A rule that repeated every pass would make the bridge
      unreadable, which fails the same way silence does.

      A disabled job is silent, and so is a monitor job: that runs
      continuously rather than firing on a tick, so it is never due.

      THE RULE ANNOUNCES AND RUNS NOTHING. Executing a command-mode job is
      its own card, so that surface arrives with its own tests rather than
      as a side effect of this rule already holding the job. t/489 asserts
      the rule body contains no system, exec or qx.

      ADDING A RULE RIPPLES, and five test files caught it - which is the
      suite working rather than the change being wrong. Three declare every
      rule the tool offers so none can be forgotten (t/79, t/86, t/98) and
      needed job-due added; t/86 additionally requires every declared rule
      to be seen FIRING against its fixture board, so it gained a job that
      is due at the fixture clock. t/85 requires every rule to appear in a
      worked --rule example in docs/POLICIES.md, which a table row alone
      does not satisfy. And t/147 caught two denials of mine that an empty
      pass would have satisfied for the wrong reason.

      Rule count is now 41. Ten places across SKILLS.md, README.md and
      docs/POLICIES.md said 40 and were corrected, including two that quote
      the phrase as an example of what t/433's guard matches - a stale
      example beside a live count reads as the count. SKILLS.md also said
      lib/Tira.pm was 14,177 lines in the present tense; it is 14,256.

5.27  2026-09-02

    - TKT-836 (EPC-014). Repeated jobs: a schedule the board owns rather
      than a loop a session owns. lib/Tira/Job.pm holds the record and the
      cron evaluation; Tira keeps lazy forwarders, so callers still say
      $tira->job_add.

      ENGINE ONLY SO FAR - this is the foundation, not the feature. There
      are no CLI verbs yet (TKT-837), no police rule to announce a due job
      (TKT-838), no command execution (TKT-841), no monitor runtime
      (TKT-842) and no dashboard section (TKT-839, TKT-843). A job can be
      created and asked whether it is due; nothing yet acts on the
      answer.

      It exists because a schedule that lived in a session died with it.
      Three standing hunts - hourly bugs, two-hourly improvements,
      three-hourly doc-gaps - had been dead for hours on 2026-09-02 and
      nobody noticed, because a loop that has stopped and a loop with
      nothing to report produce identical output: none.

      THE REFUSAL IS THE POINT. A malformed schedule is rejected when it
      is written, naming the field that was wrong and the range it takes,
      and nothing is stored. A job whose cron is nonsense would never
      fire, and a job that never fires is indistinguishable from a job
      with nothing to say - so storing it would rebuild the very
      ambiguity this feature removes, one layer down.

      A job carries either a command, whose output the police will send
      to the bridge, or a message it announces directly - never both,
      never neither, with the mode stored explicitly rather than inferred
      from which field is populated. Its schedule is a crontab string or
      the literal 'monitor', and schedule_kind records which so no caller
      re-parses to find out. Both pairs are his design (msg 6487).

      job_is_due always takes the instant as an argument rather than
      reading the clock, so an assertion is about the schedule and not
      about when the suite happened to run.

      NEW CODE WENT STRAIGHT INTO ITS OWN MODULE. TKT-746 is shrinking
      lib/Tira.pm - 15,264 lines down to 14,177 across three lifts - so
      adding a few hundred lines to it would have meant lifting the same
      lines back out later. lib/Tira/Job.pm follows the shape Tira::Toon,
      Tira::Tasklist and Tira::Render already established.

5.26  2026-09-02

    - TKT-835. Seven tests stopped locating engine code by naming
      lib/Tira.pm and now ask t/lib/Suite.pm's new engine_source() for it,
      which walks lib/ and asserts it found modules before reading any.
      t/486 refuses new instances.

      This is preparation for TKT-746's last lift, not tidying. t/177 broke
      on TKT-834 for exactly this reason, TKT-703 had "fixed" the same line
      before by naming a second file rather than by walking, and
      MISTAKE.md records four earlier instances from TKT-607. Three of the
      seven converted here read police content, so the police-engine lift
      would have broken them together.

      THE RULE IS NARROWER THAN IT FIRST LOOKED, and four predicates were
      tried before one held. "Opens any lib/*.pm" missed the slurp form.
      "Names any lib/*.pm" was too wide: it would have forced wrong changes
      on tests whose claim is about a particular file, and it caught t/279,
      which WRITES fixture modules. "Reads lib/Tira.pm" with a file-level
      write exclusion silently dropped t/126, which writes a fixture
      elsewhere. What holds is reading lib/Tira.pm, checked per occurrence:
      that file is the one being decomposed, so code read out of it is
      mobile by definition, while a stable concern module is a legitimate
      subject for a test to be about.

      Two files therefore keep naming it, and say why on the line, using
      the marker convention t/176 already established rather than a list of
      exempt filenames: t/426 asserts lib/Tira.pm ITSELF carries no page
      markup, which is true of the engine and false of lib/Tira/
      DashboardWeb.pm, the View; and t/344 takes only Tira.pm's own POD,
      which is deliberately where the lifted modules' methods stay
      documented. t/486 asserts those exceptions exist and carry a reason,
      so the escape hatch cannot be used silently.

      engine_source() excludes the Tira::CLI layer for the same class of
      reason: t/106 asserts the engine invokes no shell, and
      lib/Tira/CLI/Serve.pm legitimately does. Measured rather than
      assumed - Serve.pm is the only file under lib/ that matches that
      pattern at all.

5.25  2026-09-01

    - TKT-834 (TKT-746's third lift). The human and table renderers -
      _markdown, _markdown_fields, _markdown_value and the HTML
      _dashboard_table - move out of lib/Tira.pm into lib/Tira/Render.pm,
      required by format_output immediately before its human and table
      branches. A caller asking for json or toon compiles none of it.

      This is the Tira::Toon shape rather than the Tira::Tasklist shape,
      and the difference was measured before choosing it: all four subs
      are reached only from format_output, and nothing in t/, cli/ or the
      CLI modules names them. One caller means format_output calls into
      the module directly and no forwarder is left behind.

      Three helpers deliberately stayed on Tira because they have callers
      outside this concern. _html_escape is used by the login page HTML
      as well, and is reached as a method on $self. _render_view,
      _view_asset and json_object are plain functions used across the
      engine, so the moved code calls them fully qualified - twelve call
      sites. Left unqualified they would have resolved against
      Tira::Render, compiled cleanly under perl -c, and died the first
      time a board was rendered: the fault TKT-607 produced seven times
      and TKT-832 produced at 36 sites. This time it was found by
      grepping the moved region for its own dependencies before moving
      it, rather than by the suite afterwards. $VERSION needed the same
      treatment and perl -c did catch that one, since strict sees an
      undeclared global but not an undefined sub.

      t/485 proves the pair every lift owes: the module compiles
      standalone without Tira.pm loaded first, and format_output for json
      or toon leaves Tira::Render out of %INC. Its other twelve
      assertions are a no-behaviour-change baseline that passed before
      the lift as well as after - whole-record and narrowed human output,
      table rendering, escaping through the helper that stayed behind,
      and both refusals.

      lib/Tira.pm was 14,419 lines before this lift and is 14,177 after
      (measured, not estimated); lib/Tira/Render.pm is 346. Across the
      three lifts the engine is down from 15,264 to 14,177.

