5.17  2026-09-01

    - TKT-687. A required-action or checklist proof over 2000 characters
      routes to attachment_add_content, which hashed it with sha256_hex
      before encoding it to bytes - Digest::SHA dies with "Wide character
      in subroutine entry" on a character string containing code points
      above 255, so a long proof quoting an emoji, an em dash, or an
      accented name (all ordinary in captured terminal output, and this
      board's own dashboard renders required-action state as emoji) was
      refused with a message naming an internal hashing routine rather
      than anything about the proof. A short proof never reached the
      hash and a long ASCII proof hashed fine - only the combination
      failed.

      attachment_add_content now encodes character content to UTF-8
      bytes before hashing and writing, the same _response_bytes pattern
      DashboardWeb and OnboardWeb already use. A pure-ASCII attachment
      keeps the exact hash it always had, since encoding a byte string
      is a no-op - nothing needs migrating. New test t/477.

5.16  2026-09-01

    - TKT-686. login_verify checked a stored password record's algorithm,
      salt and hash, but passed the fourth field - iterations - straight
      into _password_derive, whose `for ( 2 .. $iterations )` loop
      silently does nothing when $iterations is undef, 0 or 1. Measured:
      all three produced the identical single-round digest in place of
      the real 210,000-round cost, and undef also printed an
      uninitialized-value warning. A record still claiming
      pbkdf2-hmac-sha256 could be verified as a bare HMAC, and an
      absurdly large stored value made sign-in hang.

      login_verify now refuses a stored record whose iterations is not a
      positive integer at or above the new $PASSWORD_ITERATIONS_FLOOR
      (210,000), or above the new $PASSWORD_ITERATIONS_CEILING
      (2,000,000) - both refused before any hashing is attempted. The
      floor is a separate constant from $PASSWORD_ITERATIONS on purpose,
      so raising the write-time cost later cannot lock out a password
      already on file. _password_derive now dies rather than returning a
      digest when given no usable count. New test t/476. No change to
      login_register or any record this board has already written.

