5.03  2026-09-01

    - TKT-788 (third attempt). .hero and .column__head are both
      position:sticky;top:0 - once scrolled past the hero's height, both
      stick to the same point on the viewport, and .hero's higher
      z-index painted over .column__head, hiding the column header
      during scroll.

      Two earlier attempts shifted .column__head's own top offset to
      var(--hero-h) instead, and both broke real pointer interactions in
      the Playwright interaction suite (dashboard-table.js/
      dashboard-browser.js), confirmed via a controlled A/B test and
      reverted twice. Hypothesis for why: .board__scroll{overflow-x:auto}
      likely forces its computed overflow-y away from 'visible' too (a
      well-known CSS Overflow spec quirk), making .board__scroll rather
      than the viewport the sticky positioning containing block for
      .column__head - so a nonzero top resolves against the wrong box.

      This attempt avoids that landmine entirely: both rules keep top:0
      exactly as shipped, unchanged. Only .column__head's z-index moves
      from 2 to 6, above .hero's 5, so it paints on top instead of
      hiding underneath - zero geometry change, so it cannot reproduce
      the interaction regression. New test t/464 asserts both rules stay
      sticky/top:0 and that .column__head's z-index is strictly above
      .hero's. Confirmed via tools/browser-tests: dashboard-table.js
      (the test that broke twice before) passes clean; the only
      remaining failure is the separate, already-tracked TKT-675-class
      dashboard-browser.js drag flake, unrelated to this change.

5.02  2026-09-01

    - TKT-675. t/playwright/required-actions.js's "marking one done updates
      the count" step failed intermittently in the push gate (~1 in 5,
      worse under host load) - not because of a race, as first suspected,
      but because its count assertion hardcoded "2/3" while the fixture,
      by that point in the file, already carried 4 required items (REQ-004
      was pushed in earlier for TKT-467's long-command popup test). The
      real heading was always "(2/4)" before the click and "(3/4)" after -
      neither string contains a bare "2", so the old loose /2\/3|2/ regex
      could never match either one, and the step deterministically stalled
      the full 15s every single run once that fixture change landed.

      Fixed to assert the real (2/4) -> (3/4) transition, tightened to a
      bounded regex instead of the same "any bare digit" shape that hid
      this, plus an explicit wait for the marked checkbox's own
      detachment (a done item's checkbox is never re-created, so its
      absence is a stable, unambiguous signal the re-render landed) before
      trusting the heading text. Confirmed via two consecutive
      tools/browser-tests runs: required-actions.js clean both times.

