4.96  2026-08-31

    - TKT-668. required_item_update, checklist_add, and checklist_update
      stored whatever --status they were given, refusing only the empty
      string - nothing checked the value against the set the gates
      actually read. _item_is_done (lib/Tira/CLI.pm) compares
      lc(status) eq 'done', so a misspelling like 'Donee' or 'donw'
      silently marked an item that no gate would ever recognize as
      done: the command exited 0, the card looked updated, and the
      move-in refusal named the item as still outstanding for a reason
      nobody wrote down.

      required_item_update's half shipped standalone earlier this
      session: the declared set is {pending, done}, case-insensitive -
      the two values required_items ever carry.

      This release adds checklist_add/checklist_update's half, per
      Q-099's answer (asked on this card, answered by the owner
      2026-08-31): checklists get the same tight vocabulary plus 'To
      Do' - {pending, done, To Do}, case-insensitive. 'To Do' is
      included because it is the actual unmarked spelling this board
      itself writes on move-in, even though a required item never
      carries it. Both checklist_add (refusing at creation) and
      checklist_update (refusing at write) validate; reading and moving
      a card whose checklist already carries an older free-text status
      (written before this fix) is unaffected - the validation is at
      the point of writing, not a retroactive migration.

      Extended t/455-a-status-worth-more-than-its-spelling.t (already
      covering required_item_update): a misspelled status is refused
      at both checklist_add and checklist_update, naming the value and
      what works; pending/done/Done/DONE/To Do/to do/TO DO are all
      accepted, in every case, at both commands; a legacy free-text
      checklist status (simulated by writing the record directly,
      since checklist_add is validated too) reads back unchanged and
      the card can still be moved.

      docs/commands.md's required-action.update entry corrected: it
      previously said --status was free text with only the done
      comparison defined - stale since required_item_update's half of
      this fix shipped, caught during this card's own document gate.

      Caught during full-suite verify: required_item_update and
      checklist_update both ran the new status validation BEFORE the
      unknown-id check, the identical ordering mistake TKT-682's own
      verify caught in tasklist_task_ref_link - t/367 and t/366 each
      set up an id-not-found refusal test using a plausible but invalid
      status ('in progress'/'In Progress') purely as fixture noise, and
      the new validation intercepted first, masking the refusal being
      tested. Reordered so both now validate only after the entry is
      confirmed to exist, matching every other lookup-then-act command.
      Also fixed two unrelated fixture breaks the same coverage run
      surfaced: t/366's checklist_add setup used a bare 'Open' status
      (checklist_add validates now too - changed to 'pending'), and
      t/396's search test linked a tasklist item to a hardcoded,
      never-real ref 'FTT-1' (tasklist_task_ref_link validates
      existence now - given a real, dedicated card instead, with the
      assertion corrected to expect both that card's own ref-field
      match and the linked task's id, since a real ref can never dodge
      that collision the way a fake one could).

      Suite 452 files / 9000 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement
      and subroutine, measured serially.

4.95  2026-08-31

    - TKT-682. Corrected scope during investigation: this card originally
      claimed no command could link an existing tasklist item to a
      card, but tasklist.task.ref.link already existed (shipped
      2026-08-25, TKT-531) - the card's own dispatch-table check had
      only looked at the tasklist.* command range, missing the
      separately-namespaced tasklist.task.ref.* commands a few hundred
      lines earlier. What was genuinely missing: tasklist_task_ref_link
      accepted any --ref string with no check that it named a real
      record, so a typo'd or invented ref was stored exactly like a
      real link and read as solved to task-unlinked and everything else
      downstream, even though nothing was actually linked.

      Fixed by validating every --ref through the same _record_data
      lookup link_add already uses, inside the project lock and only
      after the item is confirmed to exist and belong to the caller's
      session - one bad ref among several good ones refuses the whole
      call rather than applying the rest, naming the ref that was not
      found. tasklist_task_ref_unlink is unchanged: removing a ref,
      valid or not, can never manufacture a false link. The
      task-unlinked bridge message now also names the command directly
      (tira.tasklist.task.ref.link --id ID --ref REF) instead of only
      describing linking in prose.

      Caught during full-suite verify: the ref check initially ran
      BEFORE the session-ownership check, so t/397/TKT-538's
      cross-session refusal test broke - a different session linking a
      bogus ref onto another session's item got "Record not found"
      instead of the expected "No task ID..." isolation message,
      leaking which problem applied to a caller never entitled to touch
      the item at all. Reordered so session ownership is always checked
      first, matching every other tasklist.task.* command.

      New test t/456-a-link-that-points-nowhere.t: a bad ref is refused
      and named, the task is left unchanged, a real ref still links, a
      mixed good+bad list refuses without partial application, unlink
      is unaffected, task-unlinked's own message is asserted to name the
      real command, and a cross-session attempt is refused as a session
      problem even when its ref is also bogus.

      Suite 452 files / 9000 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement
      and subroutine, measured serially.

