      serially.

4.88  2026-08-30

    - TKT-638. discard-unexplained is satisfied by any comment a card
      ever had, including one written before the discard.

      The rule's test was `next if @{ $record->{comments} // [] }` - it
      counted comments, never read them, and never asked when they were
      written. A card discarded after any earlier conversation, about
      anything unrelated to the discard, was silently exempt - most
      cards worth discarding have comments.

      Fixed by finding the last move-to-discard timestamp (via
      history_list on the column field) and requiring a comment written
      at or after it, with a body that is not empty or whitespace.
      comment_add itself stays permissive (stores body => text // ''
      with no validation) - deliberately: the gate is where an empty
      explanation is caught, not write time, and widening comment_add's
      own validation was not this ticket's fix to make.

      New test t/448-a-discard-that-explains-nothing-new.t covers all
      four cases: no comment, an old unrelated one, an empty/whitespace
      one written after the discard, and a genuine reason - only the
      last one clears the rule.

      Codex review caught a genuine gap in the first pass: comparing
      `created_at` and the discard timestamp with raw string `ge`
      instead of parsed epochs. Tira timestamps can legitimately carry
      different UTC offset spellings (Z, +0100, +0500) for the same or
      nearby instants, and a lexical comparison gets some of them
      backwards - a comment that is lexically "later" but genuinely
      predates the discard by real clock time incorrectly cleared the
      rule. Fixed to compare parsed epochs via _epoch_of_datetime, the
      same function every other instant comparison in this file already
      uses. A fifth test case pins the adversarial timestamp pair.

      Updated docs/POLICIES.md and SKILLS.md, including two other
      rules' own cross-references to discard-unexplained's old "any
      comment" shape, which this fix made stale.

      Suite 443 files / 8,990 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement
      and subroutine, measured serially.

4.87  2026-08-30

    - TKT-633. A date-time validator that accepted 2026-13-45T99:99:99Z,
      and a reader that died on it.

      _valid_datetime checked only the SHAPE of a date-time via regex -
      every field was just a digit count, so month 13, day 45, hour 99
      and an offset of +9999 all passed. The refusal it would have
      printed named ISO 8601, a standard the accepted values did not
      meet. _epoch_of_datetime, the other validator in this file - the
      one police and dwell actually use to do arithmetic on due_date and
      start_date - refused the same strings outright. A card could be
      given a --due-date or --start-date that was stored happily and
      then killed whatever read it back.

      Fixed by letting _epoch_of_datetime be the judge: _valid_datetime
      now delegates to it after its own shape check passes, so it
      accepts exactly what the rest of the system can read back - the
      day checked against its actual month and year via Time::Local,
      not just a digit range. The three widened offset forms TKT-572
      added are unaffected.

      The refusal now names which part is wrong and the value actually
      typed - "Due date Month '13' out of range 1..12" - rather than
      Time::Local's own 0-indexed internal number, and rather than
      repeating the generic "must be an ISO 8601 date-time" message for
      a value that was already shaped like one.

      Codex review caught a genuine gap in the first pass: delegating to
      _epoch_of_datetime alone still let the ticket's fourth measured
      value, an offset of +9999, through - _epoch_of_datetime does
      arithmetic with an offset's hours and minutes but never
      range-checks them, since every other caller reads an
      already-validated stored stamp. Fixed with an explicit offset
      range check in _valid_datetime itself, deliberately not in
      _epoch_of_datetime - widening that function's own tolerance was
      not this ticket's fix to make.

      New test t/447-a-date-nothing-can-actually-read.t: all four
      measured bad values refused (month/day/hour as one write, the
      offset separately), a plausible-but-wrong Feb 30th refused
      separately, a genuinely valid date-time still accepted, and the
      refusal message itself pinned.

      Suite 442 files / 8,983 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement
      and subroutine, measured serially.

4.86  2026-08-30

    - TKT-632. An unrelated --status on a move silently cancelled
      TKT-596's task reset.

      _reset_linked_tasks_on_return (lib/Tira/CLI.pm) called
      tasklist_list( %{$args}, all_sessions => 1 ) - the move command's
      ENTIRE argument set, not just what tasklist_list is meant to
      receive. Getopt parses every command's options through one shared
      @spec, so a move carrying --status (an option move itself does
      nothing with) handed that value straight to tasklist_list, which
      honours --status as a filter and dies on a value it does not
      recognise. That die was swallowed by "return if ref $items ne
      'ARRAY'" one line later, so a working task's status was silently
      never reset when its card went back to backlog - the exact side
      effect TKT-596 shipped this function to guarantee.

      Fixed by passing tasklist_list only project and all_sessions, and
      by reporting a genuine lookup failure to STDERR instead of
      swallowing it - the same promise the function already kept for
      tasklist_update one line lower down.

      Checked the neighbouring helper named in the ticket,
      _remind_one_at_a_time, for the same splat: it calls record_show,
      which has no status-filter concept and ignores unrecognised keys,
      so it was already safe - not a second instance of this bug.

      New test t/446-a-move-that-cancels-its-own-side-effect.t: a
      control move with no --status resets a working linked task; the
      same move carrying --status still resets it post-fix (it silently
      did not, pre-fix).

      Suite 441 files / 8,975 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement
      and subroutine, measured serially.

4.85  2026-08-30

    - TKT-613. Renaming a column strands every required item tagged with
      its old name, and the gate goes blind.

      column_rename renamed the column's directory and its config entry,
      but a required_items entry stores the column name as its own copy
      at the time required_item_add wrote it, not a live reference to
      the column record - so it stayed stamped with the OLD name forever.
      The push/departure gate matches a card's pending required items by
      its CURRENT column, so after a rename every item still tagged with
      the old name became invisible to that gate: a card could leave the
      column with required actions silently unmet.

      Fixed by walking every record across the whole type's board (not
      only the renamed column - a card that already moved on can still
      carry an item tagged with the name it left behind) inside the same
      rename, rewriting any required_items entry whose column matches
      the old name to the new one.

      New test t/444-a-rename-that-leaves-old-tags-behind.t: creates a
      card, moves it into a column, adds a required item tagged with
      that column, renames the column, and asserts the item's column tag
      followed the rename.

