4.82  2026-08-29

    - TKT-742. A refusal no longer suggests, as the correction, the exact
      string the caller just typed.

      A card about an option names that option in its title, so titles
      beginning with two dashes are ordinary here. Passed as a separate
      argument the parser reads the value as the next option, and the
      refusal then computed its "Did you mean" list from the caller's own
      value:

          ticket create --title "--set-scope-in refuses a plain file"
          Unknown option: set-scope-in
          Did you mean:
            --set-scope-in

      Naming --set-scope-in back to somebody who had just typed
      --set-scope-in reads as a correct option being rejected, and sends
      them to the option rather than to the quoting. Nothing mentioned
      that --title=VALUE works, which it does.

      The parse is Getopt::Long's documented behaviour and is unchanged.
      What changed is the message, and the two cases turned out to be
      distinguishable without touching the parser: the reported name is
      the first whitespace-delimited word of the rejected argument, so if
      it is EXACTLY an option the command declares, the argument cannot
      have been that option - a bare declared option parses. It was a
      value carrying more text. A misspelling never matches an exact
      declared name, so TKT-298's unknown-option message is untouched for
      the case it was written for, which three assertions pin.

      SKILLS.md's invocation grammar now says a value beginning with two
      dashes must be joined with =. That belonged beside the existing
      "quote whitespace, Markdown, glob characters" line, which implies
      quoting is sufficient - and here it is not, because the shell strips
      the quotes and the parser sees an option where a value was meant.

      Review found two problems in the first version of the fix, both
      corrected before this shipped. The example line named --title
      specifically ("--title=--set-scope-in ..."), which is right for the
      one call that motivated the card and wrong for every other command
      - the function has no way to know which earlier option was
      expecting the value, so it must not guess one. It now shows the
      value joined generically, "--option=--VALUE". And the test's key
      assertion checked only that the example text was present, which a
      hardcoded --title would also have satisfied; a direct unit test of
      the message-building function now proves it echoes whatever token
      it is given rather than anything memorised.

      That work turned up something the review had not: title:s is the
      only option in this CLI with an optional value, and this fault only
      reproduces on one of those - a required-value option (=s) simply
      takes the next token as its value whatever it looks like, measured
      directly against --description. The fix is written generically and
      is correct for any future :s option; the trigger today is narrower
      than the first draft implied.

