      are the two loops that keep doing so", where "so" was judging the
      board. bridge_follow never judges anything - it streams what a pass
      already recorded, and police_pass is called from exactly three
      places, none of them the bridge. Corrected, and the three are now
      listed there, because the module that owns the knowledge is where a
      reader of the code looks for it.

      Review caught the entry naming `d2 tira.police.run`, which is not a
      command - police_run is the body behind `d2 tira.police`, and
      dispatch recognises only police and policy.bridge. A false command
      name in the normative document, in the entry being corrected for
      omitting a real option, is the fault this card is about, arriving
      while the card was being written. It now names the two commands
      that do run a pass and says what --once does to the first.

      The guard was strengthened by the same review. Matching a flag in
      CLI.pm's option table proves the parser accepts the spelling, not
      that this command reads it - the table is shared by every command.
      Each flag is now also required to be consumed in Police.pm.

      Half of this card was already delivered when it was picked up:
      docs/POLICIES.md gained the who-runs-a-pass explanation with
      TKT-684's own documentation, after the card was filed. That is
      recorded on the card rather than rewritten here.

4.80  2026-08-29

    - TKT-747. record.create refuses instead of crashing when there is no
      project. Run a create outside a board and the answer was a Perl error -
      "Can't use an undefined value as a HASH reference at
      lib/Tira/CLI/Records.pm line 36" - where the other commands that look
      for a board say "No Tira project found from '...'"; comment.add and
      record.show were both measured giving it in the identical condition.
      It is now the same refusal as record.show's, byte for byte, and it
      names the directory it searched.

      One line of precedence. eval BLOCK returns undef when its block dies,
      so eval {...}->{entry} applied the arrow to that undef OUTSIDE the
      eval's protection; column_roles reaches discover_project, which dies
      when there is no project, so the one failure the eval existed to
      tolerate was the one that crashed it. Assigning before dereferencing
      lets that die reach the caller. No other call site in lib/ dereferences
      an eval's result: the rest either assign it to a scalar first, or do
      the dereference inside the eval block where it is protected.

      The eval stays, and two of the four new assertions exist to keep it
      that way: TKT-428 added it so a board declaring no entry role still
      creates cards, and the plausible wrong fix - deleting the eval rather
      than moving the dereference out of it - passes every failing assertion
      and breaks those boards. Present since 2.96.

4.79  2026-08-29

    - TKT-606. police announces a task that arrives, and one that disappears.
      He asked on 2026-08-27 why a new task was announced by nothing - "broken?
      missing policy?" - and asked again on 2026-08-29 about another one,
      because the card raised off the first question was still in the backlog.
      task-changed was declared and firing the whole time. It reports edits to
      items it has already seen, and a brand-new item has nothing to compare
      against, so the branch was skipped - while the line after it wrote the
      item into the baseline anyway. The first pass adopted it in silence and
      every later pass found nothing changed. A new task was not announced
      late; it was unannounceable.

      This needed a decision rather than a missing else. On a board with an
      existing tasklist and a ledger no pass has written, EVERY item is a first
      sighting, and a rule that announced a hundred at once would be declined
      and never re-enabled - worse than the silence it replaced. The ledger
      already carried what separates the two cases and the code was throwing it
      away: a store nothing has written has no task_seen key at all, and
      _task_changed_mark_seen is the only thing that sets one, so `// {}` was
      flattening "no baseline" and "a baseline that happens to be empty" into
      one value. A first pass on an unpoliced board still announces nothing; a
      board that has been policed reports the next arrival.

      Removal comes from the same record, and it was half-written already:
      %still_here was populated in this rule and read nowhere in the file, so a
      task vanishing was unannounced by the same silence that swallowed a new
      one. An id that was in the baseline and is not on the list now is named,
      because a list one shorter says nothing on its own.

      Both are said once and settle. Edits to known items are unchanged, and
      t/399's own assertion that "a freshly-added item is not reported" stays
      green untouched - that is a first pass on a fresh store, which is exactly
      the case that must keep adopting silently.

      WHICH RULE CARRIES IT WAS ASKED AND ANSWERED. Q-097, answered while this
      was in verify: both - arrivals ride task-changed now, and a task-created
      rule splits out later. So this ships as built and a follow-up card holds
      the split (TKT-758), rather than the rule being shipped and renamed in
      one release.

      The cost he chose is that boards see the behaviour twice, once when
      arrivals start arriving under task-changed and again when the rule that
      sends them changes name. Worth saying here: when the source of task
      announcements changes on developer-dashboard and zenandi, that is this
      decision landing and not a regression. The alternatives were a rule named
      "changed" permanently reporting things that arrived, or an opt-in rule
      that would have left the boards which prompted this card silent longest.

      FOLDED IN BEFORE THIS RELEASE WAS EVER PUSHED (TKT-761): SKILLS.md's own
      description of this rule still said "a freshly-added item is not
      reported" - true before this card, false the moment it shipped, and
      exactly the belief that made him ask twice in the first place. Caught by
      the next documentation-gap hunt, three hours after this card was first
      committed, while it still sat in pending-push. Corrected here, in the
      same release, along with a second copy of the identical sentence in
      docs/POLICIES.md's onboarding walkthrough that a first pass at the
      correction missed - one document repeating a false claim in two places,
      caught only because a review question asked broadly rather than about
      the one passage a bug report had pointed at.

Suite 433 files / 8,894 tests green. lib/Tira.pm 100.0% statement and
subroutine, measured serially.
4.78  2026-08-29

    - TKT-684. A clean board and a stopped detector no longer give the same
      answer. tira.police.outstanding reports what is outstanding AS OF THE
      LAST PASS, and on a clean board that is an empty list - identical to the
      answer from a board whose policy bridge died eleven hours ago. Measured
      on a real board: a pass at 03:38:41 read at 14:41:50, unchanged, while
      the board called itself clean all day and a card-duration policy sat an
      hour past its age in that silence. Two cards were filed off the back of
      it by someone who could not tell, and who did the arithmetic by hand
      across ten invocations.

      The new tira.police.freshness answers when the last pass ran, how long
      ago, and whether that is recent enough to trust - { taken_at,
      age_seconds, stale } under -o json, so a caller acts on one field rather
      than parsing a timestamp. A board nobody has policed is reported STALE
      with a null pass time, because "nothing has been checked" and "nothing is
      wrong" must not be the same answer; reporting an age of zero there would
      put a confident number on an absence. Stale means older than 300 seconds,
      ten times the watcher's default interval, since a bridge that has missed
      ten consecutive passes has stopped rather than run late. A stored pass
      time that will not parse is stale too, with no age invented for it and
      the word UNREADABLE in the human output - review caught the first version
      answering "fresh" for a corrupt stamp, which is this command's own fault
      turned inward. Three ways to be stale, missing, unreadable and old, and
      they are one idea: in none of them can the board's silence be trusted.

      And tira.police.outstanding now judges the age it was already printing,
      in both outputs, with the warning BEFORE the reassurance - a reader who
      has got as far as "No violations outstanding" has stopped reading. The
      non-empty case gets it too: "5 outstanding, as of the pass at <ts>" reads
      as a live count, and a count from eleven hours ago is not one.

      THE PAYLOAD IS UNCHANGED, and that is the decision rather than an
      omission. -o json stays a bare list because two other projects pipe and
      index it in the loop they use to decide whether work is finished, and
      docs/commands.md promises them a list. The owner chose it explicitly over
      the alternative. TKT-354 chose one-shape-always for tira.next in 3.48 and
      that precedent does not transfer - that command had no documented
      consumers outside this board. The cost of a second command, a question
      answered somewhere other than where it is asked, is paid by outstanding
      itself naming the new command in its stale warning.

      Also corrected: the comment at lib/Tira/CLI/Police.pm justifying the bare
      list said it stayed one because "TKT-354 is already open". TKT-354 closed
      in 3.48 deciding the opposite. That deferral had cited a card that
      decided against it for a year of releases, and nobody noticed, because a
      deferral reads like a reason.

4.77  2026-08-29

    - TKT-593. A coverage refusal says which lines. The gate printed
      "lib/Tira/CLI.pm 99.8 100.0 99.8" and stopped, while the Devel::Cover
      database that knows exactly which statement is missing sat in cover_db
      beside it. Finding the line by hand cost a session twice: CLI.pm 4503,
      4619 and 4620 after a night of guessing, and lib/Tira.pm:12134 this week
      after two failed attempts at parsing the text report's columns. The new
      tools/coverage-holes asks the database instead and prints file:line for
      every uncovered statement and subroutine, the subroutine by name as well,
      since "sub never_called" is what a reader searches for. The percentage
      line stays - it is what proves the threshold was applied - and the lines
      go beneath it.

      Three things came out of building it that the card could not have known.
      merge_runs is not optional: without it the database reports zero files and
      the helper prints nothing, which reads exactly like a clean tree, so the
      tool would have contained the fault it exists to remove. The helper has to
      run inside the container, because gate-run deletes cover_db there and
      judges coverage afterwards on the host from captured text, so that is the
      only moment the answer can be taken - and it runs under || true, because a
      gate that refused because its explanation broke would be worse than one
      that explains nothing. And it caps at twenty lines while always saying how
      many it held back: a partial run of one test file produces 5,625 uncovered
      lines in lib/Tira.pm alone, and a refusal that prints those has replaced
      one unusable output with another. --all prints every one.

      A scoped code review then found two defects in the parts that handle
      things going wrong, which is the region a green suite never visits. The
      host grepped for the module path with grep -E, so every "." in a path
      matched any character and lib/Tira/CLI.pm would also have matched
      lib/Tira/CLIXpm - no path in lib/ triggers it today, which is why it was
      worth fixing before one does. And the database traversal sat outside the
      eval that guards the calls before it, so a malformed database could abort
      the helper with an uncaught exception instead of the exit 2 it promises -
      a diagnostic crashing while explaining a failure being the fault it exists
      to remove.

      A missing or unreadable database exits 2 and says so, rather than printing
      nothing and being read as a clean gate - the same fault TKT-684 is open
      about for police.outstanding, one tool over, and not worth shipping a
      second instance of in the same week.

