4.69  2026-08-28

    - TKT-645. A source file attached to a card can be read on the card.
      The viewer previewed nine extensions and offered every other file
      as a download, so a .pl attached on the owner's own instruction
      came back as "this file cannot be shown". Whether something is
      text is now decided once, in the engine: named lists cover the
      extensions where guessing would be worse than knowing - a .pl file
      is Perl whatever its first bytes look like, and a .zip whose bytes
      happen to read as printable must never be shown as text - and
      anything else is decided by reading the first 8KB, where a NUL
      byte or more than a tenth of the bytes outside the printable range
      means binary. Nothing to read means refusal rather than a guess.
    - The viewer's own extension list is gone. It kept a second one
      while the engine already computed a content type, which is the
      same fault TKT-224, TKT-671, TKT-681 and TKT-657 each fixed in
      their own corner this week: two readers of one question. The
      viewer now reads content_type and holds no list.
    - The twelve languages named on the card are highlighted by a 4,572
      byte tokeniser embedded in the page. The board makes no network
      requests, so a CDN highlighter was never available; highlight.js
      carrying these languages is roughly twenty times that size. HTML
      and XML needed their own branch - tags are not keywords.
    - The card dialog is given content_type, which it previously was
      not. record_show does not compute the field and still does not:
      that stats the stored file and sometimes reads it, on every gate,
      police pass and board render. The detail provider asks
      attachment_list, which already computes it, and stamps the answer
      on by sha - comment, question and answer attachments included,
      since the viewer opens those from the same strip. Without this the
      preview was dead on every real card while passing in a fixture
      that supplied the field the server did not.
    - The dialog's stamp is keyed on sha and extension together, not on
      sha alone. Attachments are content-addressed, so the same bytes
      attached under two names are two entries sharing one sha while
      needing different types - a valid SVG is also valid text, and the
      .svg copy came back as text/plain and would have opened in the
      text pane. Found in review and reproduced before it was fixed.
    - t/424 asserts the served dashboard script parses. The tokeniser
      needed a literal backslash, the page's JavaScript is built inside
      a Perl q{} where \\ collapses to one, and the browser was served a
      quote escaping itself - the whole 98KB script failed to load. All
      8,602 tests passed against that dead board, because the markup
      they assert was still correct. node --check now runs over the
      served script inside the suite.

4.68  2026-08-28

    - TKT-657. Every reader of a required item's status now agrees that
      'Done' means done.
      lib/Tira/CLI.pm compared a status against 'done' in four places.
      Three lowercased first; _remind_one_at_a_time did not. So an item
      marked --status Done - the capital the CLI accepts, and which
      TKT-434 deliberately made the gates tolerate - was DONE to every
      gate and OUTSTANDING to the move-in reminder, which then told an
      agent to work items already finished.
      Fixed as one named predicate, _item_is_done, with four callers,
      rather than as the one-word lowercase the card first proposed.
      The card raised that choice itself and left it to whoever picked
      it up; the deciding argument is that this was the THIRD instance
      of one fault in a single day. The dashboard compared against the
      literal 'done' (TKT-601). tools/card-holes did it twice, in
      opposite directions - one refusing a good push, one silently
      blind - and refused the release of 4.57, 4.58 and 4.59 (TKT-671).
      These four were the third. TKT-671 shipped a ledger that greps
      tools/ for exactly this fault and cannot see lib/, and four
      inline comparisons cannot be guarded as a set - a named predicate
      can be grepped for, which is what makes a fourth drift catchable.
      NOTHING IS NORMALISED ON WRITE. 'Done' stays 'Done' on the card;
      the predicate is the one place that reads it. Undoing that would
      undo TKT-434, and the test pins it so nobody 'fixes' the symptom
      at the wrong end.
      The reachable path is narrower than it looks, and the first
      version of the test got it wrong. Moving a card OUT and back
      makes the reminder fire correctly, because a backward move resets
      that column's done items to pending on the way through - so that
      scenario reproduced the reset, not the bug, and would have gone
      green on the fix for the wrong reason. The bug needs a FORWARD
      move into a column whose item is already Done, which is reachable
      because an item can carry a column the card has not reached yet:
      seeded there on creation since 4.67, or added directly. The test
      now pins that distinction in its own assertion.

4.67  2026-08-28

    - TKT-681. A card created through the CLI straight into a column is
      no longer born past that column's entry gate. The browser's own
      create flow calls create_record directly, seeds neither template,
      and is unchanged - excluded by this card's scope rather than
      overlooked.
      Every column carries two templates: required_actions, which a card
      must satisfy to LEAVE, and entry_required_actions, which it must
      have satisfied to be moved IN. The move path places both. The
      create path placed only the first, under a comment saying it
      mirrored the move-in logic "exactly" - and it did: it mirrored
      _populate_column_required_actions, which is the EXIT seeder. Entry
      actions on a move come from a different function,
      _populate_entry_required_actions, which creation never called.
      So a card created into such a column received no entry items,
      nothing checked it, and the gate was not failed but skipped in
      silence - which is the exact failure the whole required-action
      mechanism exists to prevent.
      The create path now calls that function rather than repeating its
      logic. The cause of this bug was one path copying another's logic
      instead of calling it, and a third copy would have been the same
      mistake a third time.
      CREATION IS NEVER REFUSED FOR AN UNSATISFIED ENTRY ACTION, and
      cannot be. The owner's reasoning, on TSK-250: "where do they store
      the proof when there is no ticket?" A required action's proof is a
      command and its output, and before the card exists there is
      nothing to run a command against. The items are recorded pending
      and the caller is told instead.
      And it is TOLD, to STDERR, each item named with its own REQ id and
      the closing command carrying a real one rather than a placeholder,
      so acting on the message is copying what was printed rather than
      cross-referencing texts against required-action.list - the same
      reasoning the refused-move message follows. Both kinds are named
      by what each is for -
      "N entry required action(s), owed now" against "N exit required
      action(s), owed before it leaves COLUMN" - and ending with the
      command to work them. To STDERR because stdout is the card, and
      -o json has to stay a document an agent can parse; the browser
      move path already reports entry-population failures the same way.
      THE WARNING FIXES A SILENCE OLDER THAN THE ONE THIS CARD WAS
      RAISED FOR. The exit template has been seeded on create since
      TKT-439 and printed by nothing, so an agent met those items only
      when a move was refused. Warning about the entry items alone would
      have left that exactly as it was.
      Two assertions in the new t/421 were wrong before they were right,
      and both are recorded on the card. One checked whether the refused
      move DIED - Tira::CLI catches a refusal and prints it, so the
      anchor failed against working code and would have read as "entry
      gates are broken". The other was green against code that printed
      no warning at all: -o toon prints the whole created card, whose
      own required_items list contains the item text, so an assertion
      against stdout passes on the record dump whether or not anything
      was printed. All three warning assertions read STDERR alone now.
      One checklist item on the card turned out to have a false premise
      and is recorded rather than actioned: it asked for the same fix on
      the move path, but the move path already places entry items
      correctly, and adding them to the exit seeder would have placed
      them a second time on every move.

4.66  2026-08-28

    - TKT-671. An item ticked as 'Done' was finished to the card and
      unfinished to the push gate.
      It stopped a real release rather than being found by looking. The
      push of 4.57, 4.58 and 4.59 was refused with "TKT-625 is in push
      with 1 checklist item(s) unfinished" and "TKT-626 is in push with
      4 checklist item(s) unfinished" - and every one of those items
      was marked done. They were stored as 'Done', which is the natural
      capitalisation and matches the 'To Do' the column templates
      themselves use. lib/Tira.pm lowercases before comparing;
      tools/card-holes did not, so the card counted 4 of 4 and the gate
      counted 3. Unblocked at the time by normalising nine items to
      lowercase across three cards, which the card itself recorded as a
      workaround rather than a fix: the next agent to write 'Done' hits
      it again.
      THE TWO SITES FAILED IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, and only the loud
      one had been noticed. premature() counted a 'Done' item as
      waiting and refused a good push. stalled() took a 'Done' item as
      proof the checklist was unfinished and returned early - so the
      check that exists because Michael caught TKT-005 fully ticked and
      still sitting in implement was, against 'Done', permanently
      blind. One shouted falsely and the other stayed quiet falsely,
      which is why lowercasing the reported line would have traded a
      false alarm for a silence.
      The gate no longer holds an opinion about the question that
      matters. record_list already attaches checklist_done and
      checklist_total to every row, and a checklist counts as finished
      only when that count says so AND no item on the card contradicts
      it - the count confirms rather than overrules. A review asked
      what a count-only shortcut does when the two disagree, and the
      answer was that stalled() announces "every checklist item is
      done" beside an item visibly marked To Do. They cannot disagree
      on a record from record_list, both being computed over the same
      array in the same call, so requiring agreement costs nothing and
      removes the one case where the gate would contradict what it
      prints. Working out WHICH items to name in
      the message is still card-holes' own, comparing each status
      itself - case-insensitively now, so the two agree. The
      completeness decision has one implementation; the naming has a
      second one that t/420 holds to agreeing, which is the card's own
      acceptance criterion in its own words: "either they share one
      implementation, or a test asserts they agree." Said precisely
      here because the first draft of the documentation claimed the
      gate had stopped classifying items at all, which a review caught
      as an overstatement - the distinction is where the next drift
      would go.
      That is the arrangement TKT-224 already established for the
      definition of a complete card: two readers, one definition,
      nothing to drift. Lowercasing both sites would have left two
      implementations that merely happened to agree, which the card's
      own acceptance criteria warn against.
      The truncated list says so now. The count was always honest and
      the list never was - "4 checklist item(s) unfinished:" followed
      by three names, with nothing to say the fourth had been dropped.
      It ends "(and 1 more)".
      And a ledger, which is the only part that stops a fifth: this is
      the FOURTH instance of one fault. The dashboard had it (TKT-601),
      these two, and the move-in reminder still does (TKT-657, its own
      card). A test now greps tools/ for a case-sensitive comparison of
      an item's status and fails when one appears.
      Three faults in the work itself, each recorded on the card rather
      than quietly fixed. The ledger first grepped for the bare string
      and matched a legitimate COLUMN comparison, so it would have
      stayed red after the real fix and taught its next reader to
      loosen it. The docstring explaining the fix quoted the old
      expression and tripped the ledger it was describing - the same
      way a POD comment tripped its own test on TKT-601. And the
      truncation assertion forbade the slice outright, when the fix
      still slices and should: the fault was never the slice, it was
      the silence beside it.

4.65  2026-08-28

    - TKT-639. Police notices when a task and the card it names tell two
      different stories.
      The owner asked by Telegram for a thirty-minute check covering
      three things: that each task's status is still correct, which
      tasks have no card linked, and which say working while the card
      they name sits in backlog - reported on the bridge so the agent
      deals with it rather than somebody remembering to look. Half of
      it existed. task-unlinked (TKT-547) catches a task with no card;
      nothing caught a task whose status contradicts the card it does
      name, so the queue and the board could disagree indefinitely and
      only a person reading both would notice. Counted by hand the day
      it was raised, across 51 tasks and 47 cards: seven mismatches -
      five tasks still saying working after their cards had moved to
      push, one saying pending while its card was in implement, and one
      card carrying the same note twice.
      The new task-card-mismatch rule reports three directions from one
      comparison: a task saying working about a card in a column nobody
      called work, a task saying pending about a card being worked, and
      a task not yet done about a card that has gone past the work
      columns altogether - the card finished and the loose end stayed
      open. It also reports a card carrying the same note twice.
      WHICH COLUMNS MEAN WORK IS DECLARED WITH --column AND THAT IS THE
      WHOLE DESIGN. The hand-run script this replaces
      counted next-to-work-on among its working columns, and that one
      line made seven of its eight findings false, every thirty
      minutes: a card waiting to be picked up has not been started, so
      its task saying pending is the board telling the truth. The board
      cannot infer the distinction either - backlog and discard are
      protected and next-to-work-on is not, so "unprotected means work"
      sweeps it straight back in. A rule that reported those would be
      right about the arithmetic, wrong about the board, and would bury
      its real findings in its own noise, which is why the three
      controls in the test file matter as much as the findings.
      The columns are one set however they are spelled, and getting
      that wrong was the sharpest thing the test caught. A policy's
      --column is a SCOPE everywhere else in the engine - "the same
      rule watching a different column is a different intention, not a
      duplicate" - which is right for every rule that acts on the
      column it names. This one does not: its columns say what work
      MEANS. Read the ordinary way, a board declaring its four working
      columns as four policies had each policy reporting the other
      three's honest tasks, noise no single policy could explain. Found
      when a second policy declared over the first turned two
      assertions in the new test green for entirely the wrong reason,
      and traced further when policy_add turned an arrayref --column
      into the string "ARRAY(0x...)" - so the repeatable flag the first
      draft documented had never existed, and the code branch reading
      it was dead. Policies of this rule now compose: several policies,
      one --column carrying a comma-separated list the way project.new
      already takes its columns, or both, all describe the same set,
      and the pass reports from the first of them. Both spellings are
      asserted to agree, with a control proving the silence is the rule
      agreeing rather than the rule switched off.
      --column-role counts as a third spelling, which answers a
      question the card raised - whether the working set should come
      from column roles rather than being named. policy_add had already
      settled it: its needs check accepts a --column-role wherever a
      --column is required, so a board could declare this rule by role
      whether or not the rule read one. A branch reading only the
      column would then compute an EMPTY working set from a declaration
      the engine accepted without complaint - and an empty set does not
      fail quietly, it reports every working task on the board as a
      mismatch and calls every genuine pending-on-work case honest,
      exactly inverted. Roles resolve, and a set that resolves to
      nothing makes the rule say nothing, leaving policy-unfollowable
      to report the declaration it cannot read.
      The third direction needs no second option: columns are declared
      in flow order, so anything after the last column named in
      --column is somewhere a card only reaches by being finished with
      it, read per record type since a sow, an epic and a ticket each
      have their own column list.
      Duplicates match on the first sixty characters, lowercased and
      trimmed, which is the reference script's own test and is crude in
      the safe direction - for a long note it catches two sharing an
      opening clause, for a short one the texts must match exactly. It
      misses duplicates rather than inventing them, because
      multiplicity alone is not a fault: an incoming message, a
      follow-up question and a scope change legitimately name one card.
      A pair that is entirely done is left alone, since the complaint
      is that one gets ticked while the other lingers and there is
      nothing left to linger.
      No --age - a contradiction is wrong the moment it exists.
      Whole-board like task-unlinked, and it reads the tasklist file
      directly so it sweeps every session rather than the caller's own
      corner of it - tasklist_list would have scoped it to the caller's
      session, which the test pins by recording a mismatch under a
      different session id and requiring it to be reported.
      It says at most one thing per task per pass, and that was found
      by walking the card's own six test_steps through the real CLI
      rather than by reading. The violation ledger keys an entry by
      rule, policy and reference, so two findings about one task under
      one policy are ONE entry - same VIO number, same first_seen, same
      seen count - and the quiet ladder lets only the first speak. A
      task both left working on a backlogged card and written twice
      produced VIO-0003 twice in a single pass, the second marked
      quiet; the bridge line for that number would then describe
      whichever finding spoke while its "seen N times" claimed one
      continuous history. The status mismatch wins, being what the card
      was raised for, and the duplicate is said as soon as the status
      is put right - the order somebody would fix them in anyway.
      Three more came out of an adversarial code review of the finished
      rule, and none of them by reading it again. A task carrying the
      same ref twice - storable today, because tasklist_add keeps refs
      as given while only tasklist_task_ref_link dedupes, and --ref is
      repeatable - met itself on its second turn through that ref and
      was reported as duplicating ITSELF, its own id quoted back at it.
      The duplicate walk did not keep the silence the status walk
      keeps, so two tasks sharing an opening clause about a ref naming
      no card were reported as duplicates of each other, a complaint
      about bookkeeping on a card nobody can open. And the column-order
      build dereferenced column_list with no guard, where card_holes
      already guards the identical call: police_pass wraps rule
      evaluation in an eval and sets found = [] when it throws, so
      nothing crashes - the pass simply goes silent, for every rule at
      once, on the board least able to afford it.
      Two of the assertions written for those fixes passed against the
      unfixed code on their first run and were rewritten rather than
      counted. One repeated this card's own earlier fixture mistake:
      texts of 59 and 68 characters that diverged at the comma, so
      their first-sixty keys never collided and no duplicate was ever
      possible. The other asserted that the pass survived a failing
      column_list, which it does either way - the thing that dies is
      the findings, so that is what it asserts now. All three go red
      against the unfixed rule and green against the fixed one, checked
      by reverting each fix and running the file again.
      The push question acceptance_criteria asked to settle is settled
      by the option rather than by an exemption: a board that counts
      push as work declares push, and one that does not is told its
      card has reached push with the task still open. Five of the seven
      mismatches in the hand-run measurement were exactly that, and
      they were real. It reports and does nothing else; closing the gap
      stays the agent's job, which is what the owner asked for.

4.64  2026-08-28

    - TKT-628. A required action can say what it is running before it
      can prove it.
      The owner asked for a state between picking an item up and
      finishing it (TSK-194): "Agent can provide the command for a
      specific item first, so the user can see it is working on it".
      A list of pending items cannot say which one is being worked
      right now, and the dashboard had no way to show it.
      It was not merely absent. _proof_entries_for returned undef
      unless the status was exactly done, so a --command arriving
      with --status pending was parsed by the CLI, accepted, and
      dropped before the pair logic was reached. The call succeeded,
      nothing was stored, the caller was told nothing - the same
      shape as TKT-625's ignored --dry-run.
      Now a command may arrive ahead of its proof. It is recorded,
      the item keeps its status, and the card dialog marks it with a
      clock. It travels with a --status or an --item rather than
      entirely alone: both update commands still refuse a call that
      changes nothing, so --status pending --command is the shape,
      and passing the status the item already has leaves it there. The
      proof follows when the command has produced something,
      repeating its command. What marks the item done is still
      --status done, which refuses without that pair; a full pair
      given with --status pending is accepted, stored, and leaves the
      item pending. Evidence is recorded and status is asserted, and
      they were never the same act. Repeating rather than attaching, deliberately: attaching
      would make an announcement addressable and give a done-claim a
      second route, and TKT-585 spent this morning closing the first
      cheap one.
      This adds a state BEFORE done and does not weaken the gate.
      Marking done still costs a matching pair, an empty supplied
      half is still refused, and six assertions that were green
      before the change are green after.
      THE ENGINE HALF REACHES CHECKLIST ITEMS TOO, and a reader
      looking for why checklist_update stopped discarding should not
      have to find this card by its title. The early return was never
      scoped to one family, so leaving checklists dropping a lone
      command would have meant ADDING a condition to preserve the bug
      for one of them. Q-092: "Fix the engine for both, render the
      clock on required actions only." Only required actions get the
      clock.
      SIX things the change created rather than found, two caught
      while building and four by a code review that sent the card
      back to tests-red. All six share one cause: a new kind of thing
      now flows through everything that reads the proof field, and
      each of those readers had to learn which of two states it is
      looking at.
      The per-pair loop checked --proof for emptiness
      unconditionally, which would have refused the very state this
      adds; it now checks only a proof that was actually offered.
      _log_proof_gate fired on "did any evidence arrive", so
      announcing wrote 'Marked "..." done' into gate_passing_log -
      the board's own record of what was proved.
      _refuse_reused_proof signatures a missing proof as the empty
      string, so two items announcing the same command were charged
      with reusing evidence that did not exist. Announcements are now
      filtered out before it signatures anything.
      An announcement could overwrite evidence behind a done claim:
      renaming a done item while announcing replaced its proof with a
      command-only entry and left the status done, which is the state
      TKT-583 and TKT-585 exist to prevent, reached from a new
      direction. Announcing over a done item is refused unless
      --status re-opens it deliberately.
      That guard and the gate-log condition both read the RESULTING
      status rather than what the caller passed - keyed off the
      argument alone, a done item given fresh evidence without
      repeating --status stored it and logged nothing.
      And the empty-half refusal forks on which half is empty rather
      than on the status, so a blank --proof is no longer told that an
      empty command announces nothing.
      An announcement that is never proved stays visible. That is the
      point rather than a leak: an item saying it started and never
      finished is something somebody should see.
      The usage line changes shape with the behaviour, from
      [--command TEXT --proof TEXT ...] to
      [--command TEXT [--proof TEXT] ...] - the proof nested inside
      the command rather than beside it, because the two are required
      together only for done.
      20 assertions in t/418 and 6 in a real browser, which assert
      the four states are four DISTINCT marks and that an announced
      item still offers its checkbox - or the clock would have
      quietly become a second way of saying done.

4.63  2026-08-28

    - TKT-601. The dashboard reads a required action's status the way
      the engine reads it.
      The engine has compared against "done" case-insensitively since
      TKT-434, deliberately: "--status Done" must not be refused
      forever with a message naming the very word the person already
      used. The browser dashboard never got the same fix and compared
      against the literal "done" in three places - the done/total
      count, the tick-or-empty-box icon, and whether an actionable
      checkbox is drawn.
      So an item stored as "Done" was finished to the gate and
      outstanding to the page. Measured on a copy of a real board:
      534 items stored that way against 2068 lowercase, mis-rendering
      across 21 cards, and two of them - ZSD-286 and ZSD-287 - showing
      all 97 of their required actions as empty boxes while carrying
      proof arrays and gate_passing_log entries that prove them done.
      The owner reported it twice, the second time as still happening.
      The third comparison is why this was a trap rather than a
      blemish. The count and the icon mislead; the checkbox invites.
      It offered a live control to complete work that was already
      complete, and taking it up re-runs required_item_update on a
      done item, which since 4.48 can meet the duplicate-proof
      refusal - so the page could walk somebody into a refusal with a
      control that should never have been drawn.
      All three now go through one predicate, isDone, placed beside
      el() where TKT-586 put its three shared editor helpers for the
      same reason. Three corrected copies would have left three
      copies, and three independent copies is how one of them came to
      disagree in the first place. It reaches the same answer as the
      engine on every value either has been given - a missing key,
      null, the empty string, whitespace, a numeric zero and the
      string "0" all read as not-done on both sides - by a route that
      is not quite identical: a numeric 0 is discarded to "" by the ||
      before toLowerCase sees it, where Perl lowercases it to "0" and
      compares that. The empty-string default IS deliberately the
      same, and it is what lets an item written before the status
      field existed render as outstanding rather than throwing and
      taking the dialog down.
      Nothing is normalised on disk: "Done" stays "Done" and is
      simply read correctly. The checklist renderer, which prints its
      status verbatim and compares nothing, is untouched - and a test
      now requires it to stay that way, so a later fix that went
      looking for "status" cannot quietly change a renderer that was
      already right.
23 checks across two files, and 15 of them passed before the
      change - which is the number that matters, because those are what
      stop an overcorrection rendering everything as done.
      t/417 is 10 assertions (prove reports 1..10), 7 green before the
      fix. It reads the comparisons out of the source and asserts the
      OUTCOME rather than the shape, so it would have passed either the
      shared predicate or three corrections in place - the card left
      that decision open and the test was written not to close it.
      t/playwright/mixed-case-done.js emits 13 checks on a clean run,
      8 of them green before the fix. It drives a real browser against
      a card carrying six required actions in four states - done, Done,
      DONE, pending, todo, and one that is both Done and exempt.

