    - TKT-680. The push hook stopped re-running the suite that verify
      had already run.
      It cost about twenty minutes on every release. Measured on the
      release of 4.60 and 4.61 the same morning: the suite ran at 08:41
      in verify and again at 11:47 in the hook, over a tree the first
      run had cleared, and the push took 21 minutes 19 seconds. The
      first attempt was killed by a foreground timeout and left an
      orphaned container still running the suite - a cost of the length
      rather than an accident beside it.
      The gate-cache built to stop that double-run (TKT-351) could not.
      It keys on the tree HEAD carries, and the verify suite runs before
      the documentation and version commits exist, so the tree it
      records is never the tree being pushed. 93 records, none matching.
      It leaves with the suite it existed to skip; tools/gate-run still
      writes those records for anyone proving a tree by hand, and is
      otherwise untouched.
      The owner's rule is a statement about where readiness is decided
      rather than about speed - "anything landed on push column is ready
      to pushed" - and asked what should replace the suite, "only trim
      away the full test run. keep other checks". So this is a
      subtraction and nothing else. A new, separate step reading
      fix_version and evidence off each card was proposed and declined,
      and is recorded in the card's scope_excluded so it is not
      reintroduced by someone reading the older text. It would also have
      been redundant: tools/card-holes, which stays, already refuses a
      card with no recorded gate, no evidence or no fix version - so the
      check was already there under another name, which is why "keep
      other checks" was the right answer rather than merely the owner's
      preference.
      Seven checks stay, and their order with them: the version against
      what is shipping, the board backup, the live-card completeness
      check and tools/card-holes before, and tools/docs-match-code,
      tools/docs-examples-run and tools/browser-tests after. Those three
      are last on purpose - documentation edited after a gate has
      shipped a broken build here twice - and that is what still defends
      "nothing was edited between the proof and the push", which is what
      the suite step was really for.
      Two consequences that are easy to miss and are handled: the hook's
      closing line announced a suite result and a coverage figure it no
      longer has, and tools/prove-the-gate declared coverage for seven
      refusals the hook can no longer make, with five live probes behind
      them. A probe left after its refusal has gone does not fail - it
      PASSES against a hook that never had the chance to refuse, and
      reports the gate proved, which is the exact shape that tool exists
      to end. Both are removed, along with the fake docker that fed the
      hook fabricated suite output and the PASSING_SUITE fixture behind
      it.
      t/233's floor on how many refusals the hook has drops from 15 to
      9, keeping its margin of two against the real count, which is now
      11 rather than 17. It is a parse guard, not a target.
      41 assertions in t/416, 24 of which pass before the change and
      must go on passing: they are what stops the subtraction taking
      more than it was asked for. Three existing tests turned out to
      depend on the removed block and none of them was foreseen -
      t/179 asserted the worktree, the container mount, the removal
      and the EXIT trap in the HOOK, and t/279 asserted the hook
      consults the gate-cache. Both were repointed at tools/gate-run,
      where the property moved, rather than deleted: the claim that a
      release is judged on the commit and not on the desk is still
      true, and only the file keeping it changed.

