    - TKT-586. The card dialog's three text editors stopped being
      three different things.
      The owner reported the description editor as "un-usable": a
      fixed five-row textarea at roughly half the modal's width, with
      no formatting and no paste. Measured before the change at 44% of
      the dialog. He asked for four things - full width, height that
      follows the content, richtext shown while markdown is stored,
      and image paste like the task card - and then expanded it: the
      new-comment textarea and the edit-comment editor have the same
      problems.
      Every one of those already worked somewhere else in the same
      file, so this is wiring rather than invention. Three helpers now
      sit beside el(): growBox, mdBarFor and pasteImageInto. All three
      editors grow through the first and build their bar from the
      second - including the comment composer, which mdBarFor was
      extracted FROM and which now calls it instead of keeping its own
      copy, removing about 660 characters of duplicated handler.
      pasteImageInto is on the long fields, where paste was asked for.
      growBox caps at 420px, which is the question answer box's
      ceiling rather than the tasklist input's unbounded growth. The
      card called them "the same handler"; there are two and they
      differ, and a description that grows without limit pushes the
      dialog's Save and Cancel off the screen. Past the ceiling the
      box scrolls instead of clipping.
      A long field is now DISPLAYED through renderMarkdown as well,
      which is the "show richtext" half - it had been showing raw
      asterisks while a comment beside it rendered formatted.
      renderMarkdown gained an optional class so a description does
      not inherit the comment body's styling; its existing caller
      passes nothing and is unaffected.
      A paste inside an open editor uploads quietly. mutate() gained
      a flag to skip its reload, because reloading re-renders the
      dialog and takes the open editor with it - the attachment would
      survive and the unsaved writing would not. Found by codex
      review, which also found that the test had hidden it by stubbing
      a response without ok:true, taking the no-reload failure path.
      Both fixed; the test now fails without the flag.
      Only .card-value--long and .card-edit--long become block-level
      flex. .card-edit itself stays inline-flex, so a one-line text
      field does not stretch across the card.

4.60  2026-08-28

    - TKT-585. Evidence is read now, not counted.
      Marking a checklist item or a required action done costs a
      --command/--proof pair. The gate checked that a pair was there
      and never looked inside it, so:

        d2 tira.checklist.update --ref REF --id CHK-NNN --status done \
          --command "" --proof ""

      exited 0 and marked the item done, at the CLI and in the engine
      alike. That was the cheapest way past any gate on this board -
      cheaper than doing the work, and cheaper than reusing another
      item's proof, which already costs a written reason since
      TKT-583.
      Measured before it was changed. Five shapes on a scratch board,
      all accepted: ('',''), ('x','   '), ('','real output'),
      ('prove -l t',''), and ('prove -l t','All tests successful').
      Only the last should have been.
      Either half empty or whitespace-only is now refused, and the
      refusal names the half that is missing rather than repeating the
      message for a missing pair - a caller who supplied one and is
      told to supply one has been sent back to what they already did.
      Both halves empty names both, so it is fixed once.
      Whitespace counts as empty: a space is not a smaller piece of
      evidence than none. The test is /\S/ rather than truthiness,
      so a proof of exactly '0' - a legitimate captured output - is
      content and is accepted. A !$proof test would have refused it,
      which is the bug this shape of guard usually ships with.
      Emptiness is checked; quality is not. A proof of 'x' passes,
      deliberately - whether evidence is any good is not mechanically
      decidable, and a gate that tried would refuse honest work.
      The check lives in _proof_entries_for, which already raised the
      pair requirement and which both commands call, so the CLI and
      the engine cannot disagree about the same pair. Marking an item
      to any status other than done still needs no pair at all.

4.59  2026-08-28

    - TKT-626. A code review no longer runs with the live checkout as
      its working directory.
      The verify column requires a code review on every card, which
      points a reviewing agent at a checkout that usually holds
      uncommitted work. On 2026-08-27 one of them ran, inside this
      repository:

        git diff -- README.md docs/POLICIES.md | head -120
        git checkout -- README.md docs/POLICIES.md && git status --short

      and reverted two paragraphs of documentation written while it was
      running. Taken from that session's own log. The loss is silent in
      the worst way: afterwards git status shows the files unmodified,
      which is indistinguishable from the edit never having been made.
      tools/review-worktree runs the review in a throwaway clone with
      the uncommitted work carried into it - tracked changes as a diff,
      the index reproduced separately so what is staged arrives staged,
      and untracked files copied, since a diff cannot carry a file git
      has never seen and a review of new code that omits the new files
      is the same failure in a different coat. The verify column's own
      wording now names the tool and the command, so the next agent
      does not rediscover this.
      A clone, not a linked worktree. The first version used
      'git worktree add', and the first review run through it found the
      hole and proved it rather than asserting it - running
      'git config --local review.escape changed' inside the throwaway
      and reading the value back out of the source repository. A linked
      worktree shares the source's git directory, so refs and config
      stay live, and 'git update-ref refs/heads/master HEAD~1' escapes
      the same way. The working tree was isolated; the repository was
      not. A local clone hardlinks its objects - 0.034s on this 70M
      repo - and has its own refs, config and index.
      Not a flat copy of the changed files either. That was tried on
      TKT-625 and is safe, but the reviewer reported it "can't run the
      supplied test directly from this reduced directory because it has
      no lib/Tira.pm" and went looking in sibling project directories
      for dependencies: a copy protects the tree by starving the
      reviewer.
      It refuses outright on submodules, whose uncommitted contents a
      superproject diff cannot carry, and on symlinks in the reviewed
      state pointing out of the tree, through which a write inside the
      clone would land outside it - rather than offering isolation it
      does not have.
      The read-only sandbox is not used because it would not start:
      "Codex's Linux sandbox uses bubblewrap and needs access to create
      user namespaces". That is a host kernel setting, and it is what
      led to running with full access in the first place.

4.58  2026-08-28

    - TKT-625. A flag whose whole meaning is DO NOT WRITE no longer
      writes.
      One global option spec parses every flag for every command, so
      --dry-run was accepted everywhere and read in exactly two
      places: bulk_import and replace_records. tira.import --dry-run
      previewed and wrote nothing, tira.replace --dry-run previewed
      and wrote nothing, and tira.ticket.create --dry-run CREATED THE
      CARD and printed it back as though nothing unusual had been
      asked.
      Measured at a cost rather than imagined. Probes were run to
      discover which options ticket.create accepts, all but one of
      them carrying --dry-run in the belief that it prevented a write.
      They left eight live junk cards on this board - TKT-617 through
      TKT-624 - every one discarded afterwards. The one probe that was
      refused was refused correctly: --affects-versions, with a
      did-you-mean pointing at --affects-version, which is TKT-298's
      unknown-option handling working and writing nothing.
      Every command but import and replace now refuses the flag,
      naming itself, saying the change is made rather than previewed,
      and pointing at the two that honour it.
      Written as an allow-list naming the two readers, not a list of
      the commands that swallowed it. A list of offenders can only
      cover the verbs somebody thought of, and what was owed was every
      verb that shares the fault - so tira.comment.add --dry-run is
      refused by the same line as tira.ticket.create --dry-run,
      without ever being named. That shape is available only because
      this flag has two readers; it is not the general per-command
      option catalogue %MISLEADING_OPTIONS declines to be, for the
      reason recorded there: deriving one would refuse things that
      work today.
      The position was a second fault inside the first, found by the
      fix failing. Added beside %MISLEADING_OPTIONS it changed
      nothing, because that check sits with the %method dispatch and
      record.* returns from the if/elsif chain some seven hundred lines
      earlier. The existing misleading-option guard had therefore
      never run for any create verb since it was written. Both tables
      now sit in the block every command passes through, which also
      gives assign.set's --assignee guard the reach it was believed to
      have.
      Only refusal was built. Honouring the flag - returning what
      would be created and writing nothing - is a larger change with
      its own tests, and is deliberately not here.

4.57  2026-08-28

    - TKT-598. A refusal now gives you the ids it tells you to use,
      and the same question can be asked without provoking one.
      The required-action refusal listed the blocking items by TEXT,
      joined with '; ' onto one line, and then handed back
      "d2 tira.required-action.update --ref REF --id REQ-NNN". REQ-NNN
      is a placeholder. The ids were in the list the whole time; the
      message mapped over the entries for their text and dropped them.
      So acting on a refusal meant running required-action.list,
      finding each item by matching its text by eye, and reading off
      the id - on a card with 75 items across a dozen columns. That
      cross-reference had already put proofs against the wrong ids
      twice on this board.
      Measured before it was changed: refusing a real card out of
      planning produced ONE line of over a thousand characters
      covering 12 items whose own texts contain semicolons, backticks
      and inline command examples.
      It now states the count, prints one item per line with its id
      beside its own text, and suggests a command carrying a real id
      from that list. Only what it says changed: the same moves are
      refused.
      The other half was that nothing could ASK. required-action.list
      returns every item on a card across every column, so the only
      way to learn what was in the way was to try a move and be
      refused - a strange shape for a system whose purpose is telling
      an agent what to do next. required-action.list --blocking
      answers what the current column still owes. A flag rather than
      a new verb because tira.card.required already exists and
      answers which FIELDS a card needs; a third similarly-named
      command would mislead. It is refused by name on any other
      command, which matters on a board carrying three open cards
      about options accepted and silently ignored.
      The refusal and the answer share one selection, so they cannot
      disagree - and that is a fact about the code rather than a
      comment, because the first version left the refusal with its
      own copy of the grep while the comment beside it claimed
      otherwise. Codex review caught that, along with a failed read
      being turned into an empty list - which made --blocking answer
      "nothing is blocking you" for a ref that does not exist, at
      exit 0, while the same command without the flag says the record
      was not found.
      Known and recorded rather than quietly met: the items are on
      their own lines in the message and NOT in what a reader sees,
      because every output format escapes the newlines. TKT-658
      covers that, for all three multi-line refusals rather than this
      one.

4.56  2026-08-27

    - TKT-591. A column can say what a card must ALREADY have done
      before it may be worked there, not only what it must finish
      before it leaves. The owner asked for it in TSK-170 - "we have
      exit required action items for each column. Do we have entry
      required action items?" - and Q-087 settled the design he
      wanted: the move IN is refused and the card stays exactly where
      it was, with the same --command/--proof, --repeated-reason and
      two-step confirmation the exit items already carry. Declared
      from the CLI with --entry-required-action and from the browser
      dashboard's column editor, which now shows an entry list beside
      the exit one.
      His own example is what the tests walk: between backlog and
      tests-red there is work belonging to neither column - "verify
      all details in the card" - and until now there was nowhere to
      put it.
      The gate populates the destination's entry items onto the card
      BEFORE refusing. An entry requirement is satisfied from outside
      the column that demands it, so a list that only appeared once
      the card was inside would leave nothing to mark and no way in.
      That was the stranding risk Q-087 asked about, and his example
      is what answers it.
      Forward moves only, like every other gate here: a card being
      sent back is not asked to qualify for where it is retreating
      to, because the unmet thing may be exactly what it is going
      back to fix (TKT-455).
      The evidence contract needed no new code. Entry items ARE
      required items, so marking one done costs a command and a proof,
      the duplicate-proof refusal applies, and a card exempted from an
      item (TKT-439) is let through - all by construction rather than
      by a second mechanism.
      The refusal stays on the CLI path, because a human dragging a
      card in the browser is not an agent skipping a gate (TKT-426).
      The BOOKKEEPING does not: a dragged card now arrives carrying
      the entry items that column asks for, rather than its exit items
      alone and a silence about the rest.
      Four things codex review caught, all fixed here. The population
      swallowed its own errors, so a column declared with an empty
      entry action let cards straight through a gate that believed it
      had nothing to enforce - the same swallowed-eval shape TKT-632
      is open about, written into new code while that card sat on the
      board. The browser move populated exit items and not entry ones.
      The column editor reused one CSS class for both lists' add
      buttons, which would have made existing Playwright locators
      ambiguous. And the browser round-trip was not asserted at all -
      the fixture now declares entry lists, edits them, empties one,
      and checks that an emptied list is sent as [] rather than
      omitted, which is the fault that once made a removal revert
      itself on the next load (TKT-481).

4.55  2026-08-27

    - TKT-575. --help names the arguments a command refuses without.
      _usage() answers from SKILLS.md's own usage catalogue and falls
      back to a bare "[options]" for any command absent from it, so
      six commands described themselves as taking nothing in
      particular: required-action.add, .list and .update, and
      question.ask, .answer and .mark - the ones an agent uses at
      every gate. checklist.update was worse: it listed three
      optional flags and omitted the two mandatory ones, so it read
      as exhaustive.
      Reported independently from another board with a cost: four
      checklist.update calls written from --help, output suppressed,
      and the author walked away believing four entries were ticked
      while the checklist read 0/9. Caught only by checklist-unmoved.
      The enforcement is unchanged and was never in question - it is
      TKT-453's proof requirement doing its job. What changed is that
      --help now says so. The pair is written as one bracketed unit
      rather than two independent optionals, because they are
      required together, and a test asserts that shape.
      The lasting half is general, and smaller than it first
      claimed. The guard originally read the %method dispatch table
      and asserted that every dispatchable command had a usage line.
      It passed, and it was wrong: %method is one of two dispatch
      routes, and reading both gives 135 commands of which 49 still
      print a bare "[options]" - police, next, policy.*, backup.*,
      project.*, question.attach and question.voice among them. A
      guard that reads one route and speaks for the whole surface is
      the same looks-exhaustive shape this entry is about, one level
      up. Caught by codex review before release.
      It now reads both routes, tests _usage() - what --help
      actually prints, so project.create's hard-coded line counts -
      and holds the 49 as a named ledger: a new command cannot join
      it silently, and one that gains a usage line cannot be left in
      it. The ledger shrinks without the guard being rewritten.
      Not one line of lib/ changed.

4.54  2026-08-27

    - TKT-597. A move guard can no longer be skipped by leaving
      --type off. column_list needs a concrete board type to know
      which columns exist; record_show and record_move resolve a card
      by ref alone. So a guard that asked for columns with the
      caller's own arguments got nothing and returned undef - which
      means "nothing to refuse". The gate did not fail closed, it
      failed silent and open.
      Reproduced on a copy of a real board before anything was
      written: a card walked from backlog to in-review through NINE
      gated columns with all 75 of its required actions pending and
      not one refusal. With --type supplied the same card was refused
      at the first gate, naming all twelve unmet items.
      The browser move provider had already solved this for its own
      bookkeeping, recovering the type from the record record_move
      returned, under the principle stated there: "a caller is never
      required to say what the engine can already tell for itself"
      (TKT-532). The CLI side never got it.
      Fixed as one shared helper rather than three patched branches.
      Four call sites use it: the three move guards - chain order,
      required actions, unjudged answers - and the post-move
      required-action bookkeeping. Only the browser move provider had
      the recovery already, doing it locally, and it keeps its own;
      consolidating the two is a separate change. One of the guards
      that lacked it was written earlier the same day, on TKT-584,
      repeating the fault while the card about it was already open. Three sites are deliberately left alone,
      where the type is inherent: both create paths, since
      create_record refuses without one, and column.list itself.
      Not reachable from tira.ticket/epic/sow.move, which carry a
      type from the dotted dispatch; reachable from code calling
      Tira::CLI directly.
      The recovered type is written back into the caller's own
      arguments rather than kept to the lookup. The chain guard ends
      its refusal by naming the move to make instead, and formats it
      from those arguments - so the first version of the fix closed
      the gate correctly and then told the caller to run
      "d2 tira..move", with an uninitialized warning beside it. It
      passed every assertion about the gate holding. It matters to
      one refusal in three; the required-action and unjudged-answer
      refusals name a command of their own and never print the type.
      Caught by codex review, reproduced as a failing test, then
      fixed.

4.53  2026-08-27

    - TKT-596. A card moved back to backlog resets the tasklist items
      that say somebody is working it. The board already understood
      that retreating undoes claims of progress - a backward move
      resets required items between destination and origin, keeping
      their proof (TKT-455) - but tasks were never part of it, so a
      card sat in the queue while its task list went on reading
      "working" until an agent ran a second command nobody prompts
      for. The owner asked for it to happen on the move.
      Anchored to backlog because it is the default builtin column: a
      fix point every board has, needing no per-board configuration.
      Three decisions, each deliberate and each asserted. A task
      already done is left alone - it records work that happened, and
      a card retreating does not unmake it. The reset crosses the
      session boundary, because tasklist items are session-scoped
      (TKT-537) and on a multi-agent board the tasks most needing
      reset belong to somebody else - a boundary-respecting reset
      would do nothing in exactly the case it exists for. And a task
      naming more than one card is NOT reset but IS named in the
      output: Q-088, where the owner chose visibility over a silent
      skip, because there is no status true about two cards at once
      and a silent skip is indistinguishable from the feature being
      broken.

4.52  2026-08-27

    - TKT-584. An answer that was read and acted on can no longer be
      left unjudged in silence. Reading is automatic - question_list
      stamps read_at on the way past, and lib/Tira.pm says so -
      while judging takes a deliberate tira.question.mark that
      nothing asked for until answer-unjudged fired hours later, by
      which time the card was finished. So the ordinary outcome was
      an answer read, understood, acted on, and never marked.
      A card now cannot move FORWARD out of the column an answer was
      given in while that answer carries no mark, and the refusal
      names the question and the command that settles it.
      The gate reads the question's own mark rather than raising a
      required-action item to stand in for it. That is the card's
      third acceptance criterion rather than a shortcut: an item is
      marked done with a command and a proof like any other, so it
      could be satisfied in the same sweep as everything else
      without anybody forming a view - an acknowledgement clicked
      through, which is the thing being prevented. There is nothing
      to satisfy but the judgement.
      Four things stay ungated on purpose, each asserted: an
      unanswered question, because waiting on the owner is its
      normal state; a discarded one, which nobody owes a judgement
      on; an answer marked not-ok, because the gate wants an
      assessment rather than agreement; and reading, because a check
      consulting read_at would release itself on the way past.
      Forward moves only. Written without that at first, it refused
      a card being sent BACK - contradicting TKT-455, whose design
      is that retreating is unconditional because the unmet thing
      may be what the card is going back to fix. An unjudged answer
      is a particularly good reason to retreat, since the person who
      would judge it may be why. Every assertion passed while that
      was wrong, because none of them moved a card backward; found
      by probing the new code against the board's existing design.
      answer-unjudged is unchanged and stays the backstop.

4.51  2026-08-27

    - TKT-572. --start-date and --due-date accept the basic-format offset
      Tira itself writes. Every timestamp Tira prints carries one -
      created_at and last_updated read 2026-08-19T09:00:00+0100 - and
      _valid_datetime required the extended form with a colon, so copying
      a timestamp out of a card and pasting it back was refused, with
      "must be an ISO 8601 date-time with timezone" about a value that
      already was one. The message sent the reader looking for a missing
      zone rather than a differently-punctuated one.
      lib/Tira.pm's other date-time validator, _epoch_of_datetime, had
      already worked this out - its comment reads "Accepts Z, +-HH:MM,
      and the +-HHMM the default clock writes" - so the two validators in
      one file disagreed about the same string, and this finishes the fix
      rather than inventing it. An offset of some kind is still required:
      widening what is accepted must not turn the check off. The refusal
      now shows the three accepted shapes instead of naming a standard
      the input already met.

4.50  2026-08-27

    - TKT-590. A task card's CARD-REF box no longer loses what you are
      typing to the poll. Reported by the owner: "For the task card, if
      i typed to type in a card-ref on it. It will wipe it out. But the
      card text edit is fine."
      The second half of that sentence was the clue. A guard already
      existed - reconcileTasklist keeps a row rather than rebuilding it
      - but it read the wrong thing. Text editing registers its intent
      in tlEditingIds the moment the editor opens, so it survived; the
      ref box was protected only by having a non-empty value. So the
      window between focusing the box and the first character landing
      was unguarded, and since the poll is setInterval(loadTasklist,
      1000) that window recurs every second: pause to read the ref you
      are about to type and the row is rebuilt under you.
      The busy test now reads INTENT as well as content: the ref box
      counts as busy when it holds a value OR when it is the focused
      element. Scoped to that box on purpose. Treating any focused
      input in the row as busy was written first and regressed the
      text editor - it stayed on screen forever, because focus was
      still inside the row it was trying to leave, and the browser
      suite caught it. Extracted into one named predicate, tlRowBusy,
      rather than added as another inline clause: TKT-554 fixed the
      half-typed case in this same conjunction and left this one open,
      and a growing inline conjunction is how that happened.
      The browser test that proves it went GREEN on its first run
      against the unfixed code - it reused a row that still had a file
      chosen, and that clause kept the row alive whatever the ref box
      did. Rewritten against an untouched row, it fails on the old code
      and passes on the new. Recorded because a test that passes for
      the wrong reason is the same defect as one proof for eleven
      items.

4.49  2026-08-27

    - TKT-573. A column that holds containers needs its own dwell
      reading, and docs/POLICIES.md now says so on the card-duration
      row. A SOW or an epic legitimately sits in a working column for
      as long as its children take, so a threshold set for work items
      reports it forever and cannot be settled by anything an agent
      does - the only settling move is finishing every child. Measured
      here rather than argued: in-progress holds the SOW and the epic
      and no tickets at all, median dwell 4h31m, p90 57h09m, max
      5d08h, against a policy of 8h. Two CRITICAL reminders had fired
      63 and 69 times over three days with no action available, which
      is worse than silence - a signal nobody can act on teaches the
      reader to skip the channel it arrives on. Re-thresholded from
      the p90 (POL-070, 58h); both settled on the first pass.
      Not exempted, only re-thresholded: the owner's own reading was
      "SOW and EPIC the waiting time is a bigger longer than ticket",
      and a genuinely stuck epic should still be caught. The other
      half of his answer - that a child should fold its work back into
      its parent - already exists as a document-column required action
      and is executed on every card, and could not settle this rule
      regardless: card-duration measures TIME IN A COLUMN, so updating
      a parent never settles it and only moving the card does. That
      distinction from card-still is now written on the row, since
      diagnosing it wrongly is what makes the threshold look broken.
      No code changes; the threshold lives on the board.

4.48  2026-08-27

    - TKT-583. One piece of evidence can no longer prove two different
      instructions. Marking a required action done is refused when
      another item in the same column already carries that exact command
      and proof - trimmed before comparison so a trailing newline cannot
      slip it through, and attachment-backed proofs compared by content
      hash. Reported by the owner from his own board: "the agent will use
      the same command and message to fill in and mark all done. Like
      TKT-555 Install column." Measured across the done column: 422 such
      reuses on 73 cards, including eleven items on one prove run
      answering instructions like "add the start date".
      The door is priced rather than locked - one suite run can honestly
      prove two items - but paying takes two calls, not one argument,
      because a gate that accepts any argument is the hole TKT-585
      records. --repeated-reason is refused as well, and answers by
      reading THIS item's own instruction back beside the reason just
      given, with a six-character code; repeating the call with
      --repeated-confirm CODE marks it done and stores the reason to be
      read back and judged later. The failure being caught is
      inattention rather than dishonesty - nobody re-read what item
      eight asked for - so being made to look at the instruction is the
      check, and signing is what makes looking unskippable.
      The code is bound to the claim it was issued for: stashed against
      card and item, invalidated and reissued if the command, proof or
      reason changes in between, and returned unchanged when the same
      claim is re-asked, so a typo is a retry and not a lockout. Five
      ways it could have been theatre were found and fixed while
      building it - the reason weighed before the duplicate scan ever
      ran, the reason stored on items that had duplicated nothing, a
      code issued but never persisted so the retry met a fresh one, a
      wrong code rotating the pending one, and a code redeemable
      against a claim it was not written for.
      The board shows it: an item done on a repeated pair is highlighted
      rather than merely ticked, and the proof modal opens with the
      reason above the evidence.
      The preventive half, from TSK-168: moving a card into a column
      that carries required actions prints how many arrived and to work
      them one at a time, each with its own command and proof. To
      STDERR, so it stays out of -o json, and only when the column
      brought outstanding items - a reminder that fires on every move is
      one nobody reads. The reuse happens at the end of a column's work,
      holding a list never read item by item, so the reminder belongs at
      the moment the list arrives.
      Also: --session's refusal on a command that does not scope by it
      gained the test it never had. The push gate found it by reading
      coverage percentages while a check of my own reported nothing
      uncovered - it had only ever looked at subroutines, never
      statements.

4.47  2026-08-27

    - TKT-552. tasklist.list --unlinked returns only the items with an
      empty refs array. TKT-547's task-unlinked rule already watches for
      those, but reports one only once it has aged past its grace, so an
      agent auditing them before the police nags had to fetch every item
      and filter refs client-side - the same shape TKT-545 found for
      --status, applied to linkage. It filters linkage only, not status:
      the rule watches pending and working items, but an audit that
      silently dropped done ones would answer a narrower question than
      the one asked. It composes with --status rather than replacing it,
      since the two read different fields, and omitting it returns every
      item exactly as before.

4.46  2026-08-27

    - TKT-580. tira.search --tasklist --session is accepted. Both
      docs/commands.md and SKILLS.md have described search's tasklist
      matching as scoped to the caller's own --session "exactly as
      tasklist.list is" since TKT-537, but the CLI refused the flag:
      --session was grouped with --collector, --agent and --heartbeat in
      the reminder-settings guard, and search was never on that guard's
      whitelist. The scoping worked - it just could not be addressed the
      documented way, only through TIRA_AGENT_SESSION - and typing the
      documented flag produced "Reminder settings belong to the
      project.update, project.new and onboard commands", naming three
      commands none of which was the one typed and never mentioning
      sessions or search. A session is a scoping argument rather than a
      reminder setting and now has its own guard; the reminder-settings
      guard keeps the three that really are. Separated rather than
      extended because that guard's own comment already recorded being
