      sub-verbs were refused --session by a two-level pattern.

4.45  2026-08-27

    - TKT-550. tira.search --tasklist --all-sessions searches every
      session's tasklist items instead of only the caller's own. TKT-539
      gave tasklist.list that opt-in so a supervising agent could check
      several subagents without already knowing each session id; search
      never got the equivalent, so the same supervisor could not find an
      item by text across sessions at all. Strictly opt-in, because the
      filter it crosses is TKT-537's deliberate privacy boundary rather
      than an oversight - a tasklist item's session privacy is not
      privacy if a search from another session can surface it - so
      without the flag nothing changes and t/396's existing cross-session
      assertions keep passing untouched. Every tasklist hit now carries a
      session field, flag or no flag, since a cross-session answer that
      cannot say whose item matched only reproduces the gap the flag was
      built to close.

4.44  2026-08-27

    - TKT-545. tasklist.list --status narrows the list to one status.
      next/shift/pop already filtered to pending internally for their own
      single-item use, and tasklist.update already accepted
      pending|working|done or 0|1|2, so the vocabulary existed and only
      list could not be asked - leaving "what is still on my plate" to be
      answered by fetching every item as JSON and filtering the status
      field by hand, which is the aggregation a list command is for. It
      goes through update's own parser rather than a second one, so the
      two cannot disagree about what a status is called, and an unknown
      value is refused rather than matching nothing: an empty list would
      read as "no such work" when it means "no such status". Filtering
      happens before sorting, so --sort orders what survived. Omitting
      --status is unchanged.

4.43  2026-08-27

    - TKT-568. t/344 now sees the methods the CLI reaches through its
      string dispatch table, and the 23 it found are documented. The
      guard exists to stop lib/Tira.pm's METHODS section going stale,
      and scoped itself to "exactly what Tira::CLI actually calls on
      $tira" - which it found by matching $tira->method( call sites. But
      a command name maps onto a method name as a string,
      'release.record' => 'release_record', and the method is then called
      through a variable, so no literal call site exists for the guard to
      see. Every such method sat outside the scope the guard believed it
      was enforcing: a fifth of the documented surface missing while the
      test passed, which is precisely the drift it was written to stop.
      Widening it turned up 23 rather than the 22 the ticket enumerated -
      required_item_list was the extra, undocumented while its own
      siblings required_item_add and required_item_update were not, so
      the family read as complete until each member was checked.

4.42  2026-08-27

    - TKT-564. The documentation no longer argues against its own feature
      before describing it. docs/commands.md and SKILLS.md each carried
      two adjacent paragraphs that disagreed: one said Starman's HUP
      "only recycles workers from the master's already-compiled code, so
      a graceful reload loads nothing new", and the next explained that
      police sends the master a HUP and each re-forked worker "re-reads
      the modules from disk and comes up on the installed version". The
      second is the true one - TKT-566's docker integration test proves
      it against a real board. The first was the residue of a belief held
      before TKT-565, and the correction had been written after it rather
      than in its place, so both survived. A reader who stopped at the
      first paragraph concluded the automatic pickup could not work. It
      is replaced by the limitation that is actually true and that the
      surrounding text needs: the master owns the listening socket and
      never serves a request, so nothing inside the board can act on a
      new version, which is exactly why police signals from outside.

4.41  2026-08-27

    - TKT-562. Onboarding no longer reports failure while leaving a whole
      project behind it. The dispatch shared by project.new and onboard
      ran project_new to completion - project, people, boards, every
      column written - and only then applied --mode, which refuses
      anything but single or chain. An invalid value therefore died at a
      point with nothing left to undo: a nonzero exit, an error, and a
      real project on disk that nothing mentioned. "It failed" and "it
      half worked" are different facts, and only one of them tells the
      reader to go and look at the directory; the next attempt then met a
      project that should not have been there. The check now runs before
      anything is written, names the option and both values it takes, and
      reads those values from onboarding_questions() rather than a
      hard-coded pair, so a third mode added there cannot be silently
      refused here. The interactive wizard was always safe - its own loop
      re-asks - so what this fixes is the --mode flag and the browser
      onboarding form, whose mode field renders its options as a hint and
      validates nothing.

4.40  2026-08-27

    - TKT-571. agent-still counts an unassigned card again. TKT-570's
      filter kept a card only when its assignee equalled the declared
      agent, which quietly dropped cards nobody had claimed as well as
      cards belonging to somebody else - so on any board that never sets
      an assignee, and assignee is optional, the rule had nothing left to
      report and went silent for good. A rule that silently never fires
      is worse than one that over-fires, because nothing announces the
      loss. It was also stricter than TKT-570's own acceptance criterion,
      which asked for cards "assigned to someone OTHER than the agent".
      Now a card is dropped only when it is assigned to a named person
      who is not the agent. Found by the standing bug hunt an hour after
      4.39 was committed and before it was pushed, so no board ever ran
      the silent rule.

4.39  2026-08-27

    - TKT-570. agent-still no longer reports an agent as stopped when the
      only cards in working columns belong to somebody else. Its waiting
      list was built from columns alone and never asked whose card it
      was, while the rule's own text claimed it "reports only when
      something is actually waiting on the agent" - so a card parked at
      in-review awaiting the owner counted as the agent stalling.
      Reported from zen-framework with measurement rather than
      description: 59 firings in a single session, every one true about
      elapsed time and wrong about what it implied. No existing escape
      reached it - the rule is whole-board so a --ref scope is refused,
      and the idle-queue exemption only helps when working columns are
      empty rather than holding another person's card - which left
      touching a card every 45 minutes to reset the clock, progress
      claimed by dragging rather than by working. A board that has
      declared no agent behaves exactly as before.

4.38  2026-08-27

    - TKT-569. release.record's batch mode now genuinely records each ref
      independently, which its own comment had promised while the code
      did the opposite: the first ref that could not be written threw
      straight out, leaving the refs before it recorded, the refs after
      it untouched, and the caller told only that one ref was wrong.
      Measured on a scratch board with [RT-001, RT-999, RT-002] - RT-001
      came back with gates=1 and fix_version=1.0 while RT-002 had
      nothing. These are the fields the push gate reads back, so a
      half-applied batch could let a push proceed for some cards while
      others silently lacked their evidence. Refusals are now collected
      and returned with their reason, a batch that records nothing at
      all still dies, and a single --ref answers exactly as it always
      did - including its error text, since "the shape it always had"
      has to cover the failures too.

4.37  2026-08-27

    - TKT-561. tira.release.record takes more than one --ref, recording
      the same gate, evidence and fix version on every card named. A
      whole batch ships in one push, and before this that meant running
      the command once per card with identical --gate/--result/
      --details/--fix-version and only --ref changing - measured on this
      project's own release of 2026-08-26, where twelve cards shipped
      together and the identical tail ran twelve times. Each ref is
      recorded independently, so one that cannot be written does not
      take the others down with it, and a single --ref answers in
      exactly the shape it always did.

4.36  2026-08-27

    - TKT-567. Police now finds the Starman master by parentage rather
      than by pid order. A pre-forked server's master and every one of
      its workers share the listening socket - all six processes on a
      live board held inode 35794984 - so the previous lookup returned
      the lowest pid, which is the master only because it happens to be
      created first. Once pids wrap past pid_max a worker can be
      numbered below its own master, and a container's pid_max is far
      smaller than a host's. The master is now the one no other holder
      of that socket fathered, read from /proc/PID/status; when that is
      not exactly one process the lookup refuses, because guessing
      wrong means signalling a worker - which would reload that worker
      onto the new code and leave the rest on the old, with the
      once-per-release mark written anyway: a board serving two
      versions at once, silently and permanently.

      tools/hup-integration was strengthened alongside it, having been
      too generous. It asserted that the worker set changed, which a
      real run satisfied while worker 2021 survived on old code -
      Starman recycles gracefully, one worker at a time, so sampling
      the moment the set first differs catches the board halfway. It
      now requires that no worker from before the signal remains.

4.35  2026-08-26

    - TKT-565. A live dashboard now picks up a newly installed Tira
      without anyone restarting it by hand. Police finds the process
      holding the board's port and sends it HUP once the code on disk
      has genuinely moved; Starman re-forks its workers, and because
      Tira serves a .psgi file path rather than an in-memory coderef,
      each new worker re-reads the modules from disk and comes up on
      the installed version - dropping nothing, since a worker finishes
      the request it holds first. The board could never do this itself:
      under a pre-forked server the process that notices a new version
      is a worker, and a worker cannot replace the board. The master is
      found by port rather than by a pidfile, which goes stale and can
      name a reused pid, and it is signalled once per release rather
      than once per pass. Every uncertainty refuses and names itself
      (no board on the port, an unreadable installed version, no known
      port, a version already signalled about), because a board on
      slightly old code still works.

      Asked for and then corrected by Michael: the first design here
      stopped the master and launched a replacement, until he asked
      "After master process killed. The children still survived. Have
      you think of this side effect too?" and pointed at Starman's own
      documentation. Testing it settled it - a two-worker Starman on a
      .psgi reading a version from a file served "one", the file
      changed, the master got HUP, and it served "two" from fresh
      worker pids. HUP has none of kill-and-relaunch's failure modes.

    - TKT-566. And police now confirms that the process holding the
      board port is a Starman before signalling it, refusing with
      "not-a-board" when the command line says otherwise or cannot be
      read at all. What it checks is Starman rather than "provably this
      board", for two reasons found on a real running board: Starman
      rewrites $0, so a live master's command line reads "starman
      master" and names neither dashboard.psgi nor the command that
      started it - a first version of this check looked for
      dashboard.psgi and would have refused every genuine board while
      looking perfectly safe - and assigning $0 on Linux clobbers the
      environ region, so there is no marker left there either. A
      Starman is enough, because this guard exists to stop the one
      destructive case: every Starman handles HUP, so a misidentified
      one merely reloads, while a process without a handler dies. SIGHUP's default
      disposition is Term, so signalling a stranger that installs no
      handler kills it - proved with a plain perl -e 'sleep 300', which
      died on HUP. The board port is a stable configured number, so any
      time the board is down and another program has taken it, this
      would have been a real process killed by a supervisor that only
      meant to reload a dashboard. Found reviewing TKT-565's own code
      before it shipped: the pidfile design it replaced had reasoned
      about this same hazard, and the rewrite kept the risk while
      dropping the guard.

