      deriving a plausible-looking name for an entrypoint the
      dispatcher could never actually find. This is the guard that
      should have caught TKT-510 before it shipped.

3.93  2026-08-25

    - TKT-509. Fixed: tasklist.update --status 0 (the
      numeric form) stored status as the JSON string "0" rather than
      a real int, unlike every other write path - a CLI argument is
      always a string, and nothing had forced it into numeric context
      before storing it. Found adversarially; Test::More's is()
      could not have caught it, since "0" and 0 compare equal after
      decode - only the raw JSON text shows the difference. Now
      forced to a real number.

3.92  2026-08-25

    - TKT-508. A further screenshot from Michael on top of TKT-504
      through TKT-507: prune, status as an enum, a default display
      sort, multi-file attachments, and per-item attach/ref sub-verbs.

    - New: tasklist.prune [--session ID] deletes every item with
      status done, scoped the same way list/add already are.

    - Changed: task-list status is now a stored integer (0 pending, 1
      working, 2 done) rather than a word - a breaking storage change,
      confirmed live (Q-077). tasklist.update --status still accepts
      the word for convenience; every command now returns the number.
      A tasklist.json written before this shipped still reads
      correctly (its old word is recognised transparently) and is
      upgraded to the int form the next time that item is written -
      no separate migration step.

    - New: tasklist.list --sort FIELD:DIR[,FIELD:DIR...], defaulting
      to last_updated:desc,status:asc when omitted. This is a purely
      display ordering, independent of the order field next/shift/pop
      use for actual queue position.

    - New: tasklist.add --attach FILE (repeatable) attaches files at
      creation time, content-addressed the same way record
      attachments already are (the store is shared).

    - New: tasklist.task.attach.add/discard --id ID --file FILE... and
      tasklist.task.ref.link/unlink --id ID --ref REF... manage an
      existing item's attachments and refs after it already exists.

    - Fixed: a pre-existing guard (TKT-338/389) silently collapsed
      --file down to one value for every command except
      attachment.add, so passing two --file to
      tasklist.task.attach.add/discard silently dropped the second
      file rather than refusing or accepting it. Found adversarially,
      by exercising the CLI option-parsing layer directly rather than
      only the engine method (which never saw the bug). Both new
      commands are now exempt from that guard alongside
      attachment.add.

3.91  2026-08-25

    - TKT-507. Follow-up to TKT-504/505/506: "treat the tasklist like
      an array list - next, pop, shift, unshift, slice to insert item
      in between, etc... And push, update, remove etc..." (Michael,
      live). push was already tasklist.add, update already existed.

    - New: tasklist.next (peek the front, no removal), tasklist.shift
      (FIFO pop, returns and removes the front), tasklist.pop (LIFO,
      returns and removes the back), tasklist.unshift (insert at the
      very front), tasklist.slice (insert at an arbitrary --position),
      and tasklist.remove (delete an item entirely, distinct from
      marking it done). Every task item now carries an explicit order
      field, independent of created_at, so the queue can be reordered
      without timestamp games.

    - New: tasklist.import --ref TKT-XXX copies a card's still-
      pending required-actions and checklist entries into linked
      tasklist items, one per entry, idempotently - a follow-up
      request from Michael, live: "you can add the required actions
      items or checklist items to the tasklist, so you can focus on a
      task at a time."

    - Fixed: an id could be reused after the item that held it was
      fully removed (via shift/pop/remove) and the list emptied -
      unlike ticket/epic/sow, whose ids never free up because nothing
      is ever truly deleted there. Found by deliberately draining a
      session's queue and adding again. A small counter file next to
      the list, which only ever goes up, now survives every item
      being deleted.

    - Fixed: tasklist.slice --position with a negative value crashed
      with a raw Perl splice error ("Modification of non-creatable
      array value attempted") instead of refusing cleanly. Found by
      deliberately feeding it an out-of-range negative position.
      Negative positions are now refused, naming why.

    - Fixed: --session was reserved for the project.update/new/
      onboard reminder-settings flags only, so tira.tasklist.*
      --session died with "Reminder settings belong to..." even
      though it is a genuinely different flag on a different feature.
      tasklist.* commands are now exempt from that guard.

    - TKT-506. "Task prefix use TSK" (Michael, live) - tasklist_add
      minted ids as TASK-NNN, breaking the short 3-letter prefix
      convention every other record type (SOW/EPC/TKT) uses.

    - Fixed: task-list items are now created as TSK-NNN.

3.90  2026-08-24

    - TKT-505. Follow-up to TKT-504: typing --session on every
      tasklist call is unnecessarily long once genuinely multi-agent.
      Michael, live: an environment variable should stand in when
      --session is not given.

    - New: tasklist.add/tasklist.list fall back to
      TIRA_AGENT_SESSION from the environment when --session is not
      passed explicitly; an explicit --session still overrides it.
      tasklist.update never consulted session in the first place - a
      core agent can already manage any item by id regardless of
      session - so it needed no change.

    - TKT-504. "Since TaskList not working, Create this simple task
      list feature to Tira, d2 tira.tasklist.add." Requested as a
      parallel system to ticket/epic/sow, deliberately lighter: free
      text, three fixed states (pending/working/done), no gates or
      checklists.

    - New: tira.tasklist.add --text TEXT [--session ID] [--ref REF...]
      creates a pending item; tira.tasklist.list [--session ID] shows
      items scoped to that session (or the single shared list, with no
      session named); tira.tasklist.update --id ID --status
      pending|working|done moves an item between the three states. Two
      distinct sessions never see each other's items - a subagent
      declaring its own session id gets its own private list, and an
      orchestrating agent can read a subagent's list by naming its
      session. An item may optionally link to existing tickets, epics,
      or sows via --ref. Attachment support was considered and
      deferred - Tira's attachment subsystem is card-scoped and
      content-addressed, and reusing it here would be substantially
      more machinery than the core ask needs.

    - TKT-411. Reporter wanted an intake/catalog column exempt from
      card-unassigned without mischaracterizing it (--protected isn't
      settable on a project's own column, --terminal is semantically
      wrong for an inbox). Investigation found the answer already
      existed and was simply not documented on this rule: --no-watch,
      which card-still and card-changed-by-owner already document.
      Verified live with no code change: 1 violation before --no-watch,
      0 after.

    - Fixed: card-unassigned's own docs/POLICIES.md entry now names
      --no-watch, matching its sibling rules.

    - TKT-502. "Can you make each of them more easy to understand and
      read" - the declared-policy row packed id, rule, action, and
      every parameter into one dense "POL-002 · rule -> action
      (param=val, param=val)" line. Considered and rejected one line
      per parameter (a self-critique pass, standing in for a second
      opinion no other agent on this single-agent board could give) -
      it would roughly triple the list's scroll length on a 60+-policy
      board.

    - New: a declared row now shows a bold id/rule/action header, then
      one secondary line listing each parameter as a labeled "name:
      value" pair rather than a bare key=value packed into a
      parenthetical - readable without losing the density a large
      board needs.

    - TKT-493. Requested directly: "create a new modal on the html
      dashboard, the user can view and edit and add the policies not
      just column policies." The Columns dialog only ever covered a
      column's own required-action template; the board-wide police
      policy engine (36 rules - card-duration, wip-limit,
      conversation-not-folded, and the rest) was CLI-only, reachable
      through tira.policy.add/list/remove/decline and nowhere in the
      browser.

    - New: a Policies button next to Columns opens a modal listing
      every declared, declined, and undeclared rule (the same
      three-way split tira.policy.review already returns), with a
      form to declare a new policy, edit or remove a declared one, or
      decline a rule with a reason - covering every rule-specific
      parameter tira.policy.add accepts (enter, before, column, age,
      read_age, max, pattern, message, require, sandbox, require_link,
      link_to) plus the type/on_column/ref scope narrowing, so nothing
      typeable on the CLI is unreachable from the dashboard.

    - New engine method policy_rule_specs exposes the same
      needs/forbids data policy_add already validates against, so the
      form's rule picker shows or hides the right fields per rule
      without duplicating that knowledge and risking drift.

    - New browser_providers closures (policies, policy_add,
      policy_remove, policy_decline) and matching DashboardWeb.pm
      routes (GET /policies, POST /policy/add, /policy/remove,
      /policy/decline), following the same provider/route pattern the
      Columns dialog already uses for /columns and /columns/apply.

    - Editing a declared policy removes and re-declares it under the
      hood, since policy_add itself refuses to update a declaration
      in place (TKT-339's own rule against silently replacing a
      stricter policy with a looser one) - the modal keeps that
      guarantee rather than working around it.

    - TKT-494. "Add a UI to allow to select which column is new
      ticket entry point. It can be multiple entries" (owner, live).
      The Columns dialog gains an Entry checkbox per column row,
      building on the multi-entry engine support TKT-496 shipped
      alongside it. Saving sends the whole checked set through
      column_roles_set in one call - full-replace semantics matching
      repeating --role entry=X on the command line, not an add-only
      merge, so unchecking a column and saving removes it rather than
      only ever growing the set. A save that never touches the
      checkboxes sends no entry field and leaves the declared entry
      columns untouched.

    - TKT-284. column-skipped's only remedy on finished work used to
      be walking the card backward and forward through the column it
      missed, writing moves into its history that never happened -
      the same falsified-history shape 2.35 already fixed for
      orphan-card, unfixed here. Reported at 4x the count: 344
      outstanding findings against 439 done cards on one board,
      mostly work that finished before the rule existed. Any comment
      on the card now settles the finding, reusing
      discard-unexplained's own settle check rather than inventing a
      second convention - the comment is the acknowledgement, and
      nothing is rewritten. A card with the same shortcut and no
      comment is still reported, exactly as before.

    - TKT-292. tira.usage documented no allowed values for
      --sdlc-gate, and gate.add's --gate had none either - both took
      free text, so no typo was ever refused and gate-missing could
      neither be satisfied nor caught being satisfied falsely.

    - New: tira.project.gates declares a project's own gate
      vocabulary (--gate-name, repeatable), opt-in the same way
      column_roles already is - unrestricted, exactly today's
      behaviour, until a project declares one. Once declared, --gate
      and --sdlc-gate are both validated against that one list,
      refusing a name outside it and naming the declared vocabulary.
      record_update's sdlc_gate handling, previously bundled into a
      generic loop that applied zero validation, is carved out into
      its own validated assignment; create_record gets the same check
      at creation time.

    - TKT-303. "Both halves are correct about the DECLARED repo and
      wrong about the work" - card-sandbox-missing assumes one project
      maps to one repository, and a card whose work legitimately lives
      in a second one had no honest way to clear the finding but
      declining the whole rule (losing the check for every other
      card) or a permanent violation nobody could act on.

    - New: tira.policy.decline takes an optional --ref CARD, scoping
      the decision to one card rather than the whole board - a
      different question than board-wide decline ("should this rule
      exist"), so it does not conflict with the rule staying declared
      everywhere else. Requires a reason exactly like board-wide
      decline, reads back through tira.policy.declined --ref CARD, and
      is reusable by any rule, not scoped to card-sandbox-missing.

    - TKT-332. "Four epic agents were spawned... All four cards had
      agent_session: null... ALL FOUR failed with 'No agent named ...
      is reachable'." None of the 35 existing policy rules asked
      whether a card being worked had an agent_session recorded, even
      though record_update updates 'sandbox agent_session' in the same
      qw() list, one word apart, and card-sandbox-missing exists for
      the first.

    - New policy rule: card-agentless (--enter COLUMN), same shape as
      card-sandbox-missing, reporting a card in the working column
      with no agent_session recorded. A different question from
      card-unassigned - assignee says who owns the work, agent_session
      says whether the worker can be reached again - so a card can be
      correctly assigned while permanently orphaned from its agent,
      and the two rules compose rather than merge. Settles the moment
      the handle is written down.

    - TKT-357. "ZEPG-6 has a checklist of ELEVEN ITEMS, ALL DONE.
      checklist-idle fired on it at 3h and escalated to CRITICAL.
