      session's worth of ticket.update/required-action.update/checklist.
      update/release.record calls landed anonymous, because --author was
      easy to forget on those four command families.

    - Fixed: all of the above now refuse the same way record_move already
      did, naming --author. Also found and fixed along the way: the
      browser dashboard's update, comment_update, checklist_add,
      checklist_update, and required_action_update providers never
      forwarded the signed-in person to the engine at all - a real
      production gap, since editing a comment, checklist item, or
      required action from the dashboard would have failed outright once
      the engine-level refusal shipped. Each provider now passes a
      deliberately client-sent author through, falling back to the
      signed-in session - except comments, where the session always wins
      (TKT-458).

3.25  2026-08-22

    - TKT-467. A done required-action item's command/proof pairs expanded
      inline (TKT-462), which could not cope with a long command on a
      narrow screen: it squeezed the proof text into a sliver that wrapped
      one character per line, unreadable. Measured live on TKT-432's own
      dialog on a phone. His words: "I can't even read anything out of
      it."

    - Fixed: a done item's proof now opens in a popup instead, wrapping at
      word boundaries and staying within the viewport at any width. The
      inline row is retained in the DOM for identification but never
      shown.

3.24  2026-08-22

    - TKT-432. Giving an orphaned card a home is one conceptual moment -
      link it, and usually give it a priority and an assignee in the same
      breath, since an untriaged card is not yet real work - but
      hierarchy.link only ever set parent/child, so it always cost a
      second round trip. Measured four times in one session this hour.

    - Fixed: hierarchy.link accepts optional --priority/--assignee,
      applied to the child in the same write as the link. Omitting both
      leaves the command exactly as before. An invalid priority or
      unknown assignee refuses the whole call, the link included, rather
      than linking and silently dropping the bad value.

3.23  2026-08-22

    - TKT-400. tira.ticket.show truncates description/problem_or_feature/
      solution_needed at 2000 characters by default, honestly marked
      truncated - but update accepted a truncated read back as though it
      were the whole field, so a read-modify-write silently destroyed
      everything past the cut. Measured on TKT-386: three edits shrank it
      from 4541 to 3187 bytes, invisible because history.list truncates
      the same way.

    - Fixed: record_update now refuses a write whose value exactly matches
      a truncated read of the field's current stored value, naming --full.
      A genuinely shorter rewrite, written on purpose, is unaffected - this
      is an exact-match check against a truncated read, not a length limit.

3.22  2026-08-22

    - TKT-464. Growing a column's required_actions template never reached a
      card that had already passed through that column before the template
      existed, if the card later moved backward into it: only a forward
      move-in populated a column's template onto a card, so the backward
      branch's reset-only behavior left the card with nothing for a column
      it had genuinely never picked up. Found live on TKT-458.

    - Fixed: _apply_column_required_actions's populate logic is now shared
      between both branches (extracted into
      _populate_column_required_actions) - a backward move-in populates a
      missing template exactly as a forward one already did, in addition to
      its existing reset-to-pending behavior for items already on the card.
      Also documented: `tira.required-action.add --column SLUG` already
      existed as a way to backfill an item without moving the card, just
      wasn't written down.

