2.75  2026-08-19

    - TKT-397. The pre-push browser gate leaked a Starman dashboard server on
      every run, not only when interrupted. 34 had accumulated by the time
      they blocked a push - every port from 7841 to 7941 held, no symptom
      until the range was full and then every release blocked at once.

    - Root cause: tools/browser-tests started each server through a bash
      function, backgrounded - `tira dashboard -o "browser=..." & serving=$!`.
      $! after backgrounding a FUNCTION call names the subshell running the
      function body, not the perl process it forks inside itself. cleanup()
      correctly killed that subshell; the actual Starman master and workers
      it had started were never signalled at all, and kept running as
      orphans holding their port. Confirmed with a direct, minimal
      reproduction before touching the real script.

    - Fixed by invoking perl directly for all three backgrounded server
      launches (the gate board, the browser board, the priority board),
      bypassing the function wrapper so $! names the real process. Nothing
      else changed: one-shot table renders still go through the function,
      since they are never backgrounded and were never the leak.

    - Found and cleared 199 real leaked Starman processes from this
      project's own working directory while investigating - confirmed by
      cwd before touching anything; four processes belonging to other
      projects sharing the port range were correctly left alone. Verified
      the fix for real: ran the actual browser-tests script end to end (11
      browser tests, all passed) and confirmed no new listeners survived it.

