2.70  2026-08-18

    - TKT-386. A map nested inside a list element is printed inside it, instead
      of flattened into the map around it.

    - Data::TOON's list branch renders each of an item's keys as "$key: $value"
      with the value already encoded, which assumes a value is one line. A map
      is many, so its first key landed on its own key's line and the rest at
      the outer map's indent - two 'author' keys at one level, with 'mark'
      reading as a field of the question rather than of its answer.

    - What it cost, measured on a real reader. A project filed two bug reports
      saying tira.question.mark stores nothing, citing 0 marks across 89
      questions. The mark was stored the whole time, at answer.mark. They read
      the flattened output, applied one bad predicate 89 times, and got a
      confident wrong number; they retracted it themselves after recounting 86
      of 89 marked. TOON is the default output and its readers are agents, so
      an output that makes the correct reading unreachable is a defect here,
      not in the reader.

    - Unlike the _encode_primitive patch beside it, this one cannot be pure
      delegation - there is nothing to delegate to when the branch itself is
      wrong. So it delegates every shape the module renders correctly (the
      tabular form, lists of primitives, lists of flat maps, arrays inside
      items) and reimplements only the case where an item carries a map.

    - The predicate was narrowed twice while being written, before this
      release: widening it to any ref turned the compact "tags[2]: a,b" into a
      dash list nobody asked for, and the first version re-indented nested
      lines flat, which read correctly for one level and destroyed everything
      below it. Both found by testing shapes the fix was not aimed at.

    - Found a third time, while finishing the test this release: the break-it
      proof's fixture, a single-key nested map, never reproduces the
      collision it exists to prove - a lone key sits inline with its parent
      and never lands on a line of its own, so it cannot collide with a
      sibling at the same indent. Switched to the original three-key shape,
      where the second and third keys reliably land where the collision is
      real.

    - Round-tripping is NOT fixed and is no longer claimed: Data::TOON's
      decoder has the matching gap and cannot read this shape even written
      correctly. Raised as TKT-393. Nothing inside Tira is affected - it
      decodes TOON in one test only, and cards are stored as YAML.

    - Why it survived: across 271 test files, none asserted what TOON looks
      like, and all 17 that call format_output name another mode. The format
      everyone reads was the format nothing checked. t/272 is the first test
      of it, at 20 assertions.

    - No docs/commands.md section for this - the earlier _encode_primitive fix
      (2.20 -> 2.2) was never given one either, because commands.md is a
      per-command reference and this is internal rendering behaviour. This
      entry and the code comment beside the override are the documentation,
      matching that precedent.

    - Full suite 272 files / 6315 tests, 100% statement and subroutine on
      Tira.pm, CLI.pm and DashboardWeb.pm.

2.69  2026-08-18

    - TKT-401. discard-with-open-questions covers every ending column, not
      only discard.

    - A card can reach done - or any board's other terminal column - carrying
      a question nobody answered, and nothing said a word. Caught live:
      TKT-349 reached done with Q-047 still open, police said nothing through
      a full pass on a board with thirty policies declared, and he asked:
      "and TKT-349 is done. why open question in a done card for?" and then:
      "crazy."

    - The done case is the worse one. A discarded card's open question is
      usually moot - the work is not happening. A done card's question was
      load-bearing on work that shipped: Q-047 asked which columns should
      notify him, and a different answer would have shipped the wrong
      configuration with the very question that would have caught it sitting
      closed and unread.

    - Endings are read from the board's own declaration - _ending_columns,
      per the record's own type, cached across a mixed sow/epic/ticket pass
      rather than fetched once per policy - so a board naming its ending
      column something other than done is covered without the rule naming it.
      discard is kept as an explicit literal alongside the declared endings,
      because it is not marked terminal on a column by default and dropping
      the literal would have silently stopped covering it - the regression
      the acceptance criteria explicitly forbid.

    - t/277 at 7 assertions, proved by reverting to the literal discard check
      - 3 of them fail. Full suite 271 files / 6293 tests, 100% statement and
      subroutine on Tira.pm, CLI.pm and DashboardWeb.pm.

    - Also this hour: a bridge report claiming card-still has no way to
      exempt a resting column was measured and found false - --no-watch on a
      column, declared before any violation is raised, already does this.
      The report's deeper claim held up: --no-watch is not respected
      uniformly by every rule that should read it, which TKT-331 already has
      independent evidence for. Corroborated there rather than duplicated.

