2.66  2026-08-18

    - TKT-338. tira.attachment.add takes repeated --file, so a batch pays one
      command resolution instead of one per file.

    - Reported measuring six PNGs at about 20s each regardless of size, guessed
      as SHA cost. Measured on 2.64: it is not - a 61KB and a 217KB file cost the
      same, a 400-card board costs the same as a 1-card one, and an UNKNOWN tira
      command that does no work at all costs 0.50s to fail. Loading the module is
      0.07s. So resolving a command, not doing its work, is nearly the entire
      cost, and six files paid it six times: 3.87s measured, about 3.0s of it at
      the door.

    - The mechanism already existed on a different command and had all along:
      comment.add --attach is repeatable and loops inside one invocation,
      delegating to the same attachment_add the new path uses. Proved by running
      it before writing anything: three files, one invocation, 0.70s. There was
      no design question to answer, only a command surface that never offered
      what the engine already did.

    - --file is shared by nine commands, so it is parsed as a list once and
      collapsed to a single value everywhere except attachment.add, rather than
      handing eight callers an arrayref and fixing each with ->[0] - which is how
      two copies of one decision drift. Giving one of the other eight commands
      --file twice is now refused, naming the flag, instead of silently keeping
      the last value with exit 0 - one of the single-valued flags TKT-389 counts,
      closed here because this card had to touch the option anyway.

    - A file that cannot be read mid-batch still fails the command, and the
      files attached before it are named in the error rather than lost with it.
      Caught by testing the failure path this card exists because of, not only
      the happy one: the first version of the batch loop was a plain map, and a
      bad path partway through died with no record at all of what had already
      landed - reproducing, in a new shape, the exact blindness the reporter
      asked to be fixed. Proved on a standalone probe before it reached the
      suite: three files, one missing in the middle, the command failed and the
      error named the one that had already attached.

    - t/275 at 18 assertions. The one that matters most is that a batch is ONE
      invocation - a loop calling the command six times would satisfy any test
      that only checked the files arrived, and would fix nothing the reporter
      hit. Proved by breaking it: restoring last-wins fails 7 assertions, and
      restoring the plain map fails the one assertion that distinguishes it from
      the fix - the file already lands on disk either way, so only the report of
      it changes.

    - Full suite 268 files / 6261 tests, 100% statement and subroutine on
      Tira.pm, CLI.pm and DashboardWeb.pm. Predicted before the first gate ran
      that nothing but t/275 would move, since six existing test files exercise
      only the single-file path and a batch could not exist to be asserted
      before this change - the suite confirmed it.

