2.64  2026-08-18

    - TKT-388. Five commands stopped handing back every account's stored
      password: tira.project.show and the four tira.project.people.* verbs. What
      they emitted was the algorithm, its work factor, the per-account salt and
      the digest - the whole of what an offline attempt needs - from routine
      unprivileged reads that land in agent transcripts, in logs, and in whatever
      gets pasted when somebody asks for help with a board.

    - One cause: project_show returned the parsed project file as it stood, and
      person_list, person_update, person_activate and person_deactivate are all
      built on that read or return the record they just wrote.

    - So the fix is one accessor rather than five deletes. _person_for_return
      decides once what of a person is safe to hand back; the first attempt
      deleted the field in project_show alone, which covered two surfaces and
      left three, and the next person_* method added would have made it four
      silently.

    - A copy, not a delete. Three of the five return a reference into the
      structure they have just written to disk, so deleting there would take the
      password out of the STORE rather than out of the ANSWER - a far worse bug
      than the one being fixed.

    - Nothing authenticates through any of them: login_verify reads the person
      with _login_person, which goes to _project_data directly. Verified before
      the cut rather than after, and t/271 proves the login still works.

    - Found by enumerating for TKT-386 - sweeping read commands for a map nested
      inside a list element, because that is the shape the TOON encoder mangles.
      people[].password is that shape.

    - t/73 changed its instrument, not its assertions. It inspected the stored
      password block through person_list, which is a public read: the leak,
      borrowed as a measuring tool. It now reads the store directly and every
      assertion is unchanged.

    - The reason this survived is that the suite asserted what commands RETURN
      and never what they REVEAL. t/271 asserts the absence across all five
      surfaces, driven off the list rather than the pair, and proves the detector
      can fail - with the guard removed, all five leak.

2.63  2026-08-18

    - TKT-384. A role declared by mistake can be taken back.

    - Found by making the mistake. Probing what tira.column.roles accepts, I ran
      '--role nonsense=backlog' against the live board. It was accepted and
      stored, and nothing removed it: --role merges what it is given into what is
      there, nothing deletes, and an empty value is refused as malformed rather
      than read as a removal. Undoing one command meant editing .tira by hand.

    - His answer: "also a card to allow agent to remove junk rules?" - and then
      the half that makes it safe to have: "if they do. they need to provide a
      reason for it and there will be a column logs to log that reason. who and
      why."

    - So --remove-role deletes, --reason is required, and every removal is
      appended to a log the board carries with who, why, when, and which column
      the role pointed at - readable after the role itself is gone. A reason
      passed beside --role is refused rather than stored where nobody would find
      it, since accepted-and-ignored is the failure it exists to prevent.

    - A role a policy names cannot be removed out from under it, and the refusal
      says which policy. A rule whose role stopped existing matches nothing at
      all, silently, while somebody believes it is still protecting them - the
      same failure the declaration guard already refuses in the other direction.

    - Not added, though it was written as an acceptance criterion first: a
      refusal for a role no rule reads. Any name can be read, because a policy
      names one through --enter-role, --before-role or --column-role, and the
      natural order is to declare the vocabulary and then the policies using it.
      Refusing an unread name would refuse the first half of every correct
      sequence.

    - TKT-376, TKT-381 and TKT-320. The card-changed-by-owner rule now refuses to
      be declared on a board that has not said which agent works it, since it
      settles by asking whether a change was the agent's own and cannot answer
      that unasked - and it stops firing on ending columns, where a card sitting
      finished is the point rather than a change nobody accounted for.

