2.61  2026-08-18

    - TKT-306 and TKT-360, worked as one fix because they are one cause. An
      option a command will not act on is refused rather than accepted, dropped
      and answered with the whole card - which reads as confirmation because the
      card is right there.

    - TKT-281 fixed --sdlc-gate on move and TKT-302 fixed --comment on discard,
      each by adding one name to a hand-kept table. That table covered exactly
      the options somebody had already been bitten by, and measured afterwards
      the surface behind it was: 24 of the 25 fields record_update writes were
      dropped by move, 6 of 6 probed were dropped by discard and restore, and
      all eight --set- replacements were dropped by create.

    - So the refusal is derived rather than listed. record_update now iterates a
      declared list of the fields it writes, and the command line refuses those
      same fields on the commands that write none of them. A field added to
      record_update is covered on the day it is added, which is the property a
      denylist extended one incident at a time cannot have.

    - Two boundaries were measured rather than assumed, and both would have made
      a plausible fix wrong. tira.<type>.create reads all eighteen append fields
      and loses none, so "only an update writes fields" is false and only the
      replacements are refused there. And --title on clone is genuinely read; a
      first probe reported it refused, which was a refusal for a MISSING --title
      read as a refusal of the option beside it.

    - The first version of the guard was too wide and the suite caught it in 112
      seconds: applied to every command it refused the browser dashboard,
      because --title is a card field on create and update and a display flag on
      tira.dashboard. A guard wide enough to catch every drop is also wide
      enough to refuse commands that read the same word for something else. It
      is scoped to record.move, .discard, .restore and .clone, plus .create for
      the replacements, and no wider.

2.60  2026-08-18

    - TKT-363, asked for directly: on the browser dashboard a card worked
      recently is tinted green and the tint fades as it ages, so progress on a
      column is visible without reading a timestamp. He asked for it after
      looking at the board and asking whether the agent had stopped, while two
      cards were being actively gated - a card being worked and a card abandoned
      for six hours looked identical.

    - Recent compared to what was the one thing worth asking rather than
      guessing, and he answered with a comparator rather than a threshold:
      -M $b.json <=> -M $a.json. So the ranking is relative to the other cards
      in the same column and read from the file's modification time, which was
      already on every card as data-mtime and is already what the default sort
      orders by. Nothing new is stored and nothing new is fetched.

    - The tint is proportional to age rather than to position. -M is a time, so
      two cards a minute apart should not look as far apart as two cards a week
      apart merely because they sit next to each other in the list. The newest
      card in a column is fully tinted and the oldest carries none either way.

    - Three cases are deliberately not painted, and the second is what his
      break-it case asserts. A column with fewer than two visible cards is not a
      ranking. A column whose cards all share one timestamp is not a ranking
      either - there is no difference to show, and inventing one would say
      something the board does not know. And a card waiting on an unanswered
      question keeps its own yellow and takes no green: his exception, and the
      one that must not be traded for the effect, because a card waiting on
      somebody is not a card that has gone stale.

    - Recomputed on every refresh, from renderColumns, where the visible cards
      of one column are already gathered - so it cannot go stale on a board that
      reloads every second, and a treatment that survived only the first paint
      would be exactly the defect shape this project keeps finding.

    - --fresh defaults to 0, so a card that is not ranked is exactly the colour
      it was before this release, and card--waiting and card--to-review both set
      their own background, which wins - the two existing treatments are
      preserved by construction rather than by remembering to preserve them.

2.59  2026-08-18

    - TKT-359: a new rule, agent-still, for the stoppage board-still cannot see.
      Measured on this board: the agent's last action was 01:31 and its next was
      07:27 - five hours fifty-six minutes - while board-still, declared here at
      4h, did not fire once.

    - The reason is exactly what board-still reads. It takes the newest
      last_updated across every card and asks how long ago that was. Seven cards
      arrived from other projects during the window and each arrival refreshed
      that stamp. The board was busy; the agent was not. On a board that
      receives reports from elsewhere, board-still is measuring somebody else's
      work.

    - agent-still counts agent action instead, from what is already recorded: a
      card changing column, or a card edited by the agent the board names. A
      card created or edited by another project is neither. A move counts
      whoever made it, because moving a card through this board's columns is
      working this board - and the browser is where the owner moves cards, so
      reading the author of a move would report a stopped agent on a board being
      worked by hand.

    - It says nothing unless a card sits in a column that is neither an ending
      nor a queue. An idle queue is not a stopped agent: an earlier 7h49m gap on
      this board was investigated and found to be correct work throughout, and a
      rule that fired on elapsed time alone would have been wrong then and right
      later, which is no rule at all. The first run of the test caught this -
      seven cards filed by other projects sat in the backlog and the rule
      reported a stoppage that was not one.

    - And it reaches somebody other than the agent, through the two variables
      tira.notify.moves already uses. Every other rule writes to the bridge and
      trusts the agent to read it; this one's subject is the agent having
      stopped. During the stoppage above, card-still reported both stranded
      cards correctly and escalated them to CRITICAL - addressed to the party
      that had stopped. Opt-in, like the notifier: a board that has set no
      address is not made to reach out to one.

    - board-still is unchanged and still right for a board only its own agent
      writes to.

    - Reading agent action means reading history rather than one stamp, so a
      card whose own last_updated is already older than the best answer found
      so far is skipped without its history being opened at all - a card's
      newest entry cannot be later than its own stamp. On this board that is
      the difference between reading 372 histories on every police pass and
      reading a handful, and a pass already cost 3.65s before this rule
      existed. It changes no verdict, only how many files are read to reach it.

    - The catalogue growing by one was caught by four separate guards, which is
      what they are for: SKILLS.md's rule count, the designed-catalogue list in
      t/79, and t/86 and t/98, which each declare every rule the tool offers so
      none can be forgotten. t/86 goes further and asserts each declared rule
      actually fires against a board that breaks it, so agent-still had to earn
      its place there rather than be added to a list.

2.58  2026-08-18

    - TKT-349, asked for by voice: police tells the owner on Telegram when a
      card moves column, set once by the agent. His framing - a police sentry
      rather than an agent sentry - so the telling costs no LLM usage.

    - He worked the design out himself and rejected the obvious version: a
      notification on leaving a column and another on arriving sends two
      messages about one event, and ten tickets becomes twenty. This is one
      message per move, carrying the card reference.

    - Off until enabled, every column on by default, any column switchable off
      with tira.notify.moves --column NAME --no-watch. His example is discard,
      where he does not need to be told a card was set aside.

    - Sent only when both TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and TELEGRAM_CHATID are set. The
      chat id carries no underscore, which is his spelling and not the one I
      would have guessed - it was settled by asking rather than assuming.

    - The verb shipped unusable in the first cut and the coverage gate caught
      it: --watch was guarded to column.update alone, so every documented
      invocation of tira.notify.moves --column NAME --no-watch was refused
      before it was dispatched. The per-column switch he asked for by name -
      his example was discard - could not be reached from the command line at
      all. Found because CLI.pm came back 99.9% covered: the new dispatch had
      no test through it, and the test that fixes the coverage is the test that
      found the defect. The guard that exists to stop an option being silently
      dropped had instead stopped it being given.

    - And it was named wrongly in the first cut too: tira.notify-moves, the
      only hyphenated command in a tool whose every other command is dotted -
      including its three siblings tira.notify.compose, .list and .record. The
      documentation check found it, because the name it reads a command out of
      cannot contain a hyphen: it read "tira.notify-moves --watch" as the
      command "notify" and reported the refusal that followed. Two spellings of
      one name, and the guard knew only one of them. It is tira.notify.moves,
      and the entrypoint now sits at skills/notify/cli/moves where the shared
      dispatcher derives that name from the path rather than hard-coding it.

    - The hard part is saying it exactly once. Every other rule here compares
      rather than remembers, and comparison cannot tell "is in a column" from
      "just arrived", so police keeps one timestamp per card in the ledger it
      already maintains. A move is remembered when the silence was deliberate -
      a column switched off - and NOT when the message could not be sent,
      because configuring the variables afterwards must not lose the move. The
      test caught that: with the stamp written either way, a move that happened
      before the variables were set stayed silent for ever.

