2.50  2026-08-17

    - TKT-330, found by the hourly bug hunt probing for two copies of one
      decision: column-unwatched kept its own answer to what a working column
      is - not protected and not an ending - the mirror image of the definition
      TKT-310 replaced. The two agreed until that fix and then disagreed by
      construction: a column a board created is never protected, so a queue
      looked like a place work happens that no policy mentions, and the
      reporting board would have been asked to declare gate-missing on its
      buglist. It asks where work waits now rather than deciding again.

    - TKT-310, reported by mt5-ai and measured on their board: tira.next
      answered with nothing while three cards sat waiting, prioritised and
      fully filled in. Their queue columns are ones they created, so protected
      is False on both and always will be - protected means Tira owns the
      column. Waiting was defined as protected-and-not-an-ending, so their
      waiting cards were invisible by construction rather than by mistake.

    - The consequence they were more worried about, and rightly: work_order is
      one method serving tira.next and priority-skipped both, so the rule went
      structurally silent on that board at the same moment. A rule that cannot
      fire is worse than one that fires wrongly, because nothing tells you.

    - Their diagnosis is the fix, in their words: protected is a statement
      about WHO OWNS a column and it was doing duty as a statement about what
      the column MEANS. Those come apart the moment a board adds columns of its
      own.

    - tira.column.update --queue lets a board say which of its columns work
      waits in, alongside --terminal and --watch. Named replaces assumed rather
      than adding to it, and a board that says nothing keeps exactly what it
      has - the same shape as the terminal default in 2.46, and for the same
      reason.

