2.34  2026-08-16

    - TKT-268: a refusal that says what is missing says what to type.
      Measured by running every entrypoint with no arguments: 83 refused
      with a message naming no option at all, forty of them "Record
      reference is required" and not one of them --ref. The message was
      right and a reader still had to guess.
    - The engine raises those messages and has no notion of a command line,
      which is why they name a thing rather than a flag - so the flag is
      added at the boundary where flag names already live, and the engine
      keeps no table of them.
    - Two shapes, because two things can be wrong: something missing is
      supplied by an option, and a value the command will not take came in
      through one, so telling somebody to supply what they just supplied
      would be its own kind of useless.
    - 83 down to 3, and the three are refusals about the state of things - a
      collector that is not installed, a project with no heartbeat, a
      directory that is not a repository. None of them is about an argument
      and none has an option to name, so they are left alone and said so.
    - Held honest by running rather than reading: nine commands a person
      runs prove the wiring, and every message the table carries is put
      through the translation by construction, with the parse counted
      against what the table declares. That counter earned itself on the
      first run - one key contains the quote character the others are
      written with, so a pattern built around quoting missed it.

2.33  2026-08-16

    - TKT-259: an assertion in a helper is held to the same standard as one
      in a test. Four guards hold this suite to standards it set itself -
      a denial must establish its subject, a refusal must say which refusal
      it got, every declared refusal must be exercised, a board must be
      reached the way real usage reaches one - and each globbed t/*.t.
      Assertions also live in t/lib, where a helper makes a claim on a
      test's behalf, and no guard had ever read one of those files.
    - Nothing was wrong in them, which is why nobody would have noticed: the
      fault is that nothing was looking. The first of those helpers was
      written while moving one decision out of nine tests into one place -
      the right shape - and it quietly moved two assertions outside every
      check this suite makes of its own.
    - Which files the guards read is decided in one place now, because four
      copies of that decision is the same drift shape one level up.
    - Proved by running: a helper carrying the fault t/147 exists to catch
      is reported by name, and with the list narrowed back to the tests
      alone the same fault passes - which is what happened for the life of
      the suite until now.

2.32  2026-08-16

    - TKT-224: no command gets a free pass from the documentation guard. It
      derived each command's dotted name by dropping every 'skills' segment,
      as the dispatcher does - but not the dispatcher's other branch, where
      an entrypoint sitting directly in cli/ is named by its basename. So
      cli/skills, the file behind d2 tira.skills, derived to the bare
      'tira.', which appears in every document, and that command was
      reported documented without anything having been checked.
    - Proved by taking it away: with tira.skills removed from SKILLS.md,
      docs/commands.md and docs/POLICIES.md, the guard as it shipped passed
      all 806 assertions and said nothing. The check was not weak on that
      command, it was incapable of failing on it. The same documents against
      the fixed guard name it in the first failure.
    - Derived once and called from both places that need it. The guard
      beside it already had the missing branch, so fixing only the loop
      would have left one file holding two derivations of one decision -
      which is the shape this board keeps finding. The derivation itself is
      now asserted on both forms.

2.31  2026-08-16

    - TKT-221: --column-role can be given. The command reference offered it
      beside --enter-role and --before-role; it was never in the parser, so
      policy.add answered "Unknown option: column-role" and refused the
      whole command, while the engine declared the field and stored it.
    - And no rule read it. The field was storable, documented and evaluated
      in the loop that saves a policy, but only enter and before were ever
      resolved through a role - so a policy declared by column role would
      have watched a column called nothing. Every rule that watches a
      column now resolves it the same way: card-duration, checklist-idle,
      wip-limit and gate-missing.
    - Why 100% coverage did not catch it, which is the more useful half.
      The loop that stores the three roles has all its statements executed
      by the tests that set the other two; the pass for this one reaches a
      covered "next if !defined" and stops. A field can be declared,
      evaluated, documented and fully covered and still have never once
      held a value. Coverage says a line ran, not that a case was tried.
    - Three lines that copied the role fields into the policy are gone.
      They arrive in the arguments every command passes through and
      policy_add reads them from there - proved by taking one away and
      watching nothing change, which is what made it worth removing.

2.30  2026-08-16

    - TKT-216: eight commands took a required argument straight into a
      message, a comparison or a path, so a missing one produced a Perl
      warning naming an internal hash key and a line number - which reads
      like a crash - followed by a refusal about the empty string it had
      been given. "Person '' not found" is true and useless.
    - Nothing was ever damaged by it, and that is asserted here rather than
      assumed: the value reached a message and the validation underneath
      still caught it, and a refused removal leaves the people byte for
      byte as they were. What was wrong was everything the caller was told.
    - Each is guarded where it stands rather than in whatever it delegates
      to, so a change to a delegate cannot quietly remove the guard from its
      caller - which matters for two of the eight, since people.activate and
      people.deactivate share one implementation and comment.attach is
      attachment.add under another name.

