2.27  2026-08-16

    - TKT-203: every parameter a rule cannot work without is now proved to
      be refused when it is left out. The other half of the promise TKT-128
      guarded: that one covered what a rule refuses, and nothing covered
      what a rule requires. 21 rules declare a required parameter and 14 of
      those declarations had no test asserting the refusal.
    - A lost requirement costs more than a lost refusal. A rule declared
      without its age reaches the comparison with undef, so it either fires
      on everything the moment it is declared or never fires at all - and
      both read as the rule being broken rather than the policy being
      incomplete.
    - Exercised rather than scanned: all 28 requirements across 21 rules are
      left out in turn and the refusal asserted, so a rule added tomorrow is
      covered without anybody extending the guard. The parse is counted
      against what the table declares - 32 of 32.
    - Proved by stopping the engine enforcing what a rule requires: the
      guard fails, names the first rule wrongly accepted, and the file still
      completes all 81 assertions. That last part is the point. The version
      of this guard staged yesterday removed its scratch policy by rule name
      rather than by id, so on the one path that matters - the rule having
      been wrongly accepted - it died and took every assertion after it out
      of the file with it. A test that dies does not only lose its own
      verdict.

2.26  2026-08-16

    - TKT-142: every declared refusal is exercised, not just the ones in the
      rule registry. TKT-128 built that guard because a rule declaring an
      option it will not honour never reads it, so a refusal that stops
      firing fails nowhere - conversation-not-folded shipped in exactly that
      state and a bug hunt found it, not the suite. Two more tables of the
      same kind sat in the CLI uncovered: the one that refuses an option
      naming another option's job, and the one added this morning for an
      option whose readers are known. Of the four pairs declared in the
      first, two appeared in no test at all.
    - Run rather than searched for. A scan blesses whatever tests happen to
      exist; this exercises each declared refusal, so a pair added tomorrow
      is covered by writing it down.
    - And the parse is counted against what the table declares. The first
      version of this guard read to the newline after an entry and never saw
      the last one, which has no newline after it - three found where four
      are declared. A guard written to prove every declared refusal is
      exercised, quietly covering less than it claims, is the fault it
      exists to catch one level up. It was the guard's own counter that
      caught it.

2.25  2026-08-16

    - TKT-267: where work ends is asked once. The engine reads it from the
      column - one marked with --terminal is an ending, and a board that has
      marked nothing ends in one called done - while the push gate worked it
      out for itself from the done column role. So a board that marks its
      ending instead of naming it made the gate refuse to run at all, with
      "this board has no done column and no done column", the same word
      twice because it asked for the done role with done as its own
      fallback. And a board with a done column that had declared no roles -
      which the gate's own comment says most boards are - got an empty
      answer, so every finished card was judged as work in progress and
      asked for the whole definition of a complete card. The protection
      against exactly that is written into the gate in as many words, and
      was keyed on the answer that came out empty.
    - tira.column.endings is the one place that answers it, the way
      tira.card.required is the one place that says what a complete card is.
      The gate asks; three more copies of the same decision, written as the
      literal name done, ask too.
    - Asked of the tree rather than of the installed dashboard, which is what
      the same gate already does for what a complete card is, and for the
      reason written beside it: a push is what makes new code installable, so
      asking the installed copy for a command that arrives with this release
      refuses the release that introduces it. Not a hypothetical - it refused
      this one, at the card check, having printed only the name of the card it
      was about.
    - This project's own board declares the role for all three types, which
      is why neither symptom ever appeared here. The gate was right about
