2.11  2026-08-15

    - TKT-222: the platform gate runs on Windows again. It had not since
      1.06, and the suite there had drifted through five releases: eight
      files failing, against a card that named three because three was
      what the last run had reported.
    - Two causes under nearly all of it. Five assertions asked whether a
      file is executable, which is what makes a file a command on a POSIX
      system and is not a concept on Windows, where the answer is about
      the extension. Four tests shelled out by building one string and
      letting the shell take it apart - correct POSIX quoting, and no
      quoting at all where cmd reads the quotes as part of the path. Both
      now have one helper each in t/lib rather than a copy per test.
    - Three assertions in t/169 run bash stubs through a POSIX shell and
      are skipped by name. Worth doing rather than leaving: on Windows the
      assertion in front of them passed, because the command never ran, so
      its exit status was zero and "a failure is not fatal" was satisfied
      by nothing having happened.
    - t/95, which exists to prove Windows file replacement, set errno and
      asserted the message says so. The code reads the Win32 error there,
      deliberately, because errno is meaningless after a Win32 API call.
      On Linux the two read the same and it passed for a reason other than
      the one it states. It now sets what the code will read, and a second
      assertion holds the reason to a denial so an empty one cannot
      satisfy the first by matching itself.
    - git is on the lab, installed through its own package manager, so
      every path that shells out to it can be gated there at last.

