2.08  2026-08-15

    - TKT-228: the served dashboard answers again. Reported by the owner:
      "d2 tira.dashboard -o browser" bound its port and then every request
      died with "Cannot resolve project path 'tira'".
    - A board may be referred to without its path. That is deliberate: the
      agent working a project is never told where the board sits, so it
      cannot go round the CLI and edit the files directly. Such a
      reference is resolved through a path resolver that the CLI installs.
      dashboard.psgi builds a plain Tira object, which has none, and
      serve() passed its argument through untouched - so the parent
      resolved nothing and the workers, which are the one place that
      cannot resolve anything for themselves, failed once per request in a
      child whose errors only reach a log while the port sat listening.
    - The board is resolved by the process that holds the resolver, and
      the workers are handed a path they can open. serve() refuses
      anything else rather than starting workers that will fail. Neither
      the refusal nor anything else repeats the value it was given: a
      message that quotes it teaches its reader how boards are referred to
      here, which is the one thing this arrangement exists to prevent.
    - Measured against a running server rather than only in the suite,
      and through the route real usage takes: a reference in the
      environment with no --project answers HTTP 200 with the board's
      sign-in page and no resolve errors, where the previous release
      answers 500 three times.
    - A worker that cannot open the board it was given now refuses at
      load instead of becoming a server that fails once per request. That
      is the shape this went out in: the port bound, the board looked up,
      and every request died in a child whose errors reach a log nobody
      is tailing. Whatever started the worker is still watching at load
      and has nowhere to look afterwards, so the failure belongs there.
    - dashboard.psgi is now loaded by the suite the way a worker loads
      it. Nothing loaded it before, and what was checked - that serve()
      hands the runner a path to it - is equally true of a psgi that
      cannot build anything at all.

2.07  2026-08-15

    - TKT-219: a card that moved while its checklist stood still is now
      reported. Asked for by the owner. checklist-idle asks how long a
      checklist has not moved inside one column, so it catches a card
      standing still; this catches the opposite and more misleading case,
      where the card MOVES and the checklist never does, so progress is
      claimed by dragging rather than by working.
    - Two narrowings keep it usable, and both were measured rather than
      guessed. Reported without them - any move with nothing ticked since
      the previous one - the rule names 154 of 226 cards on this board,
      two thirds of it, and almost all are the last move into done where
      the work finished earlier and there was nothing left to tick. So it
      reports only a move INTO a column where work happens, never into
      done or discard, and only while the checklist still has something
      unfinished. Narrowed that way it names none of them, which is what a
      board worked properly should give.
    - No age, deliberately, and that is what separates it from the rule it
      sits beside. checklist-idle needs a grace or it is nagging. This one
      asks whether a move happened without the checklist moving, which is
      an event rather than a duration: once it has happened, waiting
      longer tells nobody anything new.
    - Which columns mean work is read from the board - protected or
      terminal means resting, with done assumed when nothing is marked
      terminal - through a helper extracted from card-unassigned rather
      than a second copy of that decision.
    - The rule compares position in the journal rather than timestamps.
      The first version compared a checklist entry's time against the
      previous move's, which works only while the clock advances between
      writes - and t/86 runs on a fixed clock, so a tick and a move in the
      same second could not be told apart and the rule could never fire
      there. The journal is append-only and ordered, and which came first
      is the question anyway.

