    - Between rounds, never during a pass, because police writes the
      bridge and the enforcement ledger and a pass cut in half leaves a
      violation counted and unsaid. And once: exec never returns, so in
      a running police this can only happen once by construction, but a
      restarter that does return now ends the watch rather than trying
      again every interval - that is the loop four dashboards spent
      twenty hours in, and a police that has stopped is visible while
      one restarting on a timer is not.
    - The dashboard half is not in this release. It was written down as
      impossible - a served board cannot replace itself - and that was
      reasoned from the failure we hit rather than from the tool. He
      sent me to Starman's documentation: HUP restarts workers
      gracefully while the master keeps the listening socket. Because
      the app is built in-process before Plack::Runner sees it, the
      documented answer is Server::Starter holding the socket, which is
      a dependency and a change to how the board is launched, so it gets
      its own release rather than riding along here.
    - TKT-213: a fold written in the same second as the mark is a fold.
      mt5-ai isolated it by comparing three cards their script had
      handled identically - marked 10:40:20 written 10:40:21 silent,
      marked 10:42:33 written 10:42:34 silent, marked 10:44:32 written
      10:44:32 reported. Only the equal pair fired.
    - Stamps are second-resolution, so a fold written inside the same
      second as the mark cannot be strictly later, and the rule asked
      for strictly later. An agent that marks and folds in one script -
      which is the correct thing to do and exactly what the rule asks
      for - lands in the same second whenever the board is quick. So the
      agents that behave best were the ones most likely to be told they
      did not, and told unfalsifiably: the message says nothing was
      written down while the thing is written down.
    - What it gives up is recorded rather than glossed: a detail written
      in the same second BEFORE the mark now reads as a fold. That needs
      an agent to have just written a detail field, which is close to
      folding anyway, and the alternative is accusing the diligent.
    - A card marked ok with nothing written into it is still reported,
      which is the assertion that matters. The first attempt at this fix
      made the rule stop firing altogether and the test caught it.
    - TKT-206: card-sandbox-missing can be watched passing. There was no
      defect - t/195 builds developer-dashboard's exact arrangement and
      every assertion passes on the shipped code, so DD-532 is a rename:
      their branch is dd-532 and the card is DD-532, and the check wants
      a branch named exactly after the card, which the violation has
      said in those words since 1.73.
    - What was missing is the half they identified themselves. Five
      cards cleared that rule on their board in one morning and every
      one cleared by leaving the column, which the --enter scope makes
      it stop applying to - so nobody had ever observed it pass. A rule
      that can only be seen to stop applying is one nobody can trust,
      whatever the verdict on any single card, and that observation is
      now a standing test.

1.97  2026-08-15

    - TKT-208: the bridge prints UTF-8 rather than whatever Perl
      guesses. He hit it while tailing the bridge - "Wide character in
      print at .../lib/Tira/CLI.pm line 1932" - which is the replay
      handing decoded characters straight to a handle that is
      deliberately :raw.
    - Standard output is raw on purpose: Perl's text layer on Windows
      rewrites every newline, so the bytes leaving the process would not
      be the bytes Tira produced, and Tira compares output bytes in its
      own cache. Everything that formats output therefore encodes
      itself, through _utf8_bytes. Three prints did not - the bridge
      replay, the bridge follow loop, and the --refs-only listing.
    - The warning is the milder half. Above U+00FF Perl warns and then
      writes UTF-8 anyway, so the text arrives intact with a warning
      stitched into whatever is reading. Between U+0080 and U+00FF there
      is no warning at all: Perl writes a single latin-1 byte, which is
      not valid UTF-8. A card title carrying an accented name or a
      multiplication sign written as U+00D7 put exactly the byte
      tira.doctor exists to repair into the channel that reports it.
    - All three now encode like every other output path. The follow loop
      matters as much as the replay: fixing the first screen and leaving
      every line after it wrong is the worse half, because a tail is
      what an agent leaves running.
    - TKT-205: a backup of a board nobody touched says nothing changed,
      and stops carrying somebody's sign-in. He noticed tira.backup
      reporting changed: 1 on runs seconds apart with nothing touched,
      and guessed the backup wrote a stamp that guaranteed the next one
      had work to do. Nearly right: nothing the backup writes causes it -
      a session file under the store is rewritten whenever anybody uses
      the board, reading included.
    - The consequence he named is the small one: "is this board already
      backed up" had no cheap answer. The larger one is what a session
      is. It is the server side of a sign-in, and it was being committed
      into the backup history for ever. The manual already made this
      argument about the only other exclusion - a restored lock is
      somebody else's half-finished write - and a restored session is
      somebody else's sign-in, which is worse.
    - The exclusion is checked on every backup rather than written once
      when the store is made. It used to be written only at creation, so
      a line added there would have fixed new boards and left every
      board that already exists tracking sessions for ever - and every
      board that exists today was made before this.
    - A board that already tracked them is told to stop, in the index
      only. What it has already committed stays committed: rewriting the
      history of a backup is a worse thing to own than the tidiness it
      would buy, and the documentation says so rather than implying a
      backup gets scrubbed.
    - TKT-202: a card being worked anywhere counts as a card being
      worked. Whether anything is in progress - which work-without-card
      rests on - counted only cards in the single column named by the
      in-progress role. This project's own board declares
      in-progress=implement and has five columns work happens in, so
      tests-red, verify, document and push read as nobody working.
    - Measured here rather than reasoned about: VIO-0013 reached
      CRITICAL and was said five times while a card sat in verify,
      assigned, with its suite running and the tree changing under it
      because that is what working on a card looks like.
    - It now asks the board where work happens, the same question
      card-unassigned and priority-skipped ask: not protected, and not
      marked terminal, with done as the fallback for a board that has
      marked nothing. A board that declares no roles is unchanged.
    - The half that mattered more than the fix is that backlog, the
      ending column and discard still do not count. A rule that never
      fires would have traded a false alarm for a silent one, which is
      the worse trade.
    - The documentation half, which is why this was hard to see. The
      reference said "the vocabulary is the project's own; Tira matches
      a role without needing to understand it" while two names were
      understood and hard-coded. They behaved differently: done says out
      loud when it cannot find its role, in-progress said nothing - so a
      board using its own word for it fell through to the wide reading
      and was watched correctly by accident, while a board using Tira's
      word was watched in one column out of five. Following the
      documentation gave the right behaviour. Now only done is read by
      Tira itself, and the reference says so.

1.96  2026-08-15

    - TKT-204: a board that says where its work lives is backed up
      where it lives. developer-dashboard reported board-unbacked
      raised at 07:55 and escalated twice while the board was backed up
      three times in between, against a seven-day age.
    - The cause was one variable answering two questions. _police_world
      resolves the repository a project declared, falling back to the
      board directory, and asked everything with it. Branches, work
      trees, unpushed commits and whether the tree is changing all want
      the repository. The backup lookup wants the board: the store is
      <board>/.tira, which is where tira.backup writes and the only
      place it ever writes, and where restore and export read it from.
    - So a board that had declared a repository had its backups looked
      for inside the code, found none, and was told it had never been
      backed up - permanently, whatever anybody did about it. A board
      that declared nothing had the two directories in the same place
      and worked, which is why this project's own board settled the
      same rule at 07:28 that morning while his could not.
    - That variable arrived in 1.91 with TKT-178, which taught police to
      read a declared repository so card-sandbox-missing would stop
      reporting branches that plainly existed. It fixed exactly the
      boards it broke, which is why the report could describe one rule
      settling promptly in the same breath as the other never settling.
    - His own guess is worth recording as wrong, because it was the
      reasonable one: that police gathers the machine facts once rather
      than per round. It gathers them every round, and the loop says so.
      Acting on the guess would have produced a fix for something that
      was already right.
    - TKT-182: a recording handed to tira.question.ask reaches the
      question. The option was accepted, refused on every command it
      does not belong to, and then passed through for update alone - so
      asking with --voice produced a question whose own reminder told
      its author to supply the recording they had just supplied. The
      engine had always attached it and the manual had always documented
      it; one line in the dispatcher disagreed with both.
    - TKT-152: the hierarchy refusal names the command that does what
      the caller was reaching for. It correctly refuses ticket-to-ticket
      parentage and used to stop there, so somebody wanting "this ticket
      blocks that one" wrote it into a comment, where no automated check
      can see it. Exactly as strict, one sentence longer.
    - TKT-170: backup, export and import stop reading as the same
      thing. Another project read tira.backup.export as the way to back
      a board up, which is the reading that loses work: an export is a
      file somebody has to remember to make, and a board with exports
      and no backups has nothing to restore from. Both documents now say
      which of the three is the backup, that export and import move a
      board to another machine and receive it there, and that day-to-day
      operation uses neither - in his words. A test holds the three
      passages together, because nothing was stopping them drifting
      apart and the reading that caused this was somebody doing their
      best with what was written.
    - TKT-140: an assertion that claimed to compare the module version
      with .env now reads .env. It compared the module against a
      literal, while a second assertion compared .env against the same
      literal, so the two agreed only through a third party and the name
      promised a comparison the code never made. Both halves are kept:
      the release being made is still asserted to be the one intended.

1.95  2026-08-15

    - TKT-195: adding a column no longer silently narrows a rule. A
      policy that names a column stops covering the board the moment
      somebody adds another, and nobody has to do anything wrong for
      that to happen - the policy was complete when it was written.
    - This project did it to itself. checklist-idle and card-duration
      were declared for one column on a board with five, so a card could
      sit untouched in any of the other four for ever, and one did, for
      six hours, until the owner noticed it rather than the board.
    - column-unwatched reports a column work happens in that no
      column-scoped policy mentions at all, naming the column and the
      rules declared for the others. One violation however many columns
      are blind, because that is one state - the board has grown past
      its policies - and it is answered when they catch up.
    - Narrower than the version written first, which asked whether every
      column-scoped rule covered every working column. Running the
      review this release also ships against this project's own board
      killed that one: gate-missing is declared for verify, push and
      done deliberately, because a card in tests-red has no gate to show
      yet, and the wider check demanded it be declared there too.
      Nothing could have answered that, and a violation nobody can close
      is the complaint mt5-ai raised the same morning about card-damaged
      arriving from the other direction.
    - Which rule belongs on which column is a judgment. A column no
      column-scoped rule mentions is not: it is a place work happens
      that the policies do not know exists. Board-wide rules neither
      create the gap nor close it, and the finished column is never
      asked for - a rule watching it for idleness would report every
      shipped card for ever.
    - It takes no column, no age and no card scope, all three refused
      when they are set: it is about the columns other policies name, a
      gap is a gap the moment it opens, and a card cannot narrow it any
      more than it can narrow the board being still.
    - tira.policy.review prints the whole set in one place - every rule
      in the catalogue exactly once, declared with the columns it
      covers, declined with the reason, or unanswered. Policies are
      declared one at a time over weeks, and reading them back out of
      policy.list means holding the catalogue in your head to see what
      is missing. That is the review he asked to be able to do behind
      the agent, and it is now one command rather than an exercise.

1.94  2026-08-15

    - TKT-192: tira.doctor, which he asked for by name. It finds board
      files holding bytes that are not valid UTF-8, says which file and
      which byte at what offset, and repairs them only when asked.
    - One correction to what he described, because it changes what the
      command does. U+FFFD is not in his files - it is what a lenient
      read PRODUCES when it meets a byte it cannot decode, so it is what
      he sees in output rather than what is on disk. A doctor searching
      for it would find nothing and report every damaged file clean,
      which is the worst answer a repair tool can give. It searches for
      bytes.
    - A bad byte is repaired by reading it as latin-1 and writing it back
      as UTF-8, so 0xD7 becomes the multiplication sign somebody typed.
      Substituting a replacement character would turn the damage into
      data permanently, which is what the lenient read is careful never
      to write back.
    - Nothing is repaired without being asked, and nothing else in the
      file moves. Attachments are never touched, being bytes that were
      never meant to decode.
    - Proved on a copy of the reporting board: card-damaged 2 before, 0
      after, and the text reads "Workflow finder x2 + 3 independent
      refuters" with the multiplication sign restored rather than a
      replacement mark.
    - Two faults on the way, both caught by the test. The first offset
      arithmetic reported byte 0x00 at offset 570 for a file whose only
      bad byte was 0xD7 near the start, because Encode's FB_QUIET
      modifies the buffer it is given and every offset after the first
      was counted twice. And an assertion read ok(!$result->{repaired}),
      which an empty arrayref satisfies in neither direction - counted
      instead.
    - Perl does all of it. He offered another language if one was needed;
      it was not.

