    - The count is what the reading agent will see, because the backlog
      is filtered to whoever is reading it and a number about somebody
      else's work is worse than no number. A bridge with nothing
      outstanding still says nothing.

1.68  2026-08-14
    - TKT-165: column-skipped reports a card that arrived somewhere
      without passing through the columns it was supposed to, and names
      which ones it missed. His request, in his words: another policy
      option the agent sets, so that when a card moves from one column to
      another and misses the required columns, police shouts and calls it
      back.
    - TKT-164: and the reason he asked for it. He sent a photograph of
      his own board - TESTS-RED, IMPLEMENT, VERIFY, DOCUMENT and PUSH,
      every one empty - and asked what was being worked. Nothing was
      wrong with the tool. The board defines eight columns and this agent
      had been using two of them, implement then done, so the columns
      that exist to say what is happening were empty through a whole
      session. The standing instruction is that the board is the report,
      and it was reporting nothing.
    - The required columns are declared rather than inferred from the
      order they sit in: a documentation-only card has no red test to
      write, and a rule that assumed the whole sequence would report
      every legitimate shortcut, which is the noise that kills a channel.
    - The route is read from the work log, which the engine writes on
      every move, so the evidence is not written by whoever made the
      moves. A card still on its way is not reported - it has not skipped
      verify, it has not reached it. Police reports and moves nothing:
      calling the card back is the agent's.
    - Adding a twenty-sixth rule made three tests fail at once, which is
      the design working: two keep a table of every rule and what it
      needs, and the third counts them in the manual.

1.67  2026-08-14
    - TKT-162: an answer's follow-through is addressed to whoever asked,
      not whoever answered. Every violation went to the card's holder,
      which is right for the rest of them - one agent per ticket, and a
      bridge that hands every agent every violation is noise by
      construction. It is wrong for the four rules about what happens
      after an answer: reading it, judging it, folding it into the card
      and asking anything further are the agent's work, and the owner's
      part ends when he answers.
    - He read it off his own bridge - "for Michael ... Q-017 was marked ok
      and nothing was folded into the card", escalating to WARNING on the
      second telling - and asked why folding in was for the user when it
      is the agent's job.
    - It stopped the work rather than merely misdirecting it. His words:
      the agent confused, that is for me, and they skip to fold in. The
      agent read the addressee, concluded the folding was the owner's,
      and did not do it - so the rule escalated about work its own
      addressing had told the only capable party to ignore. A violation
      addressed to the wrong party is not a message in the wrong
      envelope; it is an instruction the right party never receives.
    - All four are addressed to the author of the question now:
      answer-waiting, answer-unjudged, answer-ok-not-folded and
      answer-not-ok-no-followup. Nothing is inferred - a question stores
      who asked and an answer stores who answered, separately. A question
      with nobody named on it falls back to the card holder rather than
      being addressed to nobody, and every other rule is unchanged.
    - The other twenty-one were examined. question-unanswered is the only
      one whose actor is the owner rather than the agent, and it is left
      as it is: the agent is who chases an unanswered question, so the
      card holder is the right reader for it.

1.66  2026-08-14
    - TKT-158: tira.policy.undeclared answers which rules this project
      has neither declared nor declined. The agent is the only party that
      can declare a policy and had no way to discover what it had not:
      policy.list answers what is declared, policy.declined what was
      refused on purpose, and nothing answered the rest.
    - Reported by mt5-ai after a real miss. An owner's answer sat
      answered-and-unread, then read-and-unmarked, for eighty-four
      minutes with nothing said, because answer-waiting had never been
      set - not declined, never considered. Police behaved correctly
      throughout: an undeclared rule cannot fire.
    - The fact already existed and went to the wrong reader. Police
      prints the undeclared rules for the owner to paste across, when it
      starts - and theirs had been running eight hours, so it was printed
      once, hours earlier, to somebody asleep. They asked explicitly that
      the owner's prompt not change, and it has not; police now asks this
      question rather than working it out a second time.
    - A declined rule is answered and does not appear, because the
      declined list exists so a deliberate no can be told from an
      omission. A project that has decided every rule gets an empty list
      rather than an unknown command - the three names they tried were
      all unknown, and an unknown command reads as "this cannot be asked"
      rather than "there is nothing to say".
    - Named in the command reference as well as the manual, because
      mt5-ai have now reported three command families they could not find
      after capturing only one document.

1.65  2026-08-14
    - TKT-155: a card is no longer shown as empty when it is full.
      ticket.show --fields with -o human narrowed the record correctly and
      then drew the fixed card summary against it, so every key the
      narrowing had removed printed as its empty placeholder - no
      description, unassigned, no priority, on a card where all of them
      were filled - and the field actually asked for appeared nowhere.
    - Reported by mt5-ai, who put the cost plainly: ignoring the flag
      would show the whole card, which is merely unhelpful; this showed a
      filled card as an empty one AND left out what was asked for, wrong
      in both directions at once. It is worst on a board following the
      rules Tira ships with, because a reviewer sees exactly the hollow
      card those rules exist to catch.
    - The readable format now shows what it was given: the fields asked
      for, lists as lists, and the two halves of a scope. A field that is
      genuinely empty is still named when it was asked for, because
      absence is worth seeing when somebody asked about it. json and toon
      were correct throughout and are unchanged.

1.64  2026-08-14
    - TKT-151: the tool that proves the gate can now prove the commit
      gate. Five of its checks looked for a card already sitting in a
      working column rather than making one, so on a board where
      everything is finished - the state a board is meant to reach - the
      whole commit gate went unproved. Found by the hourly bug hunt, by
      running a tool nothing runs.
    - It had been printing "NOT PROVED - could not find a finished and a
      working card" and exiting 1, unread, because nothing in any hook,
      test or document runs it. Its own opening line is that a check
      never seen to fail is not a check but a hope; the thing written to
      stop the gate being a hope had become one.
    - It raises the card it needs now, the way it already raises the
      hollow card that proves the card audit, and sets it aside with a
      reason afterwards - a discard with nothing on it is what the audit
      itself refuses.
    - The other sixteen checks pass, including every step of the push
      gate, and the board is left as it was found.

1.63  2026-08-14
    - TKT-135: search reaches the whole card rather than the front of it.
      mt5-ai searched for a figure, found nothing, and published that it
      appeared nowhere on a card - and it had been in that card's gate
      records the whole time. Their words: an absence proven by an
      instrument that cannot see two thirds of the record is not an
      absence.
    - It saw rather less than a third. The searchable text was the ref,
      the title, the description and the questions. The problem
      statement, what a solution needs, the key details, deliverables,
      acceptance criteria, test steps, behaviour, scope, labels,
      checklist, comments, gate records, evidence, conversation and
      attachment names were all out of reach - which on a board following
      the rules Tira ships with is nearly everything a card says, because
      those rules are what put the substance in those fields.
    - The gates and the evidence matter most on a finished card: they are
      append-only observations, so they hold what was measured rather
      than what was believed at planning, which is when somebody comes
      looking.
    - Their second suggestion, that the answer say which parts were
      searched, is unnecessary now the answer is the card. Both search
      paths go through one function, so an absence does not depend on
      whether an index was built, and a string on no card is still not
      found.

1.62  2026-08-14
    - TKT-149: the documentation check now runs the policies guide's
      examples at all, and counts what it set aside as well as what it
      ran. Found by the hourly bug hunt, and the bigger half was found by
      trying to prove the smaller one: breaking a documented example on
      purpose and watching the check report everything understood.
    - Examples were collected as a backticked "dashboard tira...." in
      prose or a bare "tira...." at the start of a line. The policies
      guide writes all 132 of its examples as "d2 tira....", which
      matched neither - so the document with by far the most worked
      examples in it was listed for checking and contributed none. The
      docstring still said the guide was written the second way, which
      had been true when the checker was written; the guide's style moved
      and the checker did not.
    - It runs 246 examples now rather than 125, and the first full run
      found a real one: a trailing "# set it" was being passed as an
      argument, so a correct example looked broken. A note to the reader
      is a comment now, and a hash inside quotes still is not.
    - And it counts what it set aside as well as what it ran. It finds a hundred and ninety example lines,
      runs a hundred and twenty-five, and used to drop sixty without a
      word - then print the number it ran as though the documentation had
      been checked. Nearly a third of it had not been tried. Found by the
      hourly bug hunt.
    - Skipping is not the fault: most of the sixty carry a shape a reader
      is meant to replace, and running those would prove nothing.
      Reporting them as checked is the fault, and this tool had already
      been bitten by it once - its own header records that treating a
      quoted project name as a shape "skipped the example silently, which
      is how a deliberately broken example passed this check". The
      quoting was fixed and the silence was left.
    - Some of the sixty are real examples. A prefix of DEV is dropped for
      being capitals, and DEV is a value somebody types. Naming them is
      what makes that visible: TIRA_EXAMPLES_SHOW_SKIPPED=1 lists them.
    - What the check refuses is unchanged - an example naming a command
      or an option that does not exist still fails the gate.

1.61  2026-08-14
    - TKT-148: the gate no longer passes while the release fails to
      happen. git push was exiting 141 - SIGPIPE - after the pre-push
      hook finished: the hook printed that the suite passed, coverage was
      100 percent, documentation agreed and the board was backed up, and
      nothing was transferred. Three times, with the remote unchanged.
    - GIT_TRACE gave the cause in one line, arriving while the hook was
      still checking the board: "Connection to github.com closed by
      remote host". git opens the connection before it runs the hook, the
      gate takes twenty minutes, and GitHub closes a session idle that
      long. The failure therefore lands after every check has passed, and
      reads exactly like a gate approving a release.
    - It hid behind a pipe. The pushes were written as git push piped to
      tail, and a pipeline's status is the last command's, so tail
      reported success for a push that had been killed.
    - The first explanation was wrong and is written into the test rather
      than deleted: that a pre-push hook must read the refs git sends it.
      It does not read them, and that is not the cause - a hook ignoring
      them pushes fine, verbose or slow. It had been reasoned about
      instead of run.
    - install-hooks now configures ssh keepalives alongside the gate, so
      the two arrive together, and says what was measured so the next
      person does not reason about it either.

1.60  2026-08-14
    - TKT-147: evidence details are no longer accepted and thrown away.
      tira.evidence.add took --details, exited zero and stored nothing:
      the entry carries a summary, a link, an author and an attachment,
      and details is not one of its fields. The printed entry looked
      complete because the summary was there, so nothing suggested half
      of what was typed had gone nowhere. Found by the hourly bug hunt,
      while reading the record shape for something else.
    - It cost this board a night of reasoning. Every card worked through
      the night recorded its evidence with --details and only the
      summaries survived. That is not recoverable, and reconstructing it
      from memory would be worse than the gap.
    - Refused now, naming --summary, beside the assign commands that
      refuse --assignee. gate.add still stores details, which is where
      the option belongs and why it exists. Storing it on evidence
      instead was rejected for the reason base and --token were: it would
      add a field under cover of a bug fix.
    - The refusal found a caller immediately - a test written an hour
      earlier had been losing its evidence details since the day it was
      written.

1.59  2026-08-14
    - TKT-146: a credential a command never looks at can no longer be
      typed at it. --token was parsed, accepted on the four login
      commands, and read by nothing. The only place it was ever looked at
      was the guard refusing it elsewhere, whose message - "A token
      belongs to the login commands" - sent the caller to the commands
      where it did nothing. Found by the hourly bug hunt.
    - login.check --token was the worst of the three: exit zero and a
      definite no about a credential the command never examined, and a
      definite no is exactly what a credential check is trusted to mean.
      login.status ignored it and listed sessions; login.logout refused,
      but for the missing --id rather than for the token.
    - Removed rather than implemented. Nothing read it, so this changes
      no behaviour; ending one named session by token is a plausible
      thing to want and would be a new feature on its own card. The
