      Two days, and it was found from a phone screenshot.
    - Set where identity is known: the command layer already resolves
      --author, or TIRA_AUTHOR said once rather than on every command,
      and now sets it for whatever runs next. That is how an unattended
      writer becomes attributable. A name the board does not know is
      still not recorded - a log that writes down any name reads as
      accounted for, which is worse than one that admits it does not
      know.

1.57  2026-08-14
    - TKT-145: the three policy actions now mean three things. Only
      bridge-reminder was ever read - the engine compared the action
      against that name twice and nowhere else - and what reached the
      owner's terminal was decided by escalation alone. So a rule set to
      log-only appeared on his terminal exactly like one set to
      bridge-reminder, and print-reminder did nothing log-only did not.
      Found by the hourly bug hunt.
    - The policies guide states it as a table: print-reminder goes to the
      owner's police terminal, log-only is recorded and said to nobody,
      for when you are tuning a rule and do not want the noise yet. The
      second row was not true, and it was untrue in exactly the situation
      it exists for - you reach for log-only to stop the noise and it put
      the rule on his terminal anyway.
    - Escalation decides when the owner is told; the action now decides
      whether. A log-only violation is still recorded, because a rule you
      cannot read back is not one you can tune, and bridge-reminder is
      unchanged. Proved by running the three side by side past escalation
      and by removing the filter again.

1.56  2026-08-14
    - TKT-144: a refusal test now proves the refusal it was written for.
      Twenty-five tests capture a refusal as a failed eval and fourteen
      of them asserted only that something died. Any death satisfied
      them: a renamed argument, a moved requirement, a typo in a rule
      name, an exception thrown three calls deeper. Found by the hourly
      bug hunt.
    - Proved rather than argued. Pointing one of them at a rule that does
      not exist made the death come from "Unknown policy rule" - nothing
      to do with the column it claims to be about - and the test passed,
      reporting that a column on that rule is refused. The rule could
      have been deleted entirely and it would still have passed.
    - It is the denial fault of 1.54 in its other form: there the subject
      was never established so emptiness passed, here the reason is never
      established so any failure passes. Both report a promise kept about
      something that did not happen.
    - Every captured refusal now asserts on the message, within sight of
      the refusal it belongs to, or says in the comment above it that any
      failure is what it means. A check enforces the choice, and is
      proved by removing one assertion and watching it be named.

1.55  2026-08-14
    - TKT-143: a policy can no longer carry a setting that does nothing.
