1.28  2026-08-13
    - Naming the wrong card on a question command is refused rather than
      silently acting on another card. All six - answer, mark, update,
      discard, attach and voice - looked the question up by id and then
      overwrote the card the caller had named, so answering with one
      card's reference and another card's question changed the other card
      and reported success. The card that was named stayed waiting, and
      nothing anywhere said so: the board afterwards looked consistent,
      because the answer really was on a card. Resolving by id alone
      stays, since the ids are unique across a board; a card that is
      named and disagrees now gets a refusal saying where the question
      actually lives.

1.27  2026-08-13
    - The process table is read on macOS. ps prints the month before the
      day on Linux and after it on macOS, and both regexes that read the
      table required a month name in the second field - so on a Mac 192
      lines of ps output produced no processes at all and
      leftover-process reported nothing whatever was running. The month
      is now whichever of the two fields is a month name, which is
      decidable without knowing the platform and so cannot be wrong on a
      machine nobody anticipated. Proved on both labs: 177 of 178 on
      macOS where there had been none, and Linux unchanged.

1.26  2026-08-13
    - The world gatherer works on Windows. It asked whether a program
      existed with a search that looked for an exact filename and tested
      it with -x; on Windows a program is name.exe and -x answers for the
      extension, so on the lab it found neither git, nor ps, nor
      tasklist.exe sitting in System32. Every git fact and the process
      table came back empty, and five of the six rules that read the
      world were silent there whatever the machine was doing - the defect
      the previous releases were about, reintroduced inside its own fix.
      There is one search now rather than two that drift, the process
      table comes from tasklist on Windows and ps on POSIX with the
      platform passed in so both can be checked from either side, and
      PATHEXT is matched in either case for the filesystems that care.
      Proved on the Windows lab: 138 processes gathered where there were
      none.

1.25  2026-08-13
    - A rule can be considered and declined, with the reason, and police
      stops asking about it. The prompt lists every rule a project has
      not declared and prints on every run, which is right - but it could
      not tell "nobody has looked at this" from "somebody looked and said
      no", so on a board that had made those decisions it asked an
      answered question for ever. A channel that repeats itself is one
      everybody learns to read past, and this is the channel that exists
      to be read. The reason is required: without one this would be a way
      of silencing the prompt rather than a decision, and a decision with
      no reason recorded is indistinguishable from having skipped the
      question. Declaring a rule later clears its declining, and a rule
      that arrives in a future release is asked about as before.

