0.61  2026-08-08
    - Fixed cards still drawing over the next column on boards with many
      columns (owner photo, message 3007) — a different defect from
      0.60's, in a different mode. The card list is a CSS grid, and a
      grid item refuses to shrink below its own content by default, so
      once a column is narrower than a card's longest word the card
      overflowed. 0.60 fixed the columns; this fixes the cards. The
      wide-board guard now also runs a thirteen-column board.

0.60  2026-08-08
    - Fixed board columns collapsing and cards spilling into the next
      column on boards with many columns (owner photo, message 3005).
      The table uses a fixed layout, which takes every column's width
      from the header row alone: the headers carried only a minimum
      width, so nine columns were divided into 134px each and the cards
      overflowed. The headers now carry the width itself, so Standard
      keeps its 17rem columns and scrolls as it always claimed to. Fit
      mode is unchanged. New nine-column browser guard, because the
      two-column fixture could not express the defect.

0.59  2026-08-08
    - A leading ~ now means the home directory wherever a path is
      accepted (owner report: answering the directory question with
      ~/foo/bar created a directory literally named ~). That covers the
      guided prompts and a quoted --dir or --project, which a shell
      never expands.
    - Prompts are editable at a terminal: Ctrl-A and Ctrl-E as asked,
      plus Ctrl-U, Ctrl-K, backspace, arrows and Home/End, written
      against core POSIX termios rather than a Term::ReadLine editing
      module, none of which is installed anywhere this runs. Away from a
      terminal the prompt stays a plain read.
    - project.update gained --listen HOST[:PORT] as the compact form of
      --dashboard-host and --dashboard-port.
    - Fixed: pressing enter past the people question in the guided setup
      reported "Every member needs a name" instead of meaning "none".

0.58  2026-08-07
    - A project now remembers its dashboard address:
      `tira.project.update --dashboard-host localhost --dashboard-port
      8080` stores it, project.show reports it, and `-o browser` uses it
      instead of always listening on 0.0.0.0:7899. `any` is the
      plain-language form of every interface. An address given on the
      command line still wins, and both values are validated where they
      are set rather than where they are used.

0.57  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.onboard`: the guided form of project.new, asking for the
      name, directory, people, each board's reference prefix, whether the
      boards share one column set, and the columns, then confirming
      before it writes anything. Flags pre-fill the answers, unusable
      answers are re-asked with the reason, declining exits 1 and
      reaching the end of input aborts — both leaving nothing behind.
      Only onboard ever prompts; project.new stays argument-driven so
      scripts and agents can never be left waiting on input.
    - project.new gained --sow-columns, --epic-columns, and
      --ticket-columns, so boards can differ instead of always sharing
      one set.

0.56  2026-08-07
    - New `tira.project.new` (owner request): one command creates a
      project, its members, each board's reference prefix, and one
      shared column set across all three boards — replacing a
      thirty-five command manual sequence. Column names are given as
      they read ("Done / Release") and slugified automatically with the
      original text kept as the label; columns that already exist,
      including the protected Backlog and Discard, are skipped, so the
      natural list works verbatim and re-running changes nothing. All
      input is validated before the first write, and prefixes are set
      before any record can exist so the first reference is 001.

0.55  2026-08-07
